5401:
** φόβος**, ὁ,
(φέβομαι) **panic flight,** the usual sense in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, cf. Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.71 (but compare φύζα, φόβου κρυόεντος ἑταίρη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.2); once in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, οἱ δ᾽ ἔσχοντο φόβου 24.57; frequently in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, Δαναῶν γένετο ἰαχή τε φ. τε 15.396; πρῶτος Πηνέλεως.. ἦρχε φόβοιο 17.597; ἐς φόβον ἀνδρῶν 15.310; φόβονδε ={φύγαδε}, ἑστάμεναι κρατερῶς, μηδὲ τρωπᾶσθε φόβονδε [prev. work] 666}}]; φόβονδ᾽ ἔχε μώνυχας ἵππους {{Refs|8.139}}; μή τι φόβονδ᾽ ἀγόρευε counsel not **to flight,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5.252; ἀΐξαντα φόβονδε 17.579; ὦρσαν φόβον Δαναοῖς 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.145.}}
**2** Φόβος personified, as son of Ares, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.299; Δεῖμός τε Φ. τε 11.37, compare 4.440, 15.119, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 934, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 45}}; worshipped at Selinus, {{Refs|“IG” 14.268.2.}}
**II** **panic fear,** [στρατῷ] φ. ἐμβάλλειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10. ; ἐν τῷ γινομένῳ φ. [prev. author] 9.69}}; generally, **fear, terror** (distinguished from δέος (which see)), τορὸς ὀρθόθριξ φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 32 (Lyric poetry); διάτορος φ. [prev. author] “Pr.” 183 (Lyric poetry); ταρβόσυνος [prev. author] “Th.” 240 (Lyric poetry); νεανικός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1204}}; joined with δέος and δεῖμα, (see entry).; opposed to θάρρος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 644c}}; sometimes in milder sense, **doubt, scruple,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 101b}}; ἔχει πολλὴν ὑποψίαν καὶ φ. ὡς.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 268a}}: also, **awe, reverence,** for a ruler or divine being, τοῦ ἡγεμόνος {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1642.17 (3rd c.AD)}}; θεοῦ {{LXX+4th c.AD+|LXX.Psa.35(36).1, “PLond.” 2.418.4 (4th c.AD): τοῦκυρίου NT.Act.931}}.—Construction, a. with _genitive_ _object_, **fear** or **dread of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 116 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.54, etc.; φ. τοῦ στρατεῦσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.18: with double _genitive_, ὀμμάτων εἰληφότας φόβον.. τῆς ἐμῆς ἐπεισόδου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 730: with Preps., φ. ἀπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.37 codices; ὁ ἀπὸ τῶν πολεμίων φ. [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.3.53; οὑξ ὀνειράτων φ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 929; πρός τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 783; πρός τινας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 16.10, 25.93}}; φ. περὶ τοῦ καρποῦ **fear** for or concerning.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.88; φ. ἑκάστων πέρι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 20b; ὑπὲρ τοῦ μέλλοντος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.41; τὸν ἐκ τῶν Ἑλλήνων εἰς τοὺς βαρβάρους φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.18}}; τῷ καθ᾽ ἑαντὸν φ. from personal **fear,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.2: with}}_infinitive_, φόβῳ εἰσορᾶν **from fear** to see, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1342:—for τεθνάναι τῷ φόβῳ τινά, see at {θνῄσκω} I.2.}}]
**II.b** with Verbs, τεύχειν φόβον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1090 (_anapaest meter_); κλάζουσι κώδωνες φ. [prev. author] “Th.” 386; φ. ποιεῖν τοῖς ἵπποις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.18; παρασχεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1113, etc.; παρασκευάζειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.86}}; φόβους ἐμβάλλειν, φόβον ἐνθεῖναί τινι, to strike **terror** into one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.18, “An.” 7.4.1; ἐνεργάσασθαί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.38, 11.25; ἔδωκ᾽ Ἀπόλλων θῆρας φόβῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.61}}; of the person who feels fear, φόβον λαβεῖν, ἔχειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 39, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 11.11; ἐκ φόβου φ. τρέφω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 28: _accusative_ _cognate_, φόβους φοβεῖσθαι, δεδοικέναι, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 360b, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 548}}; τὸν σὸν οὐ ταρβῶ φ. I fear not with thy **fear,** i.e. not like thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1251}}; Ταντάλου φ. φοβεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 6; ἐς φ. κατιστέατο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.12, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.81; ἐν φ. γενέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 578e; φ. μ᾽ ἔχει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1243, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1255}}; μοι φ. τις εἰσελήλυθ᾽, μ᾽ ὑπῆλθέ τις φ.,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1324, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1231; τοῖς Ἕλλησι φ. ἐμπίπτει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.19, etc.}}; διὰ φόβου ἔρχομαι, διὰ φόβων γίγνομαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 757 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 791b: opposed to φόβον λύειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 270, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 104; διαλῦσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 241b; φόβους ἐξαίρει τῶν πολιτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.23; ἀπεληλακέναι τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.10}}; φόβου ἀπαλλάξεσθαι to get rid of **it,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 5.2.32; φόβου ἐκλύσασθαί τινα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1002; φόβους ἀπολύεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1415b18}}; φόβου μεθεῖσα (Valck. φόβον) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 555; φόβου ἔξωθεν εἶναι [prev. author] “El.” 901; ἵνα φόβος εἴη στρατεύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.3}}; οὐ φ. μὴ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 2.1.25}}; φ. ἐστὶν ὅπως μὴ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 193a}}; but φόβος εἰ πείσω I **fear** I shall not persuade.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 184 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἡμέας ἔχειφ. τε καὶ δέος ὅκως χρὴ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.115 (φόβος ἦν ὥστε μὴ τέγξαι is corrupt in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1380): adverbial usages, φόβῳ}} **by** or **through fear,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 786 (Lyric poetry), “Th.” 240 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ἀνάγκῃ καὶ φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 554d: with Preps., διὰ φόβον, διὰ τὸν φ., 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 41, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.38, “Cyr.” 3.1.24; ἐκ τίνος φόβο; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 887; μετὰ φόβων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.26; ἄρχειν ξὺν φόβοισι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 585; προαποθνῄσκειν ὑπὸ τοῦ φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.25; poetical, ἀμφὶ φόβῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 825 (Lyric poetry)}}: _plural_, not only in Poets, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.27, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 134 (probable reading), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 531, etc.}}, but also in Prose, φόβους καὶ δείματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.80; πόνους καὶ φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 635c; κινδύνους καὶ φ. [prev. author] “Tht.” 173a.}}
**II.2** **object** or **cause of terror,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1652}}; φόβος ἀκοῦσαι **a terror** to hear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.112: _plural_, ἢν φόβους λέγῃ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 917; πολλῶν φ. προσαγομένων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.23.}}

5404:
** φοῖνιξ**, ῑκος, ὁ, ἡ,
**Phoenician,** Φοῖνιξ ἀνὴρ ἀπατήλια εἰδώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.288, compare 13.272, 15.415; ὡς Φ. ἀνήρ, Σιδώνιος κάπηλος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 909.}}
**2** _feminine_, γυνή Φοίνισσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.417}}; Φοίνισσαι, name of plays by Euripides, Phrynichus, etc.; also Φ. ἐμπολά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.67}}; χθών, νᾶσος, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 6, 204 (Lyric poetry), etc.; Φ. βοά [prev. work] 301 (Lyric poetry)}}; κώπη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hel.” 1272; Φ. ἄμπεχος “PCair.Zen.” 33.14 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** **Carthaginian,** ἀλαλατός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.72; also _feminine_, Φοίνισσαι νῆες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.116, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.80.}}
**B** **φοῖνιξ**, ῑκος, ὁ, **purple** or **crimson,** because the discovery and earliest use of this colour was ascribed to the Phoenicians, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.141, 6.219, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.201, etc.:}} —hence,
**B.2** as _adjective_ (_feminine_ φοίνισσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (see. below)}}; φοῖνιξ as _feminine_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 815)}}, **blood-bay,** of a horse, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.454}}; of **red** cattle, φοίνισσα ἀγέλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.205, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.128}}: of the colour of fire, φοίνισσα φλόξ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.24}}; πυρὸς φ. πνοά E.[same place]; also φ. ἱμάντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 17; πέπλοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 181 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**B.II** **date-palm, Phoenix dactylifera,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.163, “hymnus ad Apollinem” 117, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 75 14 (uncertain), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 458 (Lyric poetry), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.53; τόξα ἐκ φοίνικος σπάθης πεποιημένα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.69, etc.}}: the male and female distinguished by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus as [ὁ φ.] ἔρσην and [ἡ φ.] βαλανηφόρος, 1.193 (but the latter is _masculine_, [prev. passage] and in 4.172, 182); φοινίκων.. τῶν καρπίμων οἱ μὲν ἄρρενες αἱ δὲ θήλειαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.6.6, but αἱ ἀπὸ τῶν ἀρρένων πρὸς τοὺς θήλεις [βοήθειαι] [prev. work] 2.8.4.}}
**B.II.2** **palm-frond,** as a **badge of victory,** Arist {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“MM” 1196a36, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.723b, etc.; τὸν φ. τινὶ ἀποδοῦναι 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.175.}}
**B.II.3** **date,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hellanicus Historicus 56J., 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 18, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 65, 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 24; more correctly, τοῦφοίνικος ὁ καρπός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193; καρπὸς φοίνικος 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 63.22 (hexameters)}}; compare φοινικοβάλανος.
**B.III** ὁ χαμαιρριφής **dwarf-palm, Chamaerops humilis,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.6.11.}}
**B.III.2** a Bactrian tree, **Mazri palm, Nannorhops ritchieana,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.4.8.}}
**B.III.3** a sea-plant, **Callophyllis laciniata,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.6.2, 10.}}
**B.III.4** **rye-grass, Lolium perenne,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.43.}}
**B.IV** a musical instrument, like a **guitar,** invented by the Phoenicians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.192, 4th c.BC: Ephorus Historicus 4 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, Phillis 2 (_plural_), Scamon 3}}; but so called because made from the Delian palm, _accusative_ to {{Refs|Semus 1.}}
**B.V** the fabulous bird **phoenix,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 171.4, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 175; from Arabia _accusative_ to 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.73}}; but from India, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.49: proverbial, φοίνικος ἔτη βιοῦν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 53.}}
**B.VI** an **ornament,** {{LXX|LXX.Eze.41.25.}}
**B.VII** **perfume prepared from the fronds of the date-palm,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 28.}}
**B.VIII** a **fish,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 12.24.}}
**B.IX** a **bandage,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 49.11.2.}}
**B.X** ={εὐρύνοτος}, {{Refs|Agathemerus Geographus 2.7.}}
**B.XI** φ. ἐν ὁπλῇ, a disease of the hoof, {{Refs|“Hippiatrica” 10.}}
**B.XII** an eye-salve, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 7.116. [In all senses of the word ῑ in _genitive_, but _nominative_ φοῖνιξ, not φοίνιξ, Hdn.Gr. cited in 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “in Thd.” 1.292.]}}

5406:
** φον-εύς**, ὁ,
_genitive_ έως, _Epic dialect_ ῆος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.632}}; _accusative_ φονέα (in first foot of _trimeter_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 362, 721; in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus φονέᾰ, “Hec.” 882}}: _nominative_ _plural_ φονέες {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lesbonax Rhetor 3.8}}, _Attic dialect_ φονῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.7; _accusative_ φονέας [prev. author] 2.3.8, 4.3.1, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.96, Callias Epigrammaticus in “PSI” 11.1218a32, etc.}}; but _contraction_ φονεῖς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.162e}}:—**slayer,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same place], 18.335, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.434, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.45, etc.; δικαιοτάτου δὲ φονῆος Pisander Epicus 10; τῷ φονεῖ τἀδελφοῦ τὴν δεξιὰν δέδωκε 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 18.6.; αὐτόχειρας καὶ φονέας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.111}}; φονέας αὑτῶν self-**murderers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator}} [same place]; τοσούτῳ μᾶλλον φονεύς ἐστιν is so much more justly accounted a **murderer,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.3; οὐχὶ τὴν ἐμὴν φονέα νομίζων χεῖρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 586; ἀκουσίως τινὸς φ. γενέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 451a}}; of the sword on which Ajax had thrown himself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1026: as _feminine_, μητέρα φονέα οὖσαν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.3 (ὁ φ., even of a woman, [prev. work]20).}}
**2** σοῦ φονέως μεμνημένος you, my **destroyer,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1361.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, τῆς ὑμετέρας εὐσεβείας φονῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.7.}}

5407:
** φον-εύω**,
**murder, kill,** τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.35, 211, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 340 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 716, etc.}}; with _double_ _accusative_, [φόνον] φ. τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 335: _absolutely_, καὶ τίς φονεύε; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1174, compare “El.” 34}}:—_passive_, **to be slain,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.17, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1317 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.95.}}
**2** of an animal, ἐὰν.. ζῷον.. τι φονεύσῃ τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 873e.}}
**3** **stain with blood,** φασγάνῳ δέρην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 875 (troch.).}}

5408:
** φόνος**, ὁ,
(θείνω) **murder, slaughter,** τεύξασα πόσει φόνον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.430; τοίσδεσσι φόνον καὶ κῆρα φυτεύει 2.165; φ. ῥάπτειν 16.379; μερμηρίζειν 2.325; ὁρμαίνειν 4.843; σμικρῇσι φόνον φέρει ὀρνίθεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.757, etc.; φόνον πράσσειν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.46; ἀκούσιον φ. ἐξεργάσασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 869a; βουλεῦσαί τινι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1055; ἔθου φόνον [prev. author] “OC” 542 (Lyric poetry); ἐκπορίζειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1114}}; of arrows, φ. προπέμπειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 105}}; τὸν Δωριέος πρὸς Ἐγεσταίων φόνον ἐκπρήξασθαι exact vengeance for the **killing**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.158}}; κατὰ ζῴων φόνου καὶ μὴ φόνου ὧδε ἔχει **killing** or not-**killing,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 257}}; in _poetry_ phrases, φ. συρίζειν, κινύρεσθαι, πνεῖν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 357 (assuming variant), “Th.” 123 (Lyric poetry), “Ag.” 1309}}; φ. τινός the **murder** of.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 580, etc.}}; φ. Ἑλληνικὸς μέγιστος **slaughter** of Greeks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.170; ὅμαιμος αὐθέντης φ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 212; πατρῷος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 955}}; πολύκερως, ἄρνειος φ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 55, 309; ἐπὶ φόνῳ πράσσεις φόνον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1579, compare “HF” 1084 (Lyric poetry); γέρων φ. μηκέτ᾽ ἐν δόμοις τέκοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 805 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ὁ ὑπὸ Θήβης Ἀλεξάνδρον φ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.856a; ὁ κατὰ τῶν πολιτῶν φ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.8: _plural_, φόνοι τ᾽ ἀνδροκτασίαι τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.612 (personified in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 228); ἔμφυλοι φ. ἀνδρῶν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 51, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 962.}}
**2** in law, **murder, homicide,** δικάζειν τοὺς βασιλέας αἰτιῶν φόνου Legal Dracontis cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.115.12; φόνου διώκειν τινά 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.9; δικάζειν δίκας φόνου [prev. author] 5.11; παραδοῦναι φόνου δίκην [prev. author] 6.42; ἁλῶναι [prev. author] 5.59, etc.; φεύγειν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 133 (poetry, παίδων φόνον φεύγουσα}} fleeing from.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 795)}}; ἔνοχοι τῷ φόνῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.11; φόνου ὑπόδικος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.25}}; φόνου καθαρός, ἁγνός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 451b, “Lg.” 759c: ἀκούσιος φ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.72; φόνων ἀπέχεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1032 (_anapaest meter_); αἱ τῶν φ. δίκαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 778d; φόνοι.. φόνοις δεόμενοι καθαίρεσθαι [prev. work] 870c, +others}}; λαγχάνονται αἱ τοῦ φ. δίκαι πρὸς [τὸν βασιλέα] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 57.2.}}
**3** **death as a punishment,** φ. προκεῖσθαι δημόλευστον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 36.}}
**4** **blood when shed, gore,** ἂμ φόνον, ἂν νέκυας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.298; κέατ᾽ ἐν φόνῳ 24.610; ἐρευγόμενοι φόνον αἵματος 16.162; φ. κέχυται γυναίκων 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “fragment” 153 Lobel; φόνον κεύθειν 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 100.4; μέλανι ῥαίνων φόνῳ πεδίον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).55; φόνου κηκίς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1012; ἐμοῦσα θρόμβους οὓς ἀφείλκυσας φόνου [prev. author] “Eu.” 184; σταγόνες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1278; σταλαγμοί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 241; χεῖρα χραίνεσθαι φόνῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 43}}; of a sacrifice, ταυρείου φόνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 44; Ἕλλην οὗ καταστάζει φ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 72}}; rarely in Prose of **blood,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.73.}}
**5** **corpse,** πρὶν ἴδω τὸν Ἑλένας φόνον.. κείμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1357 (Lyric poetry); ἐπὶ φόνῳ χαμαιπετεῖ ματρός [prev. work] 1491 (Lyric poetry).}}
**6** **rascal that deserves death, gallowsbird,** a Dorian phrase, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 662.4.}}
**II** of the agent or instrument of slaughter, φόνον ἔμμεναι ἡρώεσσι to be **a death** to heroes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.144, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.24}}; of poison, {{LXX|“Mim.Oxy.” 413.180; ἐν φόνῳ μαχαίρας LXX.Exo.17.13, LXX.Deu.13.15(16), 20.13}}; without ἐν, {{LXX|LXX.Num.21.24.}}
**III** ={ἀτρακτυλίς}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.4.6.}}

5409:
** φορ-έω**,
_Epic dialect_ _subjunctive_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ φορέῃσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.328, 9.10}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ φορῆναι (as if from *φόρημι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.107, 7.149, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.224; φορήμεναι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.310:}}_imperfect_ ἐφόρεον(-εο- syniz.) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.456}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐφόρει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.137}}; _Ionic dialect_ φορέεσκον {{Refs|2.770, 13.372}}: _future_ φορήσω {{LXX+5th c.BC+|“Scol.” 9 (compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 632), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.32; later φορέσω LXX.Pro.16.23}}: _aorist_ ἐφόρησα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.95 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 213}}, _Epic dialect_ φόρησα {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.11, (δια-, ἐκ-) 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.43, 42; later ἐφόρεσα LXX.Sir.11.5, _falsa lectio_ in 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.7, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 48(24).80, “Sammelb.” 7247.33 (3rd-4th c.AD)}}:—_middle_, _future_ φορήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; in _passive_ sense, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.398d:}}_aorist_ ἐφορησάμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(ἐξ-) 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.39}}:—_passive_, _Aeolic dialect_ _present_ φορήμεθα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.4:}}_aorist_ ἐφορήθην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|(ἐν-) 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.703b}}: _perfect_ πεφόρημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 52a}}; _pluperfect_ πεφόρητο {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 816:}} — _frequentative verb_ of φέρω, implying **repeated** or **habitual** action, ἵπποι οἳ φορέεσκον ἀμύμονα Πηλεΐωνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.770, compare 10.323; τά τε νῆες φορέουσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.390}}; of a slave, ὕδωρ ἐφόρει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|10.358, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.457; μέθυ οἰνοχόος φ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.10; θαλλὸν ἐρίφοισι φ. 17.224}}; of the wind, **bear to and fro, bear along,** ἄνεμος ἄχνας φορέει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.499, compare 21.337, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.328; σώματα.. κύμαθ᾽ ἁλὸς.. φορέουσι 12.68; τόφρα δέ μ᾽ αἰεὶ κῦμα φ. 6.171}}; so ἀγγελίας ἐφόρεε **conveyed** messages **habitually, served as a messenger.** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.34 (nisi to be read ἐσεφόρεε)}}; φ. θρεπτήρια, of Oedipus **carrying about** food in a wallet, like a beggar, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1262; λόγχαν ἔτη ἐφόρησε ἓξ ἐν τᾷ γνάθῳ “IG” 42(1).121.95 (Epid.. 4th c.BC): _absolutely_, ἐγ γαστρὶ ἐφόρει τρία ἔτη}} **was pregnant,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 14}}:—_passive_, see #11.
**2** most commonly of clothes, armour, and the like, **bear constantly, wear,** [σκῆπτρον] ἐν παλάμῃς φ. δικασπόλοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.238; μίτρης ἣν ἐφόρει 4.137; θώρηξ χάλκεος, ὃν φορέεσκε 13.372, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.127, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71, etc.; φ. ἐσθήματα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 269; στολάς [prev. author] “OC” 1357; ζεῦγος ἐμβάδων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 872; ἱμάτιον [prev. author] “Pl.” 991, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 197b; δακτύλιον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 883.}}
**3** of features, qualities, etc., of mind or body, **possess, hold, bear,** ἀγλαΐας φ. to be pompous or splendid, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.245; φ. ὄνομα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 658; ἦθος [prev. author] “Ant.” 705; δόξαν “Arch.Papyrus” 1.220 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἕνα γομφίον μόνον φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1059; γλῶτταν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 51}}; ἀπόνοιαν φορεῖς you are mad, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 1.53.15 (4th c.AD)}}; with _genitive_ or _adjective_ added, σκέλεα φ. γεράνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.76; ἰσχυρὰς φ. τὰς κεφαλάς [prev. author] 3.12, compare 101; ποδώκη τὸν τρόπον φ. “Tragica Adespota” 519; γένειον διηλιφὲς φ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 564; ὑπόπτερον δέμας φ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 619; λῆμα θούριον φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 757; ῥύγχος φ. ὕειον 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 11; καλάμινα σκέλη φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Comedy texts 184; ὥσπερ σέλινον οὖλα τὰ σκέλη φ. “Comica Adespota” 208; τὸ στόμ᾽ ὡς κομψὸν φ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 98.21 (troch.).}}
**4** **bear, suffer,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” pp.59,62 O. (uncertain reading in both), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.692d, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.298.}}
**5** of Time, **extend, last,** ἃ φορεῖ ἐπὶ ἡμέρας δεκαπέντε uncertain meaning in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“PFlor.” 384.54 (5th c.AD).}}
**II** _passive_, **to be borne along,** ἐν ῥοθίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 362 (Lyric poetry); φορούμενος πρὸς οὖδας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 752}}; κόνις δ᾽ ἄνω φορεῖθ᾽{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 715; ἄνω τε καὶ κάτω φ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 689; πολλοῖς διαύλοις κυμάτων φ. [prev. author] “Hec.” 29, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.398d}}; πεφορημένον ἀεί always **in motion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 52a}}: hence, **to be storm-tossed,** νᾶϊ φορήμεθα σὺν μελαίνᾳ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 144; ποσσὶ φ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.83, compare 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.23: _metaphorically_, δόξαις φορεῖται τοπαζόμενα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 976a.}}
**II.2** **to be carried away,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76}}; simply, **to be shifted,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 293.}}
**III** _middle_, **fetch for oneself, fetch regularly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 309}}; λευκανίηνδε φορεύμενος **putting** food into **one's** mouth, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.192.}}

5411:
** φόρος**, ὁ,
(φέρω) **that which is brought in by way of payment, tribute,** φόρου ἀπαγωγή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.6, 27, compare “IG” 12.65.2, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.96, etc.}}; ξυμμάχους φόρου ὑποτελεῖς subject to pay **tribute,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.56}}; φόρον ὑποτελέειν to pay **tribute,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.171, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.116; ἀπάγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 707 (_anapaest meter_); φέρειν [prev. author] “Av.” 191, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.7, “Ath.” 2.1; πόλεις ἃς οἱ ἰδιῶται ἐνέγραψαν φ. φέρειν “IG” 12.212.88}}; φ. τάξασθαι to agree to pay **it** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.13}}; φόρον ταῖς πόλεσι τάξαι to fix their quotas of **tribute,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.120, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.209, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.23}}; φ. δέχεσθαι to receive **it,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.96 (of the Ἑλληνοταμίαι),compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 3.2; φ. προσῄει it came in, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.9; τὸν φ. ἀπὸ τῶν πόλεων τὸν προσιόντα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 657 (_anapaest meter_): _plural_, φόροι ἥκουσιν [prev. author] “Ach.” 505, compare “Eq.” 313 (troch.): ὁ βασιλικὸς φ., at Sparta, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.123a.}}
**2** generally, **any payment,** φόρον ἀπέφερον τῷ δήμῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.32}}; κατὰ φόρους by **instalments,** Senatus consultum cited in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.44.7}}; ἐπιβαλλειν φ. impose a **forced levy,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 24.}}

5413:
** φορτ-ίον**, τό,
**load, burden, freight,** {{LXX+7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 9.13 (_plural_), 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 352, “Lys.” 312, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.13.6, “An.” 7.1.37, +others, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 96, 2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.2Ki.15.33; λιβανωτικὰ φ. “OGI” 132.11 (Alexandria, 2nd c.BC)}}; φέρων ἀνθράκων φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 214 (Lyric poetry); φ. βαστάζειν 3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus p.10H.}}
**2** _plural_, **wares, merchandise,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 643, 693, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1, 2.179, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 899, 910, “V.” 1398, “Ra.” 573, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 6.}}
**2.b** especially of agricultural **produce, crops,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PRev.Laws 43.14, +others (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 105.24 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**3** of **a child in the womb,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.5}}; ἔρωτος _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 170.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, μεῖζον φ. ἢ καθ᾽ αὑτὸν αἰρόμενον taking too heavy a **burden** upon him, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.14; μέγα τὸ φ. 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 3; οὐκ ἔστιν οὐδὲν βαρύτερον τῶν φορτίων.. γυναικός [prev. author] 329; οὔτοι τὸ γῆράς ἐστιν.. τῶν φ. μέγιστον 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 53; τὸ φ. μου ἐλαφρόν ἐστιν NT.Matt.11.30}}; χρυσοῦν φ., of wealth, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 9. (_diminutive_ only in form, commonly used for φόρτος in Comedy texts and Prose; wrongly condemned as un-Attic by Moer. p.393 P., Thom.Mag. p.16R.)}}

5414:
** φόρτος**, ὁ,
(φέρω) **load, freight, cargo,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.163, 14.296, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 631, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 537}}, and later Prose, as {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 2.11 (3rd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marcellus” 14, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.34}}; ἐποιήσαντό με φ., explained as πεπραγμάτευμαι, προδέδομαι, φόρτος γεγένημαι, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 4.10P.}}; φ. ἔρωτος, of Europa on the bull, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|Batrachomyomachia 78, compare 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 4.118.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **heavy load** or **burden,** φ. χρείας, κακῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 20, “IT” 1306}}; compare φορτίον.
**II** _Attic dialect_, **vulgar stuff, rubbish, balderdash,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 748 (_anapaest meter_) “Pl.” 796.}}
**III** **mass of detail,** 'stuff', in semi-colloquial sense, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.4.}}

5416:
** φραγέλλ-ιον**, τό,
= Latin _flagellum,_ {{NT|NT.John.2.15.}}
**II** name of a weight, φ. σιδαρᾶ β {{Refs 4th c.AD+|Petersen-Luschan “Reisen in Lykien“ No.77a (4th c.AD).}}

5418:
** φραγ-μός**, ὁ,
**fencing in, blocking up,** τῆς ἀκουούσης πηγῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1387.}}
**2** **intestinal obstruction,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Caelius Aurelianus Medicus “CP” 3.17.}}
**II** **fence, paling,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 11.4, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.343 (1st c.BC: Archias) “BGU” 1119.32 (1st c.BC), NT.Matt.21.33, etc.}}; **hedge,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 385}}; **railing** of the bridge over the Hellespont, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.36}}: **fortification,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 142}}; of the diaphragm, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φυσῶν” 10, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 672b20}}; of the **shard** of beetles, {{Refs|[prev. work] 682b17}}; of the teeth, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.93.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **partition,** {{NT|NT.Eph.2.14.}}
**II.2.b** nickname of a man **with a bristly beard,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pseudol.” 27.}}

5419:
** φράζω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 25, etc.}}: _poetry_ _imperfect_ φράζον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.61:}}_future_ φράσω [ᾰ] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 781, etc.}}: _aorist 1_ ἔφρᾰσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Venerem” 128, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 442, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.150, etc.}}; _poetry_ φράσα [ᾰ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.22, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 231 (Lyric poetry); _imperative_ φράσσατε 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.117}}: _perfect_ πέφρᾰκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.93, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.23: _Epic dialect_}}_aorist_ πέφρᾰδον, ἐπέφραδον used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} mostly in _3rd.pers._ _singular_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.500, +others (in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.273, 8.142, πέφραδε is _imperative_)}}; _optative_ πεφράδοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.335}}; _infinitive_ πεφραδέειν, πεφραδέμεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.477, 7.49}}; _1st pers._ _singular_ ἐπέφραδον only {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.127}}: also _aorist 1_ _participle_ _genitive_ φράδαντος uncertain in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).261.15 (Mantinea, 6th c.BC)}}:— _middle_ and _passive_, φράζομαι, _Epic dialect_ _imperative_ φράζεο, φράζευ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.440, 9.251}}; _infinitive_ φράζεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.294: _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _sing._}}_imperfect_ φράζετο, φραζέσκετο,{{Refs|11.624, “hymnus ad Apollinem” 346:}}_future_ φράσομαι [ᾰ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.234}}, _Epic dialect_ φράσσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.238}}: _aorist 1_ ἐφρᾰσάμην {{Refs|17.161}}, _Epic dialect_ φρᾰσάμην {{Refs|23.75}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ and _plural_ ἐφράσσατο, φράσσαντο,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.529, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.671; _imperative_ φράσαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.331, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 113}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ φράσσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.217}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ φράσσασθαι Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.57}}: _aorist_ _passive_ ἐφράσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.485, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 546:}}_perfect_ πέφρασμαι (in _middle_ sense) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 438, (in _passive_ sense) 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.195}}; _participle_, προ-πεφραδμένος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 655}}.—The _aorist_ _middle_ is chiefly _Epic dialect_, also {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 94, 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 5.4, 34.1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 113, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 653 (Lyric poetry)}}:—**point out, show** (never **say, tell,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus _accusative_ to 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus), ἐς χῶρον ὃν φράσε Κίρκη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.22, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.138, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.424}}; also, **show the way to, show where to find,** ᾗ οἱ Ἀθήνη πέφραδε δῖον ὑφορβόν {{Refs|14.3, compare 8.68; σήματ᾽.., τά οἱ ἔμπεδα πέφραδ᾽ Ὀδυσσεύς}}**showed,** {{Refs|19.250}}; μῦθον πέφραδε πᾶσιν **show, make known** the word to all, {{Refs|1.273, compare 8.142; δεῖξε καὶ ἔφρασε “hymnus ad Venerem” 128}}; φράσσατέ μοι δόμους **show** me them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.117; ἔφρασε τὴν ἀτραπόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.213; κόποι αὐτόματοι φράζουσι νούσους 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aph.” 2.5; δμώων δή τινα.. μοι φράσον 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.47; τὸ παράδειγμα ὃ ἂν φράζῃ ὁ ἀρχιτέκτων “IG” 22.1668.96: _absolutely_, φωνῆσαι μὲν οὐκ εἶχε, τῇ δὲ χειρὶ ἔφραζε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.113; ἀντὶ φωνῆς φράζε.. χερί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1061.}}
**2** **show forth, tell, declare,** λόγον, ἔπος, ὄνομα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.60, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 173 (troch.), “Supp.” 320; φ. τοῖσι ἥκουσι τὰ πρήγματα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.100; ἑλοῦ γάρ, ἢ πόνωντὰ λοιπά σοι φράσω σαφηνῶς, ἢ τὸν ἐκλύσοντ᾽ ἐμέ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 781; τιπρός τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 359 (_anapaest meter_), etc.: with double _accusative_, φ. τινά τι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.100}}; τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 267b; περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.117 (see. below); ἐπί τινος [prev. author] “Ep.” 6.8}}: rarely with _genitive_, **tell of,** τῆς μητρὸς ἥκω τῆς ἐμῆς φράσων, ἐν οἷς νῦν ἐστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1122: followed by a _relative_ clause, φ. ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278b, etc.; φ. ὡς δεῖ γεωργεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 16.8; φ. οἷ᾽ ἐπορσύνθη κακά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 267, compare “Pr.” 995, etc.}}; with double _construction_, φ. τό τε ὄνομα καὶ ἐν ᾗ κώμῃ οἰκοῦσιν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PRev.Laws” 29.5 (3rd c.BC)}}: rarely with _participle_, πεφραδέειν πόσιν ἔνδον ἐόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.477; οὐκ ἔφραζες σῆς προκείμενον νέκυν γυναικός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1012}}: later with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus}} [same place]; **explain** (opposed to λέγω, which means simply **speak, say**), φράσον ἅπερ γ᾽ ἔλεξας **declare, explain** what thou didst say, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 559; φράζε δὴ τί φῄς [prev. author] “OT” 655; φράζουσιν ἃ λέγει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.18; φράζε λόγῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 49, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 814c; οὐχ ἁπλῶς εἰπεῖν ἀλλὰ σαφῶς φράσαι περὶ αὐτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.117, compare “Ep.” 1.2; σαφῶς φ. τοῖς βουλομένοις συνιέναι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.129}}; of teachers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 180b}}; of oracles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1048, “Pl.” 46, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 923a, etc.}}; of letters, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 15: _absolutely_, τοῦτο φράζει ὅτι}} this **signifies** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.30.}}
**2.b** **cultivate style** or **phrasing,** opposed to αὐτὸ τὸ γράφειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Duris Epigrammaticus 1 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**3** with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **tell** one to do so and so, ἵνα γάρ σφιν ἐπέφραδον ἠγερέθεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.127}}; δὴ γάρ μοι ἐπέφραδε.. Κίρκη (i.e. ἰέναι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.549; τοῖς ἀνθρώποισι φ. σιγᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 98 (_anapaest meter_); τὰ ὅπλα ὑπολαβεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.58, compare 3.15.}}
**4** _absolutely_, **give counsel, advise, suggest,** δόλος ἦν ὁ φράσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 197 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** _middle_ and _passive_, **indicate to oneself,** i.e. **think** or **muse upon, consider, ponder,** _Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, Trag., but not in _Attic dialect_ Prose; εὔκηλος τὰ φράζεαι ἅσσ᾽ ἐθέλῃσθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.554}}; φράζεσθαι βουλήν, βουλάς,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|18.313, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.510; ἐνὶ φρεσὶ μῆτιν ἀμείνω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.423; πάντα μετὰ φρεσίν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 688; θυμῷ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.646; ἐφράσθη καὶ ἐς θυμὸν ἐβάλετο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; πρὸς ταῦτα φράζου}}**bethink thee,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 383}}; ἀμφὶς φ. **to think** differently, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.14: followed by εἰ}} with _future_ _indicative_, **consider** whether..,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.83, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.192.}}
**II.2** **purpose, plan, contrive,** φ. τινὶ κακά, θάνατον, ὄλεθρον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.367, 3.242, 13.373; μέγ᾽ ὄνειαρ 4.444; ἐσθλά 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.212; φράσσατο Πατρόκλῳ μέγα ἠρίον 23.126}}; φράσσεται ὥς κε νέηται **will contrive** how.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.205; φ. ὅπως ὄχ᾽ ἄριστα γένοιτο 3.129, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1041.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **think, suppose, believe, imagine** that.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.624; οὐκ ἐφράζετο δυνατὸς εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.154.}}
**II.4** **perceive, observe,** οἷον ἐγὼν οἰωνὸν.. ἐφρασάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.161}}; τὴν (i.e. τὴν οὐλὴν) ἀπονίζουσα φρασάμην {{Refs|23.75}}; with a _participle_, τὸν δὲ φράσατο προσιόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.339, compare 23.453: later with _genitive_, χειμῶνος 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 745; πομπᾶς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.84: rarely with}}_participle_, ψυχὰν Ἀΐδᾳ τελέων οὐ φράζεται **marks** not that he will die, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.68.}}
**II.5** **watch, guard,** [ὀρσοθύρην] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.129.}}
**II.6** **beware of,** ξύλινον λόχον Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.57: frequently in _imperative_, φράζευ κύνα}}**cave canem,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1030 (hexameters); φράσσαι κυναλώπεκα μή σε δολώσῃ [prev. work] 1067 (hexameters)}}; φράζεο δή, μὴ.. μάρψῃ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pax” 1099 (hexameters), compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Lavacrum Palladis” 52}}: with _infinitive_, φράζου μὴ πόρσω φωνεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 213 (Lyric poetry): _absolutely_, φράζου}}**take care!** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 130}}. (Perh. cf. Lith **girdziù** 'I hear', _infinitive_ **girdēti.**)

5420:
** φράσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ φράττω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 2.9, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.17, +others:}}_future_ φράξω {{Refs|(δια-) “IG” 22.1668.63, etc.:}}_aorist_ ἔφραξα, _Epic dialect_ φράξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.263, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.256, etc.}}, _Attic dialect_ _infinitive_ φάρξαι {{Refs|“IG” 12.371.20}}_participle_ δια-φάρξας{{Refs|[prev. work] 373.251}}: _perfect_ πέφρᾰκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.350, later πέφρᾰγα (περι-) Scholia 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 298}}: _pluperfect_ ἐμ-πεφράκεσαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.8.5}}:—_middle_, see at {φράγνυμι}; _future_ φράξομαι (ἐμ-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 19:}}_aorist_ ἐφραξάμην, _Epic dialect_ φρ- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.566:}} —_passive_, _future_ φραχθήσομαι {{NT+2nd c.AD+|(ἐμ-) 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.616; φρᾰγήσομαι NT.2Cor.11.10}}: _aorist 1_ ἐφράχθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.268, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 84d:}}_aorist 2_ ἐφράγην [ᾰ], _subjunctive_ φρᾰγῇ {{NT|NT.Rom.3.19}}, _participle_ φρᾰγείς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “(Spiritalia) Pneumatica” 1.19}}: _perfect_ πέφραγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 733, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ ἐπεφράγμην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sat.” 11}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐπέφρακτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.142:— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses no tense but}}_aorist_ _active_, _passive_, and _middle_ The _Attic dialect_ spellings πεφαργμένος and ἐφάρξαντο[{{Refs|are given by Hdn.Gr. 2.384, compare “IG” 12.[same places], and see at {φαρκτός}, ναύφαρκτος. [ᾰ}}] by nature, for it does not become η in _Ionic dialect_]:— **fence in, hedge round,** hence with collateral notion of defence, **secure, fortify,** ῥινοῖσι βοῶν φράξαντες ἐπάλξεις **having fenced** the battlements with shields, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.263}}; φράξε δέ μιν [τὴν σχεδίην] ῥίπεσσι he **fenced** it with mats, to keep out the water, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.256; ἀρκύστατ᾽ ἂν φράξειεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1376; φ. δέμας ὅπλοισι [prev. author] “Pers.” 456}}; ἔρνεσι φ. χεῖρα **fill** them **full** with wreaths of victory, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.66:}} —_middle_, φράξαντο δὲ νῆας ἕρκεϊ χαλκείῳ they **fencedin their** ships, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.566, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 63; φραξάμενοι τὴν ἀκρόπολιν θύρῃσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.51; πύλας.. ἐφραξάμεσθα προστάταις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 798}}; but ἐφράξαντο τὸ τεῖχος they **strengthened** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.70}}: _absolutely_, **strengthen one's fortifications,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.35; φ. πρὸς τὰς διαβάσεις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 23; φραξάμενοι}}**in battle-array,** {{Refs|Batrachomyomachia 166:}} —_passive_, φραχθέντες σάκεσιν **fenced** with shields, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.268, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 826, etc.; ἡ ἀκρόπολις ῥηχῷ ἐπέφρακτο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.142}}; of the Nile, to be **embanked,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.99: _absolutely_, πεφραγμένοι}}**fortified, prepared for defence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.34, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.82}}; of a person, **armed,** ἀσπιδίτης καὶ πεφρ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 426: _metaphorically_, ἐλπίδος πεφραγμένος}} **having the defence** of hope, conjecture in {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 235 (codex Laur, πεπραγμένος, cett. and Scholia δεδραγμένος).}}
**II** **put up as a fence,** φράξαντες δόρυ δουρί, σάκος σάκεϊ **joining** spear **close** to spear, shield to shield, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.130}}; φράξαντες τὰ γέρρα **having put up** the shields **as a close, thick fence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.61:}} —_middle_ πάγας ὑπερκότους ἐφραξάμεσθα conjecture in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 823.}}
**II.2** σχάσασαι τὴν οὐρὰν καὶ φ., of dogs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.5.}}
**III** **stop up, block,** τὴν ὁδόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.7; τοὺς ἔσπλους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.13; τὰ παρασκήνια 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.17:}} —_passive_, ὑπὸ ῥευμάτων φραχθείς [ὁ πλεύμων] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus [same place]; πεφραγμένων τῶν πόρων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 935b14.}}
**III.2** _metaphorically_, **bar, stop,** τὸ ἡγεμονικόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.157d}}:—_passive_, ἵνα πᾶν στόμα φραγῇ {{NT|NT.Rom.3.19; ἡ καύχησις αὕτη οὐ φραγήσεται εἰς ἐμέ NT.2Cor.11.10.}}

5421:
** φρέ-αρ**,
epigram **φρεῖαρ** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 486, τό, _genitive_ φρέᾱτος (see. entry near the end)}}, _contraction_ φρητός {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IGRom.” 1.1167 C 6 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}, Hdn.Gr.1.409; _Epic dialect_ _dative_ φρέᾰτι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 99 (assuming variant), φρητί 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 15}}; _plural_ φρέᾱτα, also φρῆτα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 499.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ _plural_ φρείᾰτα (see. below):—**an artificial well** (thus distinguished from κρήνη,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.120, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.30}}; but φ. ἀσφάλτον naphtha-**spring,** {{LXX+8th c.BC+|LXX.Gen.14.10, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.119), πᾶσαι κρῆναι καὶ φρείατα μακρὰ νάουσιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.197}}; the stem φρεατ - first in {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” [prev. cited]}}
**2** later, **tank, cistern, reservoir,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.48, 49, “PHal.” 1.98 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; εἰς φ. καταβαίνειν καὶ κολυμβᾶν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 193c, compare “Prt.” 350a; φ. ὀρώρυκται 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.129; ποιητὰ φ., see at {ποιητός} 1}}]: generally, **pit,** φ. διαφθορᾶς {{LXX|LXX.Psa.54(55).24.}}
**2.b** perhaps **oil-jar,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 810.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, εἰς φρέατά τε καὶ πᾶσαν ἀπορίαν ἐμπίπτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 174c; ἐν φρέατι συσχόμενος [prev. work] 165b}}; ἡ περὶ τὸ φ. ὄρχησις, proverbial of persons 'on the brink of a volcano', {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.68b}}; λύκος περὶ φ. χορεύει proverbial cited in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}; πίνειν ἐξ ἀργυροῦ φρέατος, i.e. a large wine-cup, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.192a, cf. Chamaeleon cited in [same work] 11.461c}}. [_Attic dialect_ _genitive_ φρέᾱτος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1004, “fragment” 295, 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 57 (troch.), 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 179, 4th-3rd c.BC: Apollodorus Gelous Comicus 1.] (Orig. frhvṛ}} _genitive_ frhvṇτος, cf. Armenian _albiur_ 'well', Goth. and O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **brunna** 'stream, burn', Latin _ferveo_, **defrutum.**)

5424:
** φρήν**, ἡ,
_genitive_ φρενός, _plural_ φρένες, _genitive_ φρενῶν, _dative_ φρεσί: older _dative_ _plural_ φρασί (ν) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 12.971 (6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.62, “BMus.Inscription” 909 (Halic., 1st c.BC): (see. entry near the end)}}:
**I** **midriff,** κραδία φρένα λακτίζει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 881 (_anapaest meter_)}}; elsewhere always in _plural_, ἔνθα φρένες ἔρχαται ἀμφ᾽ ἁδινὸν κῆρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.481, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 22, “Art.” 41}}; τὰς φρένας διάφραγμα εἰς τὸ μέσον αὐτῶν (i.e. τοῦ θώρακος καὶ τοῦ κύτους) τιθέντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 70a; τοῦτο δὲ τὸ διάζωμα καλοῦσί τινες φρένας, ὃ διορίζει τόν τε πλεύμονα καὶ τὴν καρδίαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 672b11, compare “HA” 496b11, 506a6; also, in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, more vaguely, πρὸς στῆθος ὅθι φρένες ἧπαρ ἔχουσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.301; μένεος φρένες ἀμφὶ μέλαιναι πίμπλαντ᾽ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.103, +others; φρένας.. εἰς αὐτὰς τυπείς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 363, compare “Eu.” 159 (Lyric poetry).}}
**I.2** **heart,** as seat of the passions, e.g. of fear, τρομέοντο δέ οἱ φρένες ἐντός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.10}}; of joy and grief, φρένα τέρπεσθαι φόρμιγγι {{Refs|9.186; γάνυται φρένα ποιμήν 13.493; τί σε φρένας ἵκετοπένθο; 1.362; ἄχος πύκασε φρένας 8.124; ἔρως φρένας ἀμφεκάλυψε 3.442}}; of anger, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.147}}; of courage, ἕνα φρεσὶ θυμὸν ἔχοντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.487; ἐς φρένα θυμὸς ἀγέρθη 22.475, compare 8.202}}, etc.; of bodily appetites, such as hunger, etc., {{Refs|11.89}}: the shades of the dead are without it, ψυχὴ καὶ εἴδωλον, ἀτὰρ φρένες οὐκ ἔνι πάμπαν {{Refs|23.104}} (except the shade of Teiresias, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.493)}}: so generally in Poets, φρενὸς ἔνδοθεν ἄλγεα κεῖται {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 5.2; κῆλα δαιμόνων θέλγει φρένας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.12; φόβος μ᾽ ἔχει φρένας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 379; μαινομένα φρενί [prev. author] “Th.” 484 (Lyric poetry); στυγεῖν μιᾷ φρενί [prev. author] “Eu.” 986 (Lyric poetry); Διὸς γὰρ δυσπαραίτητοι φ. [prev. author] “Pr.” 34}}; ἐκ φρενός from one's **very heart,** ὁ ἐκ φρενὸς λόγος a **sincere** speech, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 107; ἐτύμως δακρυχέων ἐκ φρενός [prev. author] “Th.” 919 (Lyric poetry)}}; οὐκ ἀπ᾽ ἄκρας φρενός not **superficially and carelessly,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 805 (_anapaest meter_); φρενὸς ἐκ φιλίας [prev. work] 1515 (_anapaest meter_), compare 546}}; φῦσαι φρένας to produce **a haughty spirit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1463.}}
**I.3** **mind,** as seat of the mental faculties, perception, thought, ἔγνω ᾗσιν ἐνὶ φ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.296; μή μοι ταῦτα νόει φρεσί 9.600}}; μετὰ φρεσὶ μερμηρίξαι, βάλλεσθαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.438, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.434; ἴδμεν ἐνὶ φρεσίν 2.301}}; τῷ γὰρ ἐπὶ φρεσί θῆκε put in his **mind, suggested** it,{{Refs|1.55; σφῶϊν δ᾽ ὧδε θεῶν τις ἐνὶ φρεσὶ ποιήσειεν 13.55; ἐν φρεσὶ θέσθε ἕκαστος [prev. work] 121, compare 1.297, etc.; φρένας παραπεῖσαι, πείθειν, 7.120, 16.842}}; ἐπιγνάμπτει φρένας (variant for{νόον}) ἐσθλῶν {{Refs|9.514; Διὸς ἐτράπετο φρήν 10.45}}; ἀνὴρ φρένας ἀφνειός rich (only) **in his imagination,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 455}}; ὀρθᾷ, ἐλευθέρᾳ φρενί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.24, “P.” 2.57; φρένες γὰρ αὐτοῦ θυμὸν ᾠακοστρόφουν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 767; ἡ γλῶσσ᾽ ὀμώμοχ᾽, ἡ δὲ φ. ἀνώμοτος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 612; κατὰ φρένα καὶ κατὰ θυμόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.193, +others}}: _plural_, **wits,** Κύκλωπα περὶ φρένας ἤλυθε οἶνος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.362, compare 454, 18.331; πλήγη φρένας ἂς πάρος εἶχεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.394; ἐκ γὰρ πλήγη φρένας 16.403; βλάπτε φρένας Ζεὺς ἡμετέρας 15.724; ἐξ... τοι θεοὶ φρένας ὤλεσαν 7.360; φρένας ἄφρων, φρένας ἠλέ or ἠλεέ, 4.104, 15.128, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.243}}: of losing one's **wits,** φρενῶν ἀφεστάναι, ἐκστῆναι, μεθεστάναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 865, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1021, “Ba.” 944; τὰς φ. ἐκβάλλειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 648; ἔξω φρενῶν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.47; φρενῶν οὐκ ἔνδον ὤν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 709; φρενῶν κεκομμένος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 479 (Lyric poetry); κενός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 754; τητώμενοι [prev. author] “El.” 1326}}; ἔξεδροι, παράκοποι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 935, “Ba.” 33; ποῦ ποτ᾽ εἶ φρενῶ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 390; φρένες διάστροφοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 673, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 447; μαργότης φρενῶν [prev. author] “fragment” 846; ἀνακίνησις φρενῶν [prev. author] “OT” 727, etc.}}; of persons in their senses, ἐπήβολος φρενῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 492; ἀνδρὸς νοῦν ἔχοντος καὶ φρένας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 535 (Lyric poetry)}} (so in later Prose, οἱ φρένας ἔχοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.19, “Rh.” 1.240S.; οἱ τῶν σοφιστῶν τὰς κοινὰς φ. ἔχοντες [prev. work]202S.); also ἔσω φρενῶν λέγειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1052; γράφου φρενῶν ἔσω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1325; τῆς λεπτότητος τῶν φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 153}}; φρένες, opposed to σῶμα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134; so αἱ σάρκες αἱ κεναὶ φρενῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 387}}; attributed to animals, μετὰ φρεσὶ γίγνεται ἀλκή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.245, compare 16.157, etc.}}—The word is not common in early Prose, τίς αὐτῶν νόος ἢ φρή; {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 104}}; συμφορὰ τῶν φ., i.e. madness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.7; παραλλάττει τῶν φ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 90; καρποῦ μὲν ἀφθονία φρενῶν δὲ ἀφορία 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.55; νοῦς καὶ φρένες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.324, compare 25.33.}}
**I.4** **will, purpose,** οὔ τι Διὸς βέομαι φρεσίν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.194; σῆς ἀπεστάτουν φ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 993, compare “OC” 1182}}.—In usage there is little or no distinction observable between _singular_ and _plural_, but the _singular_ is not found in Prose (except {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus [same place]) or Comedy texts (except in _paratragoedia_, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 886).}}

5425:
** φρίσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **φρίττω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 387c}}: _future_ φρίξω {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.365}}: _aorist_ ἔφριξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.339, etc.}}: _perfect_ πέφρῑκα {{Refs|11.383, etc.}}; _poetry_ _participle_ πεφρίκοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.183}}: _pluperfect_ ἐπεφρίκει {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.781e, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.1:}} —_middle_, _aorist 1_ ἐφριξάμην _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 4.6.7. [ῑ by nature, hence to be accented φρῖσσον in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 171, φρῖξαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1408 (Lyric poetry)]}}:—**to be rough** or **uneven on the surface, bristle,** φρίσσουσιν ἄρουραι (i.e. σταχύεσσι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.599; φρίξας κάρπιμος στάχυς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 31}}; of a line of battle, ἔφριξεν μάχη ἐγχείῃσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.339; φάλαγγες σάκεσίν τε καὶ ἔγχεσι πεφρικυῖαι 4.282, compare 7.62}}; φρίξας εὐλόφῳ σφηκώματι, of the crest of a helmet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 341}}; of a tree, φρίσσουσα ζεφύροις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eleg.” 25; φιάλα χρυσῷ πεφρικυῖα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).40}}; χερσὶ δεξιωνύμοις ἔφριξεν αἰθήρ, of a crowd holding up their hands to vote, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 608}}; of hair, mane, or bristles, **bristle up, stand on end,** μηδ᾽ ὀρθαὶ φρίσσωσιν [τρίχες] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 540, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 560b8, “Pr.” 888a38; ἔφριξαν ἔθειραι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.244}}; of foliage, φύλλα πεφρικότα, opposed to κεκλιμένα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.9.4: with _accusative_ of respect, φρίξας εὖ λοφιήν}} **having set up his bristly** mane, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.446; φ. τρίχας 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 391}}; φ. νῶτον, αὐχένας, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.473, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 171; χαίταν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 822 (Lyric poetry)}}; also πτεροῖσι νῶτα πεφρίκοντες **bristling on** their backs with feathers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.183; λέοντος δέρος χαίτῃ πεφρικός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1121.}}
**2** ἄσθματι φρίσσων πνοάς **ruckling** in his throat, of one just dying, uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.74.}}
**3** of the **rippling** surface of smooth water {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare (φρίξ 1), φ. θάλασσαι.. πνοιῇσι 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 112, compare 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.1}}; of breakers, ῥηγμῖνες φ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1575, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.33}}; also of rain, φρίσσοντες ὄμβροι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.81, explained by Scholia as φρίσσειν ποιοῦντες,compare ὁπόταν.. φρίσσων Βορέας ἐπισπέρχῃ [prev. author] “Parth.” 2.18.}}
**II** frequently of a feeling of **chill, shiver, shudder**:
**II.1** of the effect of cold, **shiver,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 512, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ παθῶν” 11, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 963a33, 965a33}}; χωρὶς τοῦ φρῖξαι unless he **catch a chill,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.803}}; of the teeth, **chatter,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 10.9.}}
**II.2** of the effect of fear, **shudder,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1408 (Lyric poetry), “Tr.” 1044; πέφρικ᾽ ἐγὼ μέν, αὖός εἰμι τῷ δέει 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 480; φ. γαῖα πόντος τε “Hymni Homerici” 27.8}}; ἅλω δὲ πολλὴν.. ἔφριζα δινήσαντος I **shuddered** when he swung the vast shield round, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 490}}; οὐ φρίττουσιν (i.e. animals) ὡς φρίττουσιν οἱ ἄνθρωποι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.12}}: with _accusative_, **shudder at** one, οἵ τέ σε πεφρίκασι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.383; πάντες δέ με πεφρίκασιν 24.775, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.38, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 997, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1133; τῶν δημοτέων φ. τὸν ἥκιστον 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.30; τοὺς τελώνας πᾶσα νῦν θύρη φρίσσει [prev. author] 6.64; πεφρικέναι τὸν θάνατον 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 39; φρίττουσι τὴν σύντροφόν τε καὶ φίλην οἱ ἰχθύες θάλατταν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 9.57: with _accusative_ et}}_infinitive_, πέφρικα.. Ἐρινὺν τελέσαι I **tremble** at the thought of her accomplishing.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 720 (Lyric poetry) (but not with _dative_, for ἐρετμοῖσι φρίξουσι}} they **shall shudder** at the oars is _falsa lectio_ for{φρύξουσι} in Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.96): with}}_participle_, πέφρικα λεύσσων I **shudder at** seeing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 346; φ. σε δερκομένα [prev. author] “Pr.” 540 (Lyric poetry), compare 695 (Lyric poetry)}}: with _infinitive_, **fear to** do, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.135: with _preposition_, φ. πρὸς τοὺς πόνους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.8f; φ. πρὸς τὴν ἀκοὴν τῆς Ῥωμαίων τέχνης 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 24.16; φ. ὑπὲρ ὧν προσήκει παθεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 51.9.}}
**II.3** **feel a holy thrill** or **awe at,** ἐν ἱερῷ φ. ἅπαντα καὶ προσκυνεῖν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.26b; τοὺς θεοὺς πέφρικα 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.212b, +others}}
**II.4** **thrill** with passionate joy, ἔφριξ᾽ ἔρωτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 693, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 387}}.—Rare in early Prose, except in the sense of **shuddering, fearing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 387c, “Phdr.” 251a, D.[same places]}}

5426:
** φρον-έω**,
_Epic dialect_ _subjunctive_ φρονέῃσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.75}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ φρόνεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.286, φρονέεσκον 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1164}}: _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 630 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.: _aorist_ ἐφρόνησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 115, etc.}}: _perfect_ πεφρόνηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 103.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.124, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.66}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Xenophane” 980a9}}; _imperative_ φρονείσθω variant for{φρονεῖτε} in {{NT|NT.Phil.2.5}}:—**to be minded,** either of reflection or of purpose: hence,
**I** **have understanding, be wise, prudent,** rare in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἄριστοι.. μάχεσθαί τε φρονέειν τε}} best both in battle and **counsel,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.79}}: but frequently in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, [Ζῆνα] τὸν φρονεῖν βροτοὺς ὁδώσαντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 176 (Lyric poetry); φρονούντως πρὸς φρονοῦντας ἐννέπεις [prev. author] “Supp.” 204, compare 176; φρονεῖν γὰρ οἱ ταχεῖς οὐκ ἀσφαλεῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 617; φρονεῖν οἶδεν μόνη [prev. author] “Tr.” 313}}; λίαν φ. **to be** over-**wise,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 924; φ. πλέον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 371a; τὸ φρονεῖν}}**understanding, prudence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1347 (_anapaest meter_), 1353 (_anapaest meter_)}}; κράτιστοι φρονεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.1; καὶ φ. καὶ συμπράττειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.44; εἰδέναι καὶ φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.133c; τὸ φ. καὶ τὸ νοεῖν [prev. author] “Phlb.” 11b; λέγειν τε καὶ φ. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 266b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.50; τῷ φρονεῖν τε καὶ σωφρονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 712a}}; τὸ μὴ φρονοῦν, of an infant, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 753; ἐπειδὴ τάχιστα ἤρχετο φ. 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.20; ἡ φρονοῦσα ἡλικία 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.139: Comedy texts of fish, ἰχθῦς φρονοῦντας}}**full-grown,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 21.3; ζῷον λογικὸν καὶ φρονοῦν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 15: with _accusative_, φρονῆσαι τὰ κυριώτατα}} **to be wise** in respect of the most important matters, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 2.35S.}}
**I.2** with _adverbs_, εὖ φρονεῖν **think** rightly, περί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.16}}; **to be sane** (compare infr. IV), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 851, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 817, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.41, etc.; κέρδιστον εὖ φρονοῦντα μὴ φρονεῖν δοκεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 387; οἱ φρονοῦντες εὖ κρατοῦσι πανταχοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1252, compare “El.” 394, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 99, +others (but εὖ φρ.}} also, **to be** well **disposed,** see below {{Refs|11.2)}}; κακῶς, καλῶς φ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.168, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 600, “Ant.” 557; ὀρθῶς φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.23; ὀρθῶς φ. πρός τι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1000}}; μῶρα, πλάγια φ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 594, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 332 (troch.).}}
**I.3** **think,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 113, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 16.3, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 108.2, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}**de.An.**427a19; ὡς.., ὅτι.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 49, “OC” 872; φρόνει νιν ὡς ἥξοντα [prev. author] “Tr.” 289}}; **mean,** ἄλλα φ. καὶ ἄλλα λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.54; ἕτερα μὲν λέγων, ἕτερα δὲ φρονῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.47; ὁ μὴ λέγων ἃ φρονεῖ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.282, compare 19.224.}}
**I.4** **feel by experience, know full well,** σοὶ μὲν δοκεῖν ταῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽, ἐμοὶ δ᾽ ἄγαν φρονεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 942, compare “OC” 1741 (Lyric poetry)}}; πειρώμενος ὅ τι φρονέοιεν [τὰ μαντήϊα] to test the **knowledge** of the oracles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.46.}}
**II** **to be fain** that.., with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.98}}: with _infinitive_, **to be minded** to do, {{Refs|9.608, 17.286}}; without _infinitive_, οἱ δ᾽ ἰθὺς φρόνεον [ἰέναι] **were minded to go** right on ward, {{Refs|13.135, compare 12.124}}; ᾗπερ δὴ φρονέω [τελέσαι{{Refs|] 9.310; φρονῶν ἔπρασσον}}**of set purpose,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 271}}: in Prose, **mean, intend,** τοῦτο φρονεῖ ἡμῶν ἡ.. ἀγωγή this is what your bringing us here **means,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.85.}}
**II.2** frequently with _neuter_ _adjective_,
**II.2.a** φ. τινί τινα **to have** certain **thoughts for** or **towards** any one, **to be** so and so **minded towards** him, πατρὶ φίλα φρονέων kindly **minded towards** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.219, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.313, etc.; κακὰ φρονέουσι.. ἀλλήλοισιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.264; τῷ ὀλοὰ φρονέων 16.701; μαλακὰ φ. ἐσλοῖς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.95; πιστά τινι [prev. author] “O.” 3.17; φρονοῦντας ἄριστα αὐτοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 577 (_anapaest meter_): with _adverbs_, εὖ φρονεῖν τισι (compare above 1.2) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.74, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1436, etc.}}; φρονεῖς εὖ τοῖς ἠγγελμένοις you **rejoice at** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ch.” 774; also εἰς ὑμᾶς εὖ φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.4; τισὶ καλῶς φ. “SIG” 527.38 (Crete, 3rd c.BC)}}; τοιαῦτα περί τινος φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.60}}: **to be minded** so and so, **think** or **purpose** such and such things, ἀγαθὰ φ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.162, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.43}}; φίλα φ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 307; κακά 17.596; τὰ φρονέεις ἅ τ᾽ ἐγώ περ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.361}}; κρυπτάδια φ. **to have** secret **purposes,** {{Refs|1.542}}; ἀταλὰ φ. **to be** gaily **disposed,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|18.567, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 989}}; πυκινὰ φ. **have** wise **thoughts, be** cunningly **minded,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.445}}; ἐφημέρια φ. **think** only of the passing day,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|21.85; θεοῖσιν ἶσα φ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.441; θνητὰ φ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 590 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 799; ἀθάνατα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 90c; οὐ κατ᾽ ἄνθρωπον φ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 425, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 777; ἐπὶ ταῖς εὐτυχίαις ὑπὲρ ἄνθρωπον φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.3; μηδὲν ὑπὲρ τὴν πήραν φ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 57: also καίρια φ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 228 (Lyric poetry); σώφρονα [prev. author] “fragment” 64; οὐ τὰ ἄριστα φ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.22; ἡ πόλις χεῖρον φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.126}}; τυραννικὰ φ. **to have** tyranny **in mind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 507 (troch.)}}; ἀρχαιϊκὰ φ. **to have** old-fashioned **notions,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 821}}; τὰ τοῦ θεοῦ, τὰ τῆς σαρκὸς φ., {{NT|NT.Matt.16.23, NT.Rom.8.5}}; also οὐ παρδάλιος τόσσον μένος ὅσσον Πάνθου υἷες φρονέουσιν the panther's courage is not so great as **is the spirit** of the sons of Panthus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.23.}}
**II.2.b** especially frequently in the phrase μέγα φρονεῖν **to be** high-**minded, have** high **thoughts, to be** high-**spirited,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.296, 13.156}}; of lions and boars, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|16.758, 11.325, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.62; φρονεῖ γὰρ ὡς γυνὴ μέγα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1078, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.132}}; in _Attic dialect_, frequently in bad sense, **to be presumptuous,** ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῷ, ἑαυτοῖς μέγα φ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.27 (also μεγάλα φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 988}}; φ. ἐφ᾽ αὑτῷ τηλικοῦτον ἡλίκον εἰκός.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.62): with _comparative_, μεῖζον φ.}} **to have** over-high **thoughts,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.8}} (but simply, **pluck up courage,** ἐπὶ τῷ γεγενημένῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HG” 3.5.21); φ. μεῖζον ἢ κατ᾽ ἄνδρα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 768; μεῖζον τοῦ δέοντος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.7, compare 6.34}}: rarely in _plural_, μείζω τῆς δίκης φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 933}}; with _superlative_, οἱ μέγιστον φρονοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 257e; ἐφ᾽ ἱππικῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.5; also μάλιστα φ. ἐπί τινι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.2; ἐπὶ τοῖς προγόνοις οὐ μεῖον φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 7.3, compare “Ap.” 24}}; **take pride** in, ἐπὶ παιδεύσει μέγα φρονοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 342d; φ. ἐπὶ τῇ ὥρᾳ θαυμάσιον ὅσον [prev. author] “Smp.” 217a; also φ. εἰς ἡμᾶς μέγα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 6; περὶ τὸ γράφειν λόγους 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.125}}; μέγα φ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.13; μέγα φ. ὡς εὖ ἐρῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 198d}}; μέγα φ. μὴ ὑπείξειν **haughty in their resolution** not to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.45: later φ.}} alone, ={μέγα φ., φρονήσας ἐφ᾽ αὑτῷ} {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.12.5; διὰ τὸν πατέρα ἀξιώματι προέχοντα [prev. author] 4.1.2: opposed to σμικρὸν φ.}} **to be** poor-**spirited,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1120; μικρὸν φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.151; μικρὸν καὶ ταπεινὸν φ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.25, etc.}}; ἧσσον, ἔλασσον φ. τινος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 313, “Ph.” 1128; φ. ἔλαττον ἢ πρότερον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.47, etc.; οὐ σμικρὸν φ. ἐς τὰς Ἀθήνας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 386: also μετριώτερον πρὸς ἡμᾶς φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.7.}}
**II.2.c** of those who agree in **opinion,** τά τινος φρονεῖν **to be of** another's **mind, be on** his **side** or **of** his **party,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.162, etc.; τὰ σὰ φ. [prev. author] 7.102; εὖ φ. τὰ σά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 491; φ. τὰ Βρασίδον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 640 (troch.), compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.161}}; also ἶσον ἐμοὶ φρονέουσα **thinking** like me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.50, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 374 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὠυτὸ or κατὰ τὠυτὸ φ. **to be** like -**minded,** make common cuase, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60, 5.3; ἐμοὶ φ. ξυνῳδά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 635 (Lyric poetry): opposed to ἀμφὶς φ.}} **think** differently, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.345}}; ἄλλῃ φ. **think** another way, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 469.}}
**III** **comprehend,** γιγνώσκω, φρονέω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.136, +others}}: more frequently with _accusative_, **to be well aware of**.., τὰ φρονέουσ᾽ ἀνὰ θυμόν, ἃ.. {{Refs|2.116}}; οὐκ ὄπιδα φρονέοντες ἐνὶ φρεσίν **paying** no **heed to** it, {{Refs|14.82}}; φ. τὴν ἡμέραν **pay regard to** it, {{NT|NT.Rom.14.6}}; **consider, ponder,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.36, 18.4, +others}}
**IV** **to be in possession of one's senses,** sometimes almost ={ζῆν}, **to be sensible, be alive,** ἐμὲ τὸν δύστηνον ἔτι φρονέοντ᾽ ἐλέησον, for ἔτι ζῶντα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.59; θανόντι δ᾽, οὐ φρονοῦντι, δειλαία χάρις ἐπέμπετο 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 517; ἐν τῷ φ. γὰρ μηδὲν ἥδιστος βίος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 554; μηδὲ ζῆν.., μηδὲ φρονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 249a}}; but also, **to be in one's senses** or **right wits,** φρονοῦντα, opposed to μεμηνότα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 82, compare 344; ἔξω ἐλαύνειν τινὰ τοῦ φρονεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 853; φρονεῖς ὀρθὰ κοὐ μαίνῃ [prev. author] “Med.” 1129; ἐξεστηκὼς τοῦ φρονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.18; τὰ φαλάγγια τοῦ φ. ἐξίστησι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.12; οὐκ ἂν παρείμην οἷσι μὴ δοκῶ φρονεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1666; ἐγὼ νῦν φρονῶ τότ᾽ οὐ φρονῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1329}}; φρονῶν οὐδὲν φρονεῖς **though in thy wits thou'rt** nothing **wise,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ba.” 332 (for εὖ φ. see above 1.2); ὁρώντων, φρονούντων, βλεπόντων ὑμῶν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.94: ζῶν καὶ φρονῶν}} alive and **in his right mind,** frequently in Inscrr., {{Refs|“IGRom.” 1.804 (from Perinthus)}}, etc.; ζῶν καὶ φρενῶν (sic) {{Refs|“Jahresh.” 23 “Beibl.” 206 ([prev. passage]), “Rev.Arch.” 21 (1925).240 (from Callatis)}}; νοῶν καὶ φρονῶν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Test.Epict.” 1.2, “PPetr.” 3p.4 (3rd c.BC).}}

5427:
** φρόν-ημα**, ατος, τό,
**mind, spirit,** ἔστ᾽ ἂν Διὸς φ. λωφήσῃ χόλου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 378; Αἰσχύλου φ. ἔχων 5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 14}}; with limiting epithets, φ. δύσθεον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 191; ὑπέρτολμον [prev. work] 595 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὠμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 537; ἐλεύθερον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 865d; τυραννικόν [prev. author] “R.” 573b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 15.8: _plural_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.54.}}
**2** **thought, purpose, will,** φθέγμα καὶ ἀνεμόεν φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 354 (Lyric poetry); ψυχὴ καὶ φ. καὶ γνώμη [prev. work] 176, compare 207}}; τὸ φ. τῆς σαρκός, τοῦ πνεύματος, {{NT|NT.Rom.8.6: frequently in _plural_, καρτεροῖς φρονήμασι}} with stubborn **thoughts,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 209; ματαίων.. φρονημάτων ἡ γλῶσσ᾽ ἀληθὴς γίγνεται κατήγορος [prev. author] “Th.” 438; ἐμπέδοις φ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 169}}; τὰ σκλήρ᾽ ἄγαν φ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 473; τὰ φ. ἀληθινὰ καὶ πάντῃ μεγάλα ἐκέκτηντο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 120e.}}
**II** either in good or bad sense,
**II.1** **high spirit, resolution, pride,** τὸ Ἀθηναίων φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.144, compare 9.7}}.; ἀνδρί γε φ. ἔχοντι to a man of **spirit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.43; φ. τε καὶ πίστις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1313b2; φ. ἔχων ἐλεύθερον [prev. work] 1314a3}}; **courage,** opposed to δειλία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.59c (_plural_); δουλοῖ τὸ φ. τὸ αἰφνίδιον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.61: with}}_future_ _infinitive_, ἐν φρονήματι ὄντες τῆς Πελοποννήσου ἡγήσεσθαι **aspiring** to be leaders of the P., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.40}}: frequently in _plural_, **high thoughts, proud designs,** διασείσειν τὰ Ἀθηναίων φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, compare 3.122, 125, 9.54; οὐ.. ξυμφέρει τοῖς ἄρχουσι φ. μεγάλα ἐγγίγνεσθαι τῶν ἀρχομένων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 182c, compare 190b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.89; Ζεύς τοι κολαστὴς τῶν ὑπερκόμπων ἄγαν φ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 828; τῶν φ. ὁ Ζεὺς κολαστὴς τῶν ἄγαν ὑπερφρόνων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 387.}}
**II.2** in bad sense, **presumption, arrogance,** φρονήματος πλέως ὁ μῦθός ἐστιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 953, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 926 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1024 (_anapaest meter_), “Pax” 25, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 290d, etc.; τὸ τῶν Ἀτρειδῶν φ. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.217 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, etc.: _plural_, παυσάμενοι τῶν φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.37; φ. τυραννικά 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Eumenes” 13.}}
**III** _plural_, ={φρένες}, **heart, breast,** ἰὸς ἐκ φρονημάτων.. πεσών {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 478.}}

5428:
** φρόν-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**purpose, intention,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 664 (Lyric poetry); φρόνησιν λαβεῖν λῴω ἡμῖν [prev. author] “Ph.” 1078.}}
**2** **thought,** ἰδία φ., opposed to λόγος ξυνός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 2; φ. ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 110.10, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1009b18.}}
**3** **sense,** εἴ τις ἄρα τοῖς ἐκεῖ φ. περὶ τῶν ἐνθάδε γιγνομένων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.61.}}
**4** **judgment,** κατὰ τὴν ἰδίαν φ. οὐδεὶς εὐτυχεῖ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 306.}}
**5** **arrogance, pride,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 216}}; also in good sense, τὸ φῦναι πατρὸς εὐγενοῦς ἄπο ὅσην ἔχει φρόνησιν **just pride,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 739.}}
**II** **practical wisdom, prudence in government and affairs,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 209a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1140a24, 1141b23, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.204,217, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.97e, etc.; φιλοσοφίας τιμιώτερον ὑπάρχει φ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.64U.: opposed to ἀμαθία, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 202a}}; opposed to σῶμα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 461a}}; opposed to ῥώμη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.6; φρόνησιν ἀσκεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.40, compare 15.209: _plural_, ἡδοναὶ καὶ φρονήσεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 63a; ἡλικίαι καὶ φ. [prev. author] “Lg.” 665d}}; also attributed to **sagacious** animals, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 753a12, “HA” 608a15.}}
**III** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **three,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 14.}}

5429:
** φρόνιμ-ος**, ον,
also η, ον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1070b}}:— **in one's right mind, in one's senses,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 259 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II** **showing presence of mind,** ἐν τοῖς δεινοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.7; τὸ φ.}}**presence of mind,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 2.3.56.}}
**III** **sensible, prudent,** opposed to ἄφρων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 6; ψυχή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 247a}}; opposed to ἀνόητος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.14 (_comparative_)}}; τὸν φρόνιμον ζητοῦντας.. ὥσπερ ἀποδεδρακότα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 2.3; ὡς ἂν ὁ φ. ὁρίσειε 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1107a1, +others}}; φ. περί τινος **possessing sagacity** or **discernment** in a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.15, 21 (_comparative_)}}; περί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 490b (_comparative_), 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.161 (_comparative_)}}; εἴς τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.125a; ἐν τῷ σίτῳ φ. καὶ μέτριοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.17.}}
**III.2** of thoughts, acts, and the like, φ. τι ἐργάσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 42; φιλόπολις ἀρετή, φρόνιμος [prev. work] 547 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III.3** of birds as giving omens, τοὺς ἄνωθεν φρονιμωτάτους οἰωνούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1058 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III.3.b** **sagacious,** of animals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 263d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 488b15, “PA” 648a8 (_comparative_), 687a8 (_superlative_), “GA” 753a11 (_comparative_), +others}}
**III.4** τὸ φ. **practical wisdom, prudence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 52.9 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 586d, +others}}; opposed to τὸ ἄφρον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 235e; ἰέναι ἐπὶ τὸ φρονιμώτερον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.14: _plural_, ἄπορος ἐπὶ φρόνιμα}} helpless in point of **wisdom,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 692 (Lyric poetry); τὰ -ώτερα ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.211.}}
**III.5** _adverb_ **-μως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1364, “Av.” 1333 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 192e, etc.}}; opposed to ἀλόγως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.9; φ. ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.57; διακεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.114}}: _comparative_ φρονιμώτερον, διακεῖσθαι τῶν ἄλλων {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.10}}; **-ωτέρως** {{Refs|[prev. author] 13.15}}: _superlative_ -ώτατα, λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 20.}}

