5431:
** φροντ-ίζω**,
_future_ _Attic dialect_ ιῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1234, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 125, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.24, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐφρόντισα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 245 (troch.)}}, etc.: _perfect_ πεφρόντικα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 352, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 263, etc.:}} —_middle_, _future_ φροντιοῦμαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 343 (assuming variant)}}:— _passive_, see infr. IV: (φροντίς):
**I** _absolutely_, **consider, reflect, take thought,** φροντίζων εὑρίσκω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.24, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1034, “Supp.” 418 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 75, +others; ζητεῖν καὶ φ. καὶ βουλεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.41}}; **give heed, pay attention,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 558a.}}
**I.2** **to be thoughtful** or **anxious,** πεφροντικὸς βλέπεις you look **careworn,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773; τίς δ᾽ ἔστιν ὁ.. φροντίζω; 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista}} Comedy texts 21; τὸ πεφροντικός, as _substantive_, **care, thought,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.983b.}}
**II** with an object,
**II.1** with _accusative_ _of things_, **consider, ponder,** ἔχθος ἐμόν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1247; Σωκράτης.. -ίζων τι ἕστηκε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c; δεινά.. τοῖς τεκοῦσι φροντίσαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 245 (troch.)}}; [ὀνείρατα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16}}.; **devise,** μηχανήν {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.67; τοῦτο φ. ὅκως μὴ λείψομαι [prev. author] 7.8.ά}} (nisi to be read τοῦτον); ἐκεῖνο δ᾽ οὐ πεφροντίκαμεν, ὅτῳ τρόπῳ.. μνημονεύσομεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 263}}; also directly followed by a _relative_ clause, φ. ἥντιν᾽ [ὁδὸν] ἴω προτέρην {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 912; φ. πρὸς ἑωυτὸν ὡς δώσει 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.100; ἐφρόντισ᾽ ᾗ διέφθαρται βίος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 376; φ. ὅ τι βούλεται ἑαυτὸν καλεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.7.6}}; φ. τί ποτε τοῦτ᾽ ἔστι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.3.32}}; **take thought** that.., **see to it** that, ὅπως.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 29e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.8, “PHib.” 1.82.10 (3rd c.BC); later ἵνα.. [prev. work] 43.7 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.8.8}}; ὡς.. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 10.6, +others (2nd c.BC)}}: with _infinitive_, {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Tit.3.8, “BGU” 8 ii 4 (3rd c.AD), etc.: followed by μή}} with _subjunctive_, φ. μὴ ἄριστον ᾖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.155, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.39, compare below 111}}; οὐδὲν φ. εἰ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 503a}}; φ. εἴτε.., εἴτε.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 344e}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 12}}: with _participle_, φροντίζεθ᾽ ὡς μαχούμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1370.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_, **take thought for, give heed to** a thing, **regard** it, mostly with a _negative_ expressed or implied, σοὶ δ᾽ ἔμεθεν μὲν ἀπήχθετο φροντίσδην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 41 (assuming variant); Περσέων οὐδὲν φ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.97, compare 100, 151, 4.167; γῆ αὐχμοῦ φροντίζουσα οὐδέν [prev. author] 4.198; Πενθέως οὐ φροντίσας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 637 (troch.); μηδὲν ὅρκου φροντίσῃς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 915; τῶν οἰκετῶν.. μηδὲν φ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.17}}; μησενὸς ἄλλου φ., πλὴν ὅπως.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.305; οὐδὲ τῶν νόμων φροντίζουσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 563d; τὸ παράπαν θεῶν μὴ φ. [prev. author] “Lg.” 701c}}; conversely, τοὺς θεοὺς φ. οὐδὲν τῶν ἀνθρωπίνων{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 888c, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 552}}: so with _adverbs_ implying a _negative_, σμικρὰ φ. ὄχλου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 801 (troch.); ὀλίγον φ. δεσποτῶν [prev. author] “Cyc.” 163; σμικρὸν φ. Σωκράτους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91c}}; also without _negative_, οὗπερ δεῖ μάλιστα φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 242; τοῦ μὲν ὀνόματος φ., τοῦ δὲ πράγματος ἀμελεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.27; σφόδρα σοῦ φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.10; εἰ σοφὸς ἀνὴρ ταφῆς φροντιεῖ 2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1012.26}}; later, **to be steward** or **bailiff,** τῶν ὑπαρχόντων τινός {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 300.4 (2nd c.AD): so with Preps., φ. περί τινος}} **to be concerned** or **anxious** about, σφέων αὐτῶν πέρι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.36, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.12, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 709; ὑπέρ τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4d; ὑπὲρ σωτηρίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.2; less frequently in this sense with _accusative_ only, [Σωκράτης] τἄλλα μὲν πεφρόντικεν 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 352; ἄλλο οὐδὲν φροντίζειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 501e; ἀλλ᾽ οὐ τὰ βίου.. δεῖ φροντίσαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 330; ἡ δ᾽ ἐφρόντισ᾽ οὐδὲ ἕν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 302 (troch.).}}
**II.2.b** later, **see to, provide for, furnish,** σιτάριον, χρήσιμα, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“ZUP” 73.5, 69.2 (both 2nd c.BC)}}; τὰ ἐπιβάλλοντα τῇ ἑορτῇ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 845.18 (2nd c.AD)}}; μηδὲν φροντίσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 80.}}
**II.2.c** with _accusative_ et _participle_, οὐ φροντίζει σκληρῶς σε καθήμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 783 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.3** the object is frequently unexpressed, ἐφρόντιζε ἱστορέων, i. e. inquired carefully, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.56}}; οἲ τοὺς φίλους βλάπτοντες οὐ φροντίζετε who though ye do mischief to your friends **reck** not **of** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 256}}; μὴ φροντίσῃς **heed** it not {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 228; οὐ, μὰ Δἴ, οὐδ᾽ ἐφρόντισα [prev. author] “Ra.” 493, compare 650, “Pl.” 215, 704}}; with _participle_, τοιαῦτα.. γιγνόμενα.. ὁρῶντες οὐδὲν φροντίζετε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.23.}}
**III** _middle_ in _signification_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.1, φροντιζόμενον μή.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.10: with _accusative_, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 343 (assuming variant).}}
**IV** later in _passive_, **to be an object of thought** or **care,** πεφροντισμένος **carefully thought out;** λόγος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.78, 16.32; λόγοι πεφρ. εὖ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.11; τρέφονται τροφῇ πεφροντισμένῃ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.9}}; of a ward, ἡ-ομένη ὑπ᾽ ἐμοῦ θυγατριδῆ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 300.16 (2nd c.AD).}}

5432:
** φρουρ-έω**,
poetical _imperfect_ φρούρουν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 915}}: _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 31, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐφρούρησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.30, etc.:}} —_middle_, _future_ -ήσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 603:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ ἐφρουρήθην{{Refs|[prev. work] 1390}}: _perfect_ πεφρούρημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 23 (2nd c.AD: Pseudo-Democritus Alchemista), (δια-) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 265}}: (φρουρός):—**keep watch** or **guard,** ἐν Ἐλεφαντίνῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same place], compare 9.106, “IG” 12.99.21, “OGI” 38.1 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; of ships, φ. περὶ Ναύπακτον or ἐν Ναυπάκτῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.80, 83}}; φ. ἐπί τινι **to keep watch** over.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 35 (_anapaest meter_)}}; οἱ φρουροῦντες the **guardians,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 763d; συνάπτειν.. φρουροῦντας.. φρουροῦσι [prev. work] 758b}}; proverbial, ἐν παντὶ.. σκορπίος φ. λίθῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 37}}; generally, 'keep a sharp look-out', {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 915.}}
**II** _transitive_, **watch, guard,** τὴν χώρην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.90; τὴν γέφυραν [prev. author] 4.133; τὴν ἀτραπόν [prev. author] 7.217; βρέτας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 1024; σὲ δαίμων.. φρουρήσας τύχοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1479}}; τὴν Ποτείδαιαν φ. **garrison** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3. [17], compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.17, etc.}}; φυλακαῖσι φ. σῶμ᾽ Ὀδυσσέως **set** a watch over.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 690: _metaphorically_, φ. πέτραν}} **keep watch over** it, of Prometheus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 31}}; στόμα εὔφημον φρουρεῖν ἀγαθόν **keep** silence, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 98 (_anapaest meter_); ἡ εἰρήνη τοῦ θεοῦ.. φρουρήσει τὰς καρδίας ὑμῶν NT.Phil.4.7}}:—_passive_, **to be watched** or **guarded,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.203, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 218, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1013, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 995, etc.}}: of the watch kept by besiegers, κύκλῳ φρουρούμενος ὑπὸ πάντων πολεμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 579b.}}
**II.b** **hold in subjection,** opposed to παραφυλάττειν, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.4.6.}}
**II.c** Astrology texts, **occupy,** in _passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 106.18.}}
**II.d** **bind,** ἁλύσεσι πεφρουρημένος {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.3093.}}
**II.2** **watch for, observe,** φρουρῶν τόδ᾽ ἦμαρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 27; φ. ὄμμα ἐπὶ σῷ.. καιρῷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 151 (Lyric poetry)}}; φ. χρέος **to be observant of** one's duty, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 74.}}
**III** _middle_, **to be on one's guard against, beware of,** with _accusative_, φρουρούμενος βέλεμνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1135}}:—_active_ also in the same sense, ἐφρούρει μηδὲν ἐξαμαρτάνειν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 900}}; φ. ὅπως or ὅπως ἂν.., with _subjunctive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1402, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 742}}; φ. μή.., with _subjunctive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 1139.}}

5433:
** φρυ-άσσομαι**,
_Attic dialect_ **φρυάττομαι**, properly of spirited, high-fed horses, **neigh, whinny and prance,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Lavacrum Palladis” 2, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.201 (3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus or 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus), compare 6th c.AD: Thomas Epigrammaticus Mag. p.381R.}}; φ. πρὸς τοὺς ἀγῶνας **neigh eagerly** for the race, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 22}}; also of a cock, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.7, compare below 11.}}
**2** _metaphorically_ of men, **to be wanton, haughty, insolent,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.151, 297, +others, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.27; μὴ γαῦρα φρυάσσου “Anthologia Graeca” 12.33 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.180: also with _accusative_, Ἔρωτες σκῦλα φρυασσόμενοι “APl.” 4.215 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}; φ. ἐπί τινι **to be proud** of a thing, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.74; ἔν τισι “Anthologia Graeca” 4.3.27 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**3** _transitive_, ={καταπλήττω}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 1081.}}
**II** _active_ φρυάσσω {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.2.1 (quoted in NT.Act.4.25)}}; of birds, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cyranus 81, compare “Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}

5434:
** φρύγᾰνον** [ῡ], τό,
(φρύγω) **dry stick**; mostly in _plural_, **firewood,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.62, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 642, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.111, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.11, “SIG” 1027.13 (Cos, 4th-3rd c.BC), NT.Act.28.3; φρυγάνοις καὶ λίθοις περιφράξαντες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 603a9: Comedy texts, Φρύγες ἐστὶ καινὸν δρᾶμα τοῦτ᾽ Εὐριπίδου.. ᾧ καὶ Σωκράτης τὰ φρύγαν᾽ ὑποτίθησι probably in 5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 40}}: _singular_ only in _collective_ sense ={τὰ φρύγανα}, μαντικῶς τὸ φ. τίθεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1026; τὸ φ. ἐπικαίουσι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.553c.}}
**II** **undershrub,** opposed to δένδρα, θάμνος, πόα, defined as τὸ ἀπὸ ῥίζης πολυστέλεχες καὶ πολύκλαδον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.3.1.}}

5437:
** φῠγή**, ἡ,
(φεύγω) **flight** in battle, ἀΐξαντε φυγῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.117; οὐδέ τις ἀλκὴ.. οὐδὲ φ. 22.306; ἐς φ. ἐτράποντο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.89}}; ἐς φ. ὁρμᾶσθαι, ὁρμᾶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 143, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.28}}; φυγὴν αἱρεῖσθαι, ἀρεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 481 (sed to be read αἴρονται), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 54; ἰσχυρὰν τὴν φ. τοῖς πολεμίοις.. ἐποίει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.22; ἰσχυρὰ φ. ἐγένετο [prev. work] 7.1.26}}; generally, **flight, **{{NT|NT.Matt.24.20}}; φ. ἑλέσθαι take **to flight,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PGnom. 102 (2nd c.AD): _dative_ φυγῇ}} used adverbially, **in hasty flight,** φυγᾷ πόδα νωμᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 468 (Lyric poetry); φυγᾷ ποδὶ ἴχνος ἔφερε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1468 (Lyric poetry); φ. ἐξαλύξωμεν ποδί [prev. author] “El.” 218, compare “Ba.” 437, “Hec.” 1066 (Lyric poetry)}}; φεύγειν φ., φ. ἀναχωρεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 195b, 221a; φ. φευκτέον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 16: _plural_, ἐν ταῖς φυγαῖς}} of the **flight** of the country people of Attica into the city, in the Pelop. war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 243; φεύγουσί τινας οὐκ αἰσχρὰς φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 706c.}}
**2** **flight** or **escape** from a thing, **avoidance of** it, with _genitive_, γάμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 395 (Lyric poetry); νόσων ἀμηχάνων φυγὰς ξυμπέφρασται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 364 (Lyric poetry), compare “OC” 280; λέκτρων φυγαί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 799; ἀγαθῶν φυγάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 69d; τῶν σιτίων 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.180}}; opposed to αἵρεσις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.62U., +others, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.87; τὰς ὀρθὰς αἱρέσεις καὶ φ. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.11}}; opposed to δίωξις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 25.}}
**3** **place of refuge,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.78.}}
**4** **slipping** of a bandage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 9.}}
**II** **banishment, exile,** νῦν μὲν δικάζεις ἐκ πόλεως φυγὴν ἐμοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1412, compare “Ch.” 254; ἐκ γῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 659, etc.; ἐνιαυσία ἔκδημος φ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 37; τῆς φυγῆς ἧς αὐτοὶ ἔφυγον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.74}}; συμφυγεῖν τὴν φ. ταύτην (i.e. ὑμῖν) to go into **banishment** with.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21a}}; φυγὴν ἐπιβαλὼν ἑωυτῷ imposing **banishment** on oneself {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.3; φυγῇ ζημιοῦν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 900, compare “IG” 12.39.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 516d; φυγὴν καταγνῶναί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.106, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.38}}; φυγῆς τιμήσασθαι (i.e. δίκην) the penalty of **exile,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 37c, compare “Cri.” 52c}}; ἐπὶ φόνῳ.. φ. Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.78 (uncertain reading); τῆς πατρίδος φ. ποιήσασθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.42}}; εὐθύνας.. εἶναι.. πλὴν φυγῆς καὶ θανάτου καὶ ἀτιμίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” [prev. cited] 73: _plural_, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1043, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325b, etc.; φυγὰς ἐμὰς χθονός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 400; φυγαὶ καὶ διώξεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 638a.}}
**II.2** as a _collective_ Noun, ={φυγάδες}, **body of exiles** or **refugees,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 74 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.64, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.143}}; κατάγειν τὴν φυγήν to recall **them,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.9; _plural_, συλλέξαι τὰς φυγάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 682e, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Flamininus” 12.}}

5438:
** φῠλᾰκ-ή**, ἡ,
(φύλαξ) **watching** or **guarding,** especially by night, φυλακῆς μνήσασθε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.371}}; φυλακὰς ἔχειν keep **watch and ward,** {{Refs|9.1, 471}}; φ. κατέχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 194 (Lyric poetry)}}; φυλακὴ ἔχει αὐτόν **watching** engages him, variant in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 188.4; φ. νυκτερινή 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 2: proverbial, γυμνῷ φυλακὴν ἐπιτάττειν}} tell an unarmed man to stand on the defensive, i.e. to give commands that cannot be obeyed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 144, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 12; περὶ φυλακῆς Εὐβοίας.. ἐπιμέλεσθαι “IG” 12.39.76}}; ὅπως ἀφανὴς εἴη ἡ φ. that there might be nothing visible **to watch,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.67; φυλακὴν [τῶν τειχῶν] ἔρημον καταλιπεῖν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 17}}; φυλακὰς φυλάξειν keep **watch and ward,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 758d; τὴν ἐν θαλάττῃ φ. φυλάττειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 7.14; φ. ποιῆσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.31; τὴν φ. ποιεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.16; φυλακὰς ποιήσασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.21; ἰσχυρὰς φ. ποιεῖσθαι [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.6.37}}; φυλακὰς καταστήσασθαι, κατασκευάσασθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 841, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.23.}}
**2** **watch** or **guard,** of persons, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321d (_plural_), NT.Act.12.10, etc.; φ. ἑωυτοῦ ποιεύμενός [τινας] 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.154}}; φ. τοῦ σώματος a body **guard,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.3; τῶν σωμάτων 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.9; φ. περὶ τὸ σῶμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.58, compare “PHib.” 1.59.5 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; **garrison** of a place or fortress, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.30}}; ἡ ἐν τῇ Ναυπάκτῳ φ., of a squadron of ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.22.}}
**2.b** **the rank of** φύλακες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 415c.}}
**3** **station, post,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.408 (_plural_), 416 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.49; φυλακὰς προλιπών 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 18 (_anapaest meter_)}}; Διὸς φ., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for the centre of the universe, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 293b3.}}
**4** of time, a **watch** of the night, ἐπεὰν τῆς νυκτὸς ἦ δευτέρη φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.51}}; πρώτης φ. ἀρχομένης Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 1 ii 18 (3rd c.BC)}}; φυλακαῖσι νυκτέροισιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 765; φ. νυκτερινὰς καὶ ἡμερινὰς καθιστάναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.43}}: of these there were **three,** _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 5}}; but **five** are mentioned in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Stesichorus Lyricus 55, 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 219 A, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 543 (Lyric poetry)}}; and the Roman division was **four,** {{NT|NT.Matt.14.25, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**5** **place for keeping** others in, **ward, prison,** δημοσία φ. {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 10.30; εἰς φυλακὴν βληθείς “Anthologia Graeca” 11.276 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus); βαλεῖν τινὰ εἰς φ. NT.Matt.18.30, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.1.24; θέσθαι τινὰ ἐν φυλακῇ LXX.Gen.40.3, cf. NT.Matt.14.3}}; πολιτικὴ φ. the town-**prison,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|POxy. 259.8 (1st c.AD).}}
**6** Astrology texts ={ταπείνωμα}, {{Refs|“PMich.” in “Class.Phil.” 22.22 (_plural_).}}
**II** **guarding, keeping, preserving,** whether for security or custody, ἐν φυλακῇ ἔχειν τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24; ἐν φ. ἀδέσμῳ ἔχειν τινά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.34; ἐν φυλακῇσι μεγάλῃσι ἔχεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.99}}; τὸν Ἰσθμὸν ἔχειν ἐν φ. to keep the Isthmus **guarded** or **occupied,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.207, compare 8.40}}; τὸν ἠνείκαντο γλώσσης χαρακτῆρα τοῦτον ἔχειν ἐν φ. **to preserve** the same character of language, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.57; ἔχειν νόον ἐν φ. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 439; τὰ παρὰ πᾶσιν ἐν πλείστῃ φ., παῖδας καὶ γυναῖκας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.215; τὰ κατὰ τὸ στρατόπεδον διὰ φυλακῆς ἔχων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.8; τὸν πλοῦν διὰ φ. ποιησάμενοι [prev. author] 8.39}}; στόματος φυλακᾷ κατασχεῖν φθόγγον probably in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 236 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐν φ. σχεθέμεν μεγάλᾳ **be** very **ware of,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.75}}; φυλακὴν ἔχειν, ={φυλάττεσθαι}, **keep guard, be on the watch,** περί τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.39; φ. ἔχων εἴ κως δυναίμην.. [prev. work] 38}}; φ. ἔχειν μή.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.69}}; φ. λαμβάνειν μή.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 20}}; δεινῶς ἦσαν ἐν φυλακῇσι were straitly on their **guard,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.152, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 592 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** **custody** of property, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309b6, “EN” 1120a9.}}
**II.3** **safeguard,** τὴν μεγίστην φ. ἀνῄρηκε τῆς πόλεως its chief **safeguard,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.19; φ. παρέχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.13}}; δημοκρατίας, μοναρχίας φ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.28, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1315a8.}}
**III** (from _middle_) **precaution,** πολλῆς φ. ἔργον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 537d; φ. θαυμαστῆς δεομένη [prev. author] “Lg.” 906a, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.99.}}
**III.2** with _genitive_, **precaution against,** εὐλάβεια φ. κακοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 413d; ὑποψίας φυλακὴν ποιήσασθαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.2; φ. τῶν πάντα μολυνόντων 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 80, compare 73.}}

5440:
** φῠλᾰκ-τήριον**, τό,
**guarded post, fort, castle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.52}}: especially **an outpost communicating with fortifications,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.31, 33, 110, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.12}}: _plural_, **guardrooms,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1331a20.}}
**2** **safeguard, security,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 917b}}: **preservative,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.24}}; **amulet,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.154, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.378b, etc.}}; among the Jews φυλακτήρια were small rolls of parchment with texts from the Law written on them, bound to the forehead by persons praying, {{NT|NT.Matt.23.5}}; φ. χρυσᾶ, **symbols** denoting the kingdoms of Upper and Lower Egypt, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.45 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}; **amulet,** {{Refs|PMag.Lond. 121.298 (_plural_); _metaphorically_, τὸ ὄνομά σου ἔχω ἓν φ. ἐν καρδίᾳ “PMag.Leid.W.” 18.2.}}
**3** perhaps **guard** or **chain, PLond.unpublished**2199.

5441:
** φῠλᾰκ-τήριος**, α, ον,
**serving as a protection,** τὰ περί τι φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 842d}}, **φῠλᾰκ-της**, ου, ὁ, **one who preserves,** τῶν ἰδίων ἐθῶν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.577 (_plural_), sed to be read -τικοί.}}
**II** ={φυλακτήρ}, a magistrate at Cumae, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.291f.}}

5442:
** φῠλάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **φυλάττω**, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ φυλασσέμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.312, 419}}; _poetry_ _imperfect_ φύλασσε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Pae.” 6.91}}: _future_ φυλάξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.195, etc.:}}_aorist_ ἐφύλαξα, _Epic dialect_ φύλ- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.686, etc.}}: _perfect_ πεφύλᾰχα {{LXX+5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.408f, (δια-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.3 (-πεφυλακ- codices), 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.9 (-πεφυλακ- codices), (παρα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 632a; πεφύλακα LXX.1Ki.25.21, 5th c.BC: Arg. Euripides Tragicus “Med.” (πεφυλακέναι and πεφυκέναι codices)}}:—_middle_, _future_ **-άξομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 205, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1012, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 831, etc.}}; also in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 4.9}}: _aorist_ ἐφυλαξάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.130 (variant), 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.7, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-αχθήσομαι**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 5.6, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.426}}: _aorist_ ἐφυλάχθην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 15}}: _perfect_ πεφύλαγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 472.19 (_anapaest meter_), compare below with 1, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 54.74 (in _passive_ sense)}}; _imperative_, only in _middle_ sense in early writers, πεφύλαξο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 797, Oracle texts cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.148}}; _participle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.343, etc.}}
**A** _absolutely_, **keep watch and ward, keep guard,** especially by night, ἀνίη καὶ τὸ φυλάσσειν πάννυχον ἐγρήσσοντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.52; οὐδ᾽ ἐθέλουσι νύκτα φυλασσέμεναι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.312, compare 419, 421}}; εἰ μέν κ᾽ ἐν ποταμῷ δυσκηδέα νύκτα φυλάσσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.466, compare 22.195}}; (_middle_, see below c); σὺν κυσὶ.. φυλάσσοντας περὶ μῆλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.303; αὐτοῦ φ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 243; τὴν μὲν ἡμέραν κατὰ διαδοχὴν φ. τὴν δὲ νύκτα καὶ ξύμπαντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.28; ἐφύλαττον περὶ τὰ βασίλεια 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.68; οἱ φυλάττοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.34}}; φ. τοῖς Ἀθηναίοις **keep watch** for.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.53; κατὰ θάλατταν ἐφύλαττεν ὅπως μηδὲν εἰσπλέοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.29}}; φ. ἕως.. **watch** or **wait** till.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.15}}; φ. πηνίκα.. {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.308: with _accusative_ _cognate_, φυλακὰς φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.10, NT.Luke.2.8.}}
**A.2** **to be on one's guard,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.135.}}
**B** _transitive_, **watch, guard, defend,** ἀθανάτων ὅστις σε φυλάσσει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.35, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.417, +others}}; σύας, μῆλα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.593, 12.136; τὴν ἑωυτῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.46; πόλιν φ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 136 (Lyric poetry), “IG” 12.108.46; πύλας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 950; σε φυλάττοι Ζεύς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 499 (_anapaest meter_); βρέτας ἧσαι φυλάσσων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 440}}; φ. τινὰ ἀπὸ τῶν δυσχωριῶν **guard** one from.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.7 (but τὴν γραῦν φ. ἀπὸ τῶν κεραμίων}} **keep away** from.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 87)}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, τοὐμὸν φυλάξει ὀ ὄνομα μὴ πάσχειν κακῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 667; ὁ νόμος φ. μὴ ἅπτεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 838b; φ. μηδένα περαιοῦσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.17; φ. τὸ μηδὲν ἐναντίον γενέσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.313}}: followed by a _relative_ clause, φ. ἑαυτὸν ὅπως μὴ ἀδικήσει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 480a; φύλαττέ με μὴ παρακρούσωμαί σε [prev. author] “Cra.” 393c}}; φ. τινά, εἰ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 220d}}:—_passive_, **to be watched, kept under guard,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.27.}}
**B.2** **watch for, lie in wait** or **ambush for,** αὐτὸν ἰόντα λοχήσομαι ἠδὲ φυλάξω ἐν πορθμῷ Ἰθάκης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.670; νόστον φ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.251}}; φ. τὸ σύμβολον **look out for** the signal-fire, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 8; τοὺς πολεμίους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 12.2; φ. τοὺς παράνομα γράφοντας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.34}}; **keep a watch on,** [τινα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.6; τοὺς παραβαίνοντας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1289a19.}}
**B.2.b** especially **watch, wait for, observe** an appointed time or a fixed event, τὴν κνρίην τῶν ἡμερέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.48; φ. τὴν ἡμέραν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.37}}; φυλάξαντες νύκτα **wait for** night, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.3; φυλάσσουσι γραφόμενοι τὸ ἀποβαῖνον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.82; τοὺς ἐτησίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.31}}: with a _participle_ added, δείλην ὀψίην γινομένην φυλάξαντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.9: φ. Ξέρξην.. δεῖπνον προτιθέμενον [prev. author] 9.110}}; ἀριστοποιουμένους φ. τοὺς στρατιώτας
**D** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.165: followed by a _relative_ clause, φ. ὅ τι χρήσεται.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.12.}}
**D.3** _metaphorically_, **preserve, maintain, cherish,** [χόλον] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.30; αἰδῶ καὶ φιλότητα 24.111; ὅρκια 3.280}}; φ. ἔπος **observe** a command,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|16.686; φ. ῥῆμα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.9; τελετάς [prev. author] “O.” 3.41; νόμον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 616; τοὺς νόμους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 292a, compare “Grg.” 461d, etc.; τὸ σὸν πιστόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 626; τὰς συνθήκας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.20; τὰ τοῦ γάμου δίκαια “POxy.” 905.9 (2nd c.AD)}}; λόγον πρός τινα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 56.21 (3rd c.AD, _passive_)}}; φ. σιγήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 542}}; οὐ γὰρ ἀπειλὰς ὑμετέρας ἐφύλαξα I **regarded** not your threats, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 204}}; φ. σκαιοσύναν **cling to** it, **foster** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1213 (Lyric poetry); ἄξια ἤθη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 736; φ. τῇ μνήμῃ τὰ λεχθέντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 783c}}; φ. τὸν θυμόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 867a; τὴν τιμωρίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.40; φ. καὶ ταμιεύειν πάντα τινί 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.40; τὸ μέρος τοῖς θεοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.3.4}}; τἀγαθά, opposed to κτήσασθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.23; μᾶλα ἐν κόλποισι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.120, compare 7.64; εἰ μὴ φυλάσσεις μίκρ᾽, ἀπολεῖς τὰ μείζονα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 172; ἀθάνατον ἔχθραν μὴ φύλαττε [prev. work] 4}}: with a _predicate_ added, φ. τινά δεδεμένον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.47; ἀδέκαστον φ. τὴν διάνοιαν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 34; ἀκύμααντον τὸ πέλαγος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 5.1:}} —_passive_, ὅσος παρ᾽ ὑμῖν ὁ φθόνος φυλάττεται **is fostered** by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 382.}}
**D.4** **keep in** a place, **continue in,** τόδε δῶμα φυλάσσοις, ἀθάνατός τ᾽ εἴης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.208.}}
**D.5** **notice, observe,** Ath. [prev. cited]
**D.6** **maintain, hold fast to** a view, τινὰ σπουδαῖον εἶναι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.4.9.}}
**D.7** with _accusative_, **beware of, avoid,** ἅπαντα ταῦτα φυλάττειν κελεύει {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).791.}}
**D.8** φ. μὴ with _subjunctive_, **take care** lest.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 145 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 154d}}; φ. ἐμὲ καὶ τηρεῖν ὅπως μή.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.276.}}
**D.9** _middle_, φυλάξασθε τοῦ ἀγαπᾶν Κύριον **be careful to**.., {{LXX|LXX.Josh.23.11.}}
**C** _middle_,
**C.I** _absolutely_, **to be on one's guard, keep watch,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 769; used by 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _participle_, νύκτα φυλασσομένοισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.188}}; πεφυλαγμένος εἶναι to be **cautious, prudent,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.343, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.9; φυλαττομένους πορεύεσθαι}}**with caution,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.2.30, compare “Cyn.” 10.10.}}
**C.I.2** with _accusative_, **keep** a thing **by one, bear** it **in mind** or **memory,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 263, 561}}; more fully, ἐν θυμῷ δ᾽ εὖ πάντα φυλάσσεο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 491; φρεσί “hymnus ad Apollinem” 544, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.40; τὰ λελεγμένα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1012.}}
**C.I.3** **guard, keep safe,** καὶ κεφαλὴν πεφύλαξο Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.148.}}
**C.I.4** with _genitive_, φυλάσσεσθαι τῶν νεῶν μὴ ξυντρίψωσιν **act cautiously** with regard to the ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.11}}; **beware of,** τῶν εὖ φύλαξαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 161 (Lyric poetry); Ἄρκτοι πεφυλαγμέναι ὠκεανοῖο 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 48; πεφύλαξο παντοίων ἀνέμων [prev. author] 930.}}
**C.II** φυλάσσεσθαί τι or τινα **to beware of, be on one's guard against, avoid** a thing or person, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 27, etc.: ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108, 7.130, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 4}}; τινας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 715, 804; τοὺς Ἀτρείδας εἰσορῶν φυλάξομαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 455; τέττιξ ποιμένας.. πεφυλαγμένος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.95.}}
**C.II.b** ἐφυλαξάμην διαλέκτους I **put in as a precaution** 'except in dialects', {{Refs|Hdn.Gr. 2.932.}}
**C.II.2** φ. πρός τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.69; ἀπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.9, “HG” 7.2.10.}}
**C.II.3** with _infinitive_, φυλάξομαι δὲ τάσδε μεμνῆσθαι.. ἐφετμάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 205, compare 1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 4.13; φ. μηδὲν ἐξαμαρτεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.11; φ. μηδένα βαλεῖν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.7}}; but also without μή, ἵνα.. τις ὕστερον φυλάσσηται ἐπὶ γῆν τὴν σὴν στρατεύεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.5; φ. τὸ λυπῆσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.258; φ. ὁρᾶσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 611a28, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.70; λέγειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1441b20:—two constructions joined, δισσὰ γὰρ φυλάσσεται [ψυχή], φίλων τε μέμψιν κἀς θεοὺς ἁμαρτάνειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 472.}}
**C.II.4** φ. μή followed by _subjunctive_, **take care** lest.., τούτου φυλάσσου μή ποτ᾽ ἀχθεσθῇ κέαρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 392, compare “Supp.” 498, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 67, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 257, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.16, etc.}}; so φ. ὅπως μή.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.2.37; ἃς ἐγὼ φυλάξομαι.. μὴ κατουρήσωσί μου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 831, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.14.}}

5443:
** φῡλή**, ἡ,
(φύω) properly, like{φῦλον}, **a race, tribe;** but _accusative_ to {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dicaearchus Historicus 9}} **a union formed in an organized community** (whether πόλις or ἔθνος): hence, **tribe,** i.e.
**I** **a body** of men **united**
**I.1** **by supposed ties of blood and descent, clan,** such as the three Dorian **tribes,** Rhetra cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 6, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.68, 5th c.AD(?): Stephanus Byzantius s. vv. Ὑλλεῖς, Δυμᾶνες, “IG” 4.596 (from Argos)}}; of the four Ionic **tribes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.69, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 8.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 19, etc.}}; of the Laconian, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.145}}; of the old Roman, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.7, etc.}}; of the Persian, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.5 and 12}}; of the Jewish, {{LXX|LXX.Num.1.4, +others}} (but also of subdivisions of the tribe (σκῆπτρον),{{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ki.10.20.21), NT.Matt.19.28, etc.}}
**I.2** **by local habitation,** such as the ten local **tribes** at Athens formed by Cleisthenes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.69, 6.131, “IG” 12.10.44, +others}}; or those formed by Servius at Rome, φ. τοπικαί, opposed to γενικαί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.14, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 20; in Roman Egypt, “BGU” 1113.3 (1st c.BC), “PFlor.” 39.4 (4th c.AD), etc., compare (in general) 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1264a8, 1300a25, 1309a12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 753c, etc.}}
**I.3** **subdivision of the priests in each Egyptian temple**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 56.24 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC), “PAmh.” 2.112.7 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** **military contingent furnished by a tribe,** among the Athenians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.111, “IG” 12.1085; ὁπλιτῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.98, compare 3.90, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.2.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 755c, 755d; ταξίαρχος εἰς τὴν φυλὴν κατατάξας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.79.}}
**II.2** **representatives of a tribe,** on political bodies, φυλῆς πρυτανευούσης, προεδρευούσης, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 1.26a16, “SIG” 589.2 (Magn.Mae., 2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**III** of things, ={γένος}, **kind, species,** κατὰ φυλὰς διεκρίνομεν τὰ ἔπιπλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.6.}}

5444:
** φύλλον**, τό
**leaf**; in _Epic dialect_ and {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} always in _plural_ **leaves,** or collectively **foliage,** φύλλα καὶ ὄζους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.234, +others; φύλλα δ᾽ ἔραζε χέει 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 421; τὰ φ. καταδρέποντες κατήσθιον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.115; ὅσσα τε χθὼν ἠρινὰ φ. ἀναπέμπει 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.46; ψυχὰς ἐδάη.. οἷά τε φύλλ᾽ ἄνεμος δονεῖ 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.65; _sing._, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 701 (Lyric poetry), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.10.6, etc.; οἵη περ φύλλων γενεή, τοίη δὲ καὶ ἀνδρῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.146, compare 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 2.1; φύλλων γενεᾷ προσόμοιοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 685 (_anapaest meter_); φύλλοις βάλλειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 574}}; πλεκτὰ φύλλα wreathed **leaves,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 807}}; φύλλον ἐλαίας, _poetry_ for ἐλάα, S.[same place]: _metaphorically_ of choral songs, φύλλ᾽ ἀοιδᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).27}}; of **leaves** used as **voting-papers,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(5).595 A 12 (Ceos, 3rd-2nd c.BC).}}
**2** of flowers, **petal,** [ῥόδον] ἔχον ἑξήκοντα φύλλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.138}}; ὑακίνθινα φ., λειμώνια φ., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.26, 18.39.}}
**II** **plant,** in general, φ. ὂν ἐπινηχόμενον τῷ ὕδατι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.12, compare 2nd c.AD: Numenius Apamensis Platonicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.371b}}; ἡ κατὰ φύλλον (with or without γεωμετρία) survey according to **plants,** i. e. **crops** grown, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 38.3, 78.4 (2nd c.BC): ποτίσαι εἰς φύλλον [prev. work] 72.362, 105.32 (2nd c.BC)}}: especially of medicinal **herbs,** φ. εἴ τι νώδυνον κάτοιδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 44; ἠπίοισι φ. [prev. work] 698 (Lyric poetry), compare 649.}}
**II.2** as a name of definite species:
**II.2.a** ={βρυωνία}, **dog Mercury, Mercurialis perennis,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.18.5, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.125.}}
**II.2.b** the leaf-like fruit of silphium, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 72, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.3.1, 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 4.3.32.}}
**II.2.c** ={λευκάκανθα}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.19.}}

5445:
** φύρ-ᾱμα** [ῡ], ατος, τό,
**that which is mixed** or **kneaded, dought,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 4.11 (_anapaest meter_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 929a25, LXX.Exo.8.3 (7.28), 12.34, +others, NT.Gal.5.9, NT.Rom.9.21, +others; in brewing, “PTeb.” 401.27 (1st c.AD)}}; generally, **paste,** κονίας καὶ βολβίτου φ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Geoponica” 15.2.8; καλάμου 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.55}}; opposed to θραῦσμα, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.84}}: _metaphorically_, of the human frame as **a mixture, compound,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.184, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 7.68.}}
**2** generally, **mixture,** σύμμιγμα καὶ φ. ἀέρος καὶ πυρός {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.922a, etc.}}: in _plural_, **cements,** {{Refs|[prev. work]811c.}}

5446:
** φῠσικός**, ή, όν,
**natural, produced** or **caused by nature, inborn, native,** once in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, “Mem.” 3.9.1, not in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, frequently in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus (τὰ περὶ γένεσιν φ. “Ph.” 191a3, +others)}}, and later Prose; opposed to διδακτός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus}} [same place]; opposed to νομικός (conventional), δίκαιον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1134b19}}; ἡ φ. χρῆσις, opposed to ἡ παρὰ φύσιν, {{NT|NT.Rom.1.26}}; of style, **natural, simple,** ἀληθὲς καὶ φ. χρῶμα [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 42; τὸ φ., opposed to τὸ τεχνικόν, [prev. work] 34: φ. υἱός, ={ὁ ἐκ πορνείας γεγονώς}, opposed to γνήσιος, Thom.Mag. p.362 R.; υἱὸς γνήσιος καὶ φ. “PLips.” 28.18 (4th c.AD).}}] _adverb_ -κῶς **by nature, naturally,** κινητόν, κινεῖσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 201a24, “Cael.” 307b32; ὠχυρωμένη φ. λίμνῃ 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.55; ἀκατασκεύως καὶ φ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.4.7, etc.}}
**b** **belonging to the nature** of a plant, **characteristic,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.4.4, +others}}
**2** **belonging to growth,** {{Refs|Stoic. 2.205, +others}}
**3** φ. ὀδόντες **milk**-teeth, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus cited in “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 49.}}
**II** **of** or **concerning the order of external nature, natural, physical,** ἡ φ. ἐπιστήμη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 640a2}}; φ. φιλοσοφία{{Refs|[prev. work] 653a9; ἡ φ. [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1026a6, etc.; opposed to μαθηματική, θεολογική, [prev. work] 1064b2; τὰ φ. [prev. work] 1026a4}}; οἱ φ. λόγοι _falsa lectio_ for{οἱ φυσιολόγοι}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1154b7}}; φ. προτάσεις, opposed to ἠθικαί, λογικαί, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Top.” 105b21}}; τὸ φ., τὸ ἠθικόν, τὸ λογικόν, the three branches of philosophy, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.15, etc., compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.13}}; τὰ πρῶτα καὶ -ώτατα the primal **elements** of things, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.395d.}}
**II.2** ὁ φ. **an inquirer into nature, natural philosopher,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 403a28, “PA” 641a21, “Metaphorical” 1005a34; περὶ πασῶν [τῶν αἰτιῶν] εἰδέναι τοῦ φ. [prev. author] “Ph.” 198a22, compare “Metaphorical” 1026a5}}: especially of the Ionic and other pre-Socratic philosophers, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 184b17, 187a12, 205a5, +others: also ὁ φ., of Epicurus, 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 24J.}}; ὁ φυσικώτατος, of Thales, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ner.” 4.}}
**II.2.b** army **surgeon,** uncertain in {{Refs|“IG” 12.950.153.}}
**II.3** ἡ φ. ἀκρόασις, title of a treatise by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}; τὰ φυσικά, a name given to his **physical treatises,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 267b21, “Metaphorical” 1042b8; ἐπιτομὴ φυσικῶν [prev. author] “Pr.” 10t it.}}
**II.4** _adverb_ **-κῶς** **according to the laws of nature,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 198a23; opposed to λογικῶς, [prev. work] 204b10}}: _comparative_ -ώτερον εἰπεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “GC” 335b25.}}
**III** later, **belonging to occult laws of nature, magical,** φ. φάρμακα **spells** or **amulets,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Trall. 1.15; φυσικοῖς χρῆσθαι “Geoponica” 2.18.8; φ. θεραπεία [prev. work]2.42.3; φ. δακτύλιοι Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 884}}. _adverb_ **-κῶς**{{Refs|“Geoponica” 9.1.5.}}

