5449:
** φύσις** [ῠ], ἡ,
_genitive_ φύσεως, _poetry_ φύσεος probably (for the sake of meter) in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 886, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1282 (Lyric poetry), 1458 (Lyric poetry), _Ionic dialect_ φύσιος: dual φύσει}} (variant{φύση}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 410e}}, (φύω):
**I** **origin,** φ. οὐδενός ἐστιν ἁπάντων θνητῶν οὐδὲ.. τελευτή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 8.1 (compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1112a); φ. βούλονται λέγειν γένεσιν τὴν περὶ τὰ πρῶτα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 892c; ἡ φ. ἡ λεγομένη ὡς γένεσις ὁδός ἐστιν εἰς φύσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 193b12; φ. λέγεται ἡ τῶν φυομένων γένεσις [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1014b16}}; frequently of persons, **birth,** φύσει νεώτερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1295, compare “Aj.” 1301, etc.; φύσι γεγονότες εὖ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.134}}; φύσει, opposed to θέσει (by adoption), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 9.25; φύσει Ἀμβρακιώτης, δημοποίητος δὲ Σικυώνιος 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.183d}}; so ὁ κατὰ φύσιν πατήρ, υἱός, ἀδελφός, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.9.6, 3.12.3, 11.2.2}}; also in _accusative_, ἐκ πατρὸς ταὐτοῦ φύσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 325}}; ἢ φίλων τις ἢ πρὸς αἵματος φύσιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1125, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.42.}}
**I.2** **growth,** τριχῶν, παιδίου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 20, 29, compare 27: _plural_, γενειάσεις καὶ φύσεις κεράτων 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.3.13.}}
**II** **the natural form** or **constitution** of a person or thing **as the result of growth** (οἷον ἕκαστόν ἐστι τῆς γενέσεως τελεσθείσης, ταύτην φαμὲν τὴν φ. εἶναι ἑκάστου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1252b33): hence}}
**II.1** **nature, constitution,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, καί μοι φύσιν αὐτοῦ (i.e. τοῦ φαρμάκου) ἔδειξε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.303; φ. τῆς χώρης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.5; τῆς Ἀττικῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 1.2, compare “Oec.” 16.2, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.146, etc.; τῆς τριχός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 5.5}}; αἵματος, ἀέρος, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 648a21, “Mete.” 340a36, etc.: _plural_, φύσεις ἐγγιγνομένας καρπῶν καὶ δένδρων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.74; αἱ φ. καὶ δυνάμεις τῶν πολιτειῶν [prev. author] 12.134; ἡ τῶν ἀριθμῶν φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 525c; ἡ τῶν πάντων φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.11, etc.; ἡ ἰδία τοῦ πράγματος φ. “IG” 22.1099.28 (Epist.Plotinae).}}
**II.2** **outward form, appearance,** μέζονας ἢ κατ᾽ ἀνθρώπων φύσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.38}}; ἢ νόον ἤτοι φύσιν either in mind or **outward form,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.5; οὐ γὰρ φ. Ὠαριωνείαν ἔλαχεν [prev. author] “I.” 4(3).49 (67); μορφῆς δ᾽ οὐχ ὁμόστολος φ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 496; τὸν δὲ Λάϊον φύσιν τίν᾽ εἶχε φράζε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 740 (read εἷρπε, taking φ. with ἔχων),compare “Tr.” 379}}; δρακαίνης φ. ἔχουσαν ἀγρίαν probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1358; τὴν ἐμὴν ἰδὼν φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1071 (troch.), compare “Nu.” 503; τὴν τοῦ σώματος φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.75.}}
**II.3** in Medicine texts, **constitution, temperament,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.2 (_plural_), +others; ἡ φ. καὶ ἡ ἕξις [prev. author] “Acut.” 43; φ. φύσιος καὶ ἡλικία ἡλικίης διαφέρει [prev. author] “Fract.” 7; φύσιες νούσων ἰητροί [prev. author] “Epid.” 6.5.1.}}
**II.3.b** **natural place** or **position** of a bone or joint, ἀποπηδᾶν ἀπὸ τῆς φ., ἐς τὴν φ. ἄγεσθαι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Art.” 61, 62, +others; ὀστέον μένον ἐν τῇ ἑωυτοῦ φ. [prev. author] “VC” 5, +others; φύσιες τῶν ἄρθρων [prev. author] “Nat.Puer.” 17.}}
**II.4** of the mind, one's **nature, character,** ἦθος ἕκαστον, ὅπῃ φ. ἐστὶν ἑκάστῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 110.5; εὐγενὴς γὰρ ἡ φ. κἀξ εὐγενῶν.. ἡ σή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 874; τὴν αὑτοῦ φ. λιπεῖν, δεῖξαι, [prev. work] 902, 1310}}; φ. φρενός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 103 (_anapaest meter_); ἡ ἀνθρωπεία φ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76; φ. τῆς μορφῆς καὶ τῆς ψυχῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.2; ὀνόματι μεμπτὸν τὸ νόθον, ἡ φ. δ᾽ ἴση 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 168}}; φ. φιλόσοφος, τυραννική, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 410e, 576a, etc.; δεξιοὶ φύσιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 489; ἀκμαῖοι φύσιν [prev. author] “Pers.” 441; τὸ γὰρ ἀποστῆναι χαλεπὸν φύσεος, ἣν ἔχοι τις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1458 (Lyric poetry), compare 1282 (Lyric poetry)}}; Σόλων.. ἦν φιλόδημος τὴν φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1187; ἔνιοι ὄντες ὡς ἀληθῶς τοῦ δήμου τὴν φ. οὐ δημοτικοί εἰσι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.19}}; φύσεως ἰσχύς force **of natural powers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138}}; φύσεως κακία badness **of natural disposition,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.140; ἀγαθοὶ.. γίγνονται διὰ τριῶν, τὰ τρία δὲ ταῦτά ἐστι φ. ἔθος λόγος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1332a40}}; χρῶ τῇ φύσει, i.e. give rein to your **natural propensities,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1078, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.38; τῇ φ. χρώμενος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 18; θείας κοινωνοὶ φ. NT.2Pet.1.4: _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.113, variant in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 956; οἱ ἄριστοι τὰς φ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 526c, compare 375b, +others: proverbial, ἔθος, φασί, δευτέρη φ. 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Misopogon” 353a.}}
**II.4.b** **instinct** in animals, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 278; οὐκ ἐπιστήμῃ οὐδὲ τέχνῃ ἀλλὰ φύσει 2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.41.6; ἐν τοῖς ἄλλοις ζῴοις ἡ αἴσθησις τῇ φ. ἥνωται, ἐν δὲ ἀνθρώποις τῇ νοήσει “Corpus Hermeticum” 9.1, compare 12.1.}}
**II.5** frequently in periphrases, καὶ γὰρ ἂν πέτρου φύσιν σύ γ᾽ ὀργάνειας, i.e. would'st provoke a stone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 335; χθονὸς φ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 633; especially in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, ἡ τοῦ πτεροῦ φ. “Phdr.” 251b; ἡ φ. τῶν σωμάτων “Smp.” 186b}}; ἡ φ. τῆς ἀσθενείας its **natural** weakness, {{Refs|“Phd.” 87e; ἡ τοῦ μυελοῦ φ. “Ti.” 84c; ἡ τοῦ δικαίου φ. “Lg.” 862d, +others}}; ἡ φ., with _genitive_ understood, {{Refs|“Smp.” 191a, “Phd.” 109e.}}
**III** **the regular order of nature,** τύχη.. ἀβέβαιος, φ. δὲ αὐτάρκης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 176; κατὰ φύσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 444d, etc.}}; τρίχες κατὰ φύσιν πεφυκυῖαι growing **naturally,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.38, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 156.7 (troch.); κατὰ φύσιν νόμος ὁ πάντων βασιλεύς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 169 (compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 488b); κατὰ φ. ποιεῖν 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 112}}; opposed to παρὰ φύσιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 395, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.17, etc.; παρὰ τὴν φ. 4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 1.18}}; προδότης ἐκ φύσεως a traitor by **nature,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.165}}; πρὸ τῆς φ. ἥκειν εἰς θάνατον before the **natural term,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp. Dem.Cic.” 5: frequently in _dative_ φύσει (ἐν φ. 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 14)}} **by nature, naturally,** opposed to τύχῃ, τέχνῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 889b, compare “R.” 381b; φύσει τοιοῦτος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 275, compare 279, +others; ὁ ἄνθρωπος φ. πολιτικὸν ζῷόν ἐστι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1253a3}}; ὁ μὴ αὑτοῦ φ. ἀλλ᾽ ἄλλου ἄνθρωπος ὤν, οὗτος φ. δοῦλός ἐστιν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1254a15; φ. γὰρ οὐδεὶς δοῦλος ἐγενήθη ποτέ 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 95.2}}; opposed to νόμῳ (by convention), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 9, Archelaus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 482e, compare “Prt.” 337d, etc.; τὰ μὲν τῶν νόμων ὁμολογηθέντα, οὐ φύντ᾽ ἐστίν, τὰ δὲ τῆς φύσεως φύντα, οὐχ ὁμολογηθέντα 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 44 “Ai” 32 (“Vorsokr.” 5); ἅπας ὁ τῶν ἀνθρώπων βίος φύσει καὶ νόμοις διοικεῖται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.15; τοὺς τῆς φ. οὐκ ἔστι λανθάνειν νόμους 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 492; οὐ σοφίᾳ, ἀλλὰ φύσει τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22c; φ. μὴ πεφυκότα τοιαῦτα φωνεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 79, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 14c, etc.; φύσει πάντα πάντες ὁμοίως πεφύκαμεν καὶ βάρβαροι καὶ Ἕλληνες εἶναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 44 “Bii” 10 (“Vorsokr.” 5)}}; φύσιν ἔχει with _infinitive_, it is **natural,** κῶς φύσιν ἔχει πολλὰς μυριάδας φονεῦσαι (i.e. τὸν Ἡρακλέα); {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.45, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 473a}}; οὐκ ἔχει φύσιν it is contrary to **nature,**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 489b; οὔτ᾽ εὔλογον οὔτ᾽ ἔχον ἐστὶ φύσιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.26; τὸ τόλμημα φύσιν οὐκ ἔχει 1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista “Callimachus” 36.}}
**IV** in Philosophy:
**IV.1** **nature as an originating power,** φ. λέγεται.. ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις ἡ πρώτη ἐν ἑκάστῳ τῶν φύσει ὄντων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1014b16; ὁ δὲ θεὸς καὶ ἡ φ. οὐδὲν μάτην ποιοῦσιν [prev. author] “Cael.” 271a33}}; ἡ δὲ φ. οὐδὲν ἀλόγως οὐδὲ μάτην ποιεῖ{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 291b13; ἡ μὲν τέχνη ἀρχὴ ἐν ἄλλῳ, ἡ δὲ φ. ἀρχὴ ἐν αὐτῷ [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1070a8, compare “Mete.” 381b5, etc.; φ. κρύπτεσθαι φιλεῖ 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 123; ἡ γοητεία τῆς φ. 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.4.44}}; φ. κοινή, the **principle of growth** in the universe, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Cleanthes Stoicus 1.126}}; as Stoic technical term, the inner fire which causes preservation and growth in plants and animals, defined as πῦρ τεχνικὸν ὁδῷ βαδίζον εἰς γένεσιν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 1.44, compare 35, +others, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.81}}; **Nature,** personified, χάρις τῇ μακαρίᾳ Φ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 469; Φ. καὶ Εἱμαρμένη καὶ Ἀνάγκη 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 12; ἡ κατωφερὴς Φ. “Corpus Hermeticum” 1.14.}}
**IV.2** **elementary substance,** κινδυνεύει ὁ λέγων ταῦτα πῦρ καὶ ὕδωρ καὶ γῆν καὶ ἀέρα πρῶτα ἡγεῖσθαι τῶν πάντων εἶναι καὶ τὴν φ. ὀνομάζειν αὐτὰ ταῦτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 891c, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 52 (defined as τὴν πρώτην οὐσίαν.. ὑποβεβλημένην ἅπασι τοῖς γεννητοῖς καὶ φθαρτοῖς σώμασι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.3); τῶν φύσει ὄντων τὰ στοιχεῖά φασιν εἶναι φύσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1014b33: _plural_, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.6U., +others; ἄτομοι φ.}}**atoms,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 5, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.7U.; ἄφθαρτοι φ. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 83.}}
**IV.3** concrete, the **creation, 'Nature',** ἀθανάτου.. φύσεως κόσμον ἀγήρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 910 (_anapaest meter_); περὶ φύσεώς τε καὶ τῶν μετεώρων ἀστρονομικὰ ἄττα διερωτᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315c}}; περὶ φύσεως, title of works by Xenophanes, Heraclitus, Gorgias, Epicurus, etc.; [σοφία] ἣν δὴ καλοῦσι περὶ φύσεως ἱστορίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 96a; περὶ φ. ἀφοριζόμενοι διεχώριζον ζῴων τε βίον δένδρων τε φύσιν λαχάνων τε γένη 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 11.13 (_anapaest meter_); so later, ἡ φ. τὸ ὑπὸ ψυχῆς τῆς πάσης ταχθέν 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.2.1; τὰ στοιχεῖα τῆς φ. “Corpus Hermeticum” 1.8}}; αἱ δύο φ., i.e. heaven and earth, light and darkness, etc., {{Refs|“PMag.Leid.W.” 6.42.}}
**IV.4** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **two,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 12.}}
**V** as a concrete term, **creature,** frequently in _collective_ sense, θνητὴ φ. man**kind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 590 (_anapaest meter_), compare “OT” 869 (Lyric poetry)}}; πόντου εἰναλία φ. the **creatures** of the sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 345 (Lyric poetry); ὃ πᾶσα φ. διώκειν πέφυκε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 359c, compare “Plt.” 272c}}; ἡ τῶν θηλειῶν φ. woman-**kind** (opposed to τὸ ἄρρεν φῦλον) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 3.4}}: also in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 674, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 588c, “Plt.” 306e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 13.9}}; in contemptuous sense, αἱ τοιαῦται φ. such **creatures** as these, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.113, compare 20.11, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.191.}}
**V.b** of plants or material **substances,** φ. εὐώδεις καρποφοροῦσαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.49; ὑγράν τινα φ. καπνὸν ἀποδιδοῦσαν “Corpus Hermeticum” 1.4.}}
**VI** **kind, sort, species,** ταύτην.. ἔχειν βιοτῆς.. φύσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 165 (_anapaest meter_); ἐκλέγονται ἐκ τούτων χρημάτων μίαν φ. τὴν τῶν λευκῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 429d}}; φ. [ἀλωπεκίδων] **species,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.1}}; **natural group** or **class** of plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.1.1 (_plural_).}}
**VII** **sex,** θῆλυς φῦσα (probably to be read instead of οὖσα) κοὐκ ἀνδρὸς φύσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1062, compare “OC” 445, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 770d, 944d: hence}}
**VII.2** **the characteristic of sex,** ={αἰδοῖον}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Tab.Defix.” 89a6 (4th c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 107, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 32.10, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.150, etc.}}: especially of the female organ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.143, 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 41, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 5.63, “PMag.Osl.” 1.83,324, 4th c.AD(?): Horapollo 1.11: _plural_, τῶν δύοφ.}} of the testes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 92, compare “PMag.Par.” 1.318.}}

5451:
** φῠτ-εία**, ἡ,
**planting,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.20, 19.1, “PSI” 4.433.5 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.: _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.12, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.5.1.}}
**2** **generation, production,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 121c.}}
**II** **growth, habit of a plant,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.8.4.}}
**III** **plantation** or simply **a plant,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Moschio Tragicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.207d, NT.Matt.15.13, “OGI” 606.7 (Abila, 1st c.AD).}}

5452:
** φῠτ-εύω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.340, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ φυτευέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 812}}; _future_ εύσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.13}}; _aorist_ ἐφύτευσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.419, etc.:}}_perfect_ πεφύτευκα, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ πεφύτευκαν {{LXX|LXX.Eze.19.13}}:—_middle_, _future_ **-εύσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.15}}: _aorist_ **-ευσάμην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.8}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ευθήσομαι**{{Refs|“Geoponica” 5.19.1}}: _aorist_ ἐφυτεύθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.3.12}}, _poetry3rd.pers._ _plural_ φύτευθεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.69}}: _perfect_ πεφύτευμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.138, etc.}}: (φυτόν).
**I** with _accusative_ **of the thing planted, plant** trees, especially fruit-trees, οὔτε φυτεύουσιν χερσὶν φυτὸν οὔτ᾽ ἀρόωσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.108; δένδρεα φ. 18.359, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 44, etc.; ἄλσος πεφυτευμένον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same place]; συκᾶς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 108 (troch.)}}; ὄρχους, ἀμπέλους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.3, 4}}; joined with σπείρω,{{NT+8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 11.16, “Mem.” 2.1.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276e; φυτείαν NT.Matt.15.13; ἀρώμεναι ἠδὲ φ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 22; φ. ἐν γῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.2}}; ἐν ἀφόρῳ{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 20.3; εἰς γῆν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.986f; φ. ἀπὸ κορύνης “Geoponica” 10.8.1}}:—_middle_, **plant for oneself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.15, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 20:}} — _passive_, δένδρα πεφυτευμένα, opposed to to those of spontaneous growth, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 55.13.}}
**I.2** _metaphorically_, **beget, engender,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 812, “Sc.” 29, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.145, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.84, etc.; φυτεύων παῖδας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 662, compare “Or.” 11, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1133, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 50d; ὁ φυτεύσας πατήρ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 793, 1514, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1177 (assuming variant)}}; ὁ φυτεύσας alone, the **father,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 904, “Tr.” 1244; ὁ φ. αὐτόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 49, etc.: opposed to ἡ τεκοῦσα, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 11.4}} (rarely of the mother, σὲ.. φύτευσεν Αἴθρα Ποσειδᾶνι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 16.59)}}; οἱ φυτεύσαντες the **parents,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1007, “OC” 1377; τοὺς τεκόντας καὶ φυτ. [prev. author] “fragment” 64, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1092: _metaphorically_, ὕβρις φυτεύει τύραννον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 873 (Lyric poetry); Μούσας λέγουσι Ἁρμονίαν φυτεῦσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 832 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_passive_, **to be begotten, spring** from parents, κείνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.144; ἐκ Κρόνου [prev. author] “N.” 5.7; τοῦ κακοῦ πότμου φυτευθείς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1324.}}
**I.3** generally, **produce, bring about, cause,** mostly of evils, ὅτι τοι κακὰ πολλὰ φυτεύει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.340; πρὶν ἡμῖν πῆμα φυτεῦσαι 4.668 (variant); φόνον καὶ κῆρα φ. 2.165, 17.82; once in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, κακὸν μέγα πᾶσι φ. 15.134; φύτευέ οἱ θάνατον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.59; φ. πῆμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 953}}; also in good sense, φ. γάμον, δόξαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.111, “I.” 6 (5). 12; Μίνωϊ τιμάν 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 16.68; καδέων ἀνάπαυσιν [prev. author] 18.35}}:—_passive_, σὺν θεῷ φυτευθείς ὄλβος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.17.}}
**I.4** **implant in,** τοῦθ᾽ ἡμῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 80e; τι εἴς τι [prev. author] “Phdr.” 248d.}}
**I.5** **set up,** ἄγαλμα ἐν τῷ οἴκῳ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 18.84.}}
**II** less frequently with _accusative_ **of the ground planted, plant with fruit-trees,** φ. γῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2; χωρίον φ. καὶ γεωργεῖν 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.28: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 13, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 116}}:—_middle_, ἀγρόν φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.8}}:—_passive_, γῆ πεφυτευμένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.127, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.10}}; opposed to ψιλή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 230, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.115; τὰ πεφυτευμένα “PHal.” 1.102 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; γεωργία καὶ ψιλὴ καὶ πεφ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1259a2.}}

5453:
** φύω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.148, etc.}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **φυίω** perhaps to be read in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 97:}}_imperfect_ ἔφυον, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ φύεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.347:}}_future_ φύσω [ῡ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|] 1.235, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 438}}: _aorist_ ἔφῡσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.393, etc.}}:—_passive_ and _middle_,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|9.109, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.28, etc.}}: _future_ φύσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 871, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mochl.” 42, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 831a, etc.}}: similar in sense are the _intransitive_ tenses, _perfect_ πέφῡκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.114, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ πεφύᾱσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.484, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.128}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ πεφύῃ (ἐμ-) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 396}}; _Epic dialect_ _participle_ _feminine_ πεφυυῖα (ἐμ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.513, _accusative_ _plural_ πεφυῶτας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.477}}; _Doric dialect_ _infinitive_ πεφύκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 173.3}}: _pluperfect_ ἐπεφύκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 69e}}; _Epic dialect_ πεφύκειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.109}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐπέφῡκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 152, “Op.” 149:}}_aorist 2_ ἔφῡν (as if from φῦμι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.397, etc.}}: _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ φῦ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.253, etc., _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔφυν (for ἔφῡσαν, which is also _3rd.pers._ _plural_ of _aorist_ 1) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.481, etc.}}; _subjunctive_ φύω or φυῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 377.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 415c, 597c, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ σαρκῶν” 12}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _optative_ φύη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.94, (συμ-) 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.89}}; _infinitive_ φῦναι, _Epic dialect_ φύμεναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.39, φῦν 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.10}}; _participle_ φύς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.410, etc.}}, _Boeotian dialect_ _feminine_ φοῦσα [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 21: ἔφυσεν, ={ἔφυ}, uncertain in “IG” 14.2126.5 (from Rome)}}]; conversely ἔφυ, ={ἔφυσεν}, {{Refs|[prev. work]3.1350, “Sammelb.” 5883 (from Cyrene)}}: later, _future_ φυήσω {{LXX|LXX.Isa.37.31}}, _passive_ φυήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Geoponica” 2.37.1, 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 21.248c (in 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 19 ἀναφύσεσθαι is restored)}}: _aorist 2_ _passive_ ἐφύην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.1.1, probably in “BSA” 28.124 (from Didyma), (ἀν-) 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.16.2}}; _infinitive_ φυῆναι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.6, (ἀνα-) 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.7}}; _participle_ φῠείς {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 22, “Tragica Adespota” 529, “PTeb.” 787.30 (2nd c.BC), NT.Luke.8.6}}: _aorist 1_ _passive_ συμ-φυθείς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.725}}. [Generally ῠ before a vowel, _Epic dialect_, Trag. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 535, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 910.2)}}, etc., ῡ before a consonant; but φῡει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Tragica Adespota” 454.2, φῡεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 88.4, “Tragica Adespota” 543 (= 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 565); φῡομεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 106}}; ἐφῡετο probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 680, compare 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 14, 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 941, 1013}}; even in thesi, προσφῡονται {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 506, φῡουσιν 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 1031}}; also in compounds]
**A** _transitive_, in _present_, _future_, and _aorist 1_ _active_:—**bring forth, produce, put forth,** φύλλα.. ὕλη τηλεθόωσα φύει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.148; τοῖσι δ᾽ ὑπὸ χθὼν δῖα φύεν νεοθηλέα ποίην 14.347, compare 1.235, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.119, etc.; ἄμπελον φύει βροτοῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 651}}; so τρίχες.., ἃς πρὶν ἔφυσεν φάρμακον **made** the hair **grow,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.393, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 535; φ. χεῖρε, πόδε, ὀφθαλμὰ ἀνθρώποις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.19}}; of a country, καρπόν τε θωμαστὸν φύειν καὶ ἄνδρας ἀγαθούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.122; ὅσα γῆ φύει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 621a, compare 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 4.}}
**2** **beget, engender,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 869, etc.; Ἄτλας.. θεῶν μιᾶς ἔφυσε Μαῖαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 3, compare “Tragica Adespota” 454.2}}; so of God **creating** man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.2, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1065c}}; ὁ φύσας the **begetter, father** (opposed to ὁ φύς the **son,** see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.2), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1019, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1472 (Lyric poetry); ὁ φ. πατήρ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 87; ὁ φ. χἠ τεκοῦσα [prev. author] Alcaeus290; τὴν τεκοῦσαν ἢ τὸν φύσαντα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.8}}; of both parents, γονεῦσι οἵ σ᾽ ἔφυσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 436; οἱ φύσαντες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 34, compare “fragment” 403.2; φ. τε καὶ γεννᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 274a; ὦ γάμοι γάμοι, ἐφύσαθ᾽ ἡμᾶς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1404}}; ἥδ᾽ ἡμέρα φύσει σε **will bring to light** thy **birth,**{{Refs|[prev. work] 438; χρόνος φύει τ᾽ ἄδηλα καὶ φανέντα κρύπτεται [prev. author] “Aj.” 647.}}
**3** of individuals in reference to the growth of parts of themselves, φ. πώγωνα, γλῶσσαν, κέρεα, **grow** or **get** a beard, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.104, 2.68, 4.29; φ. πτερά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 106, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251c; σάρκα [prev. author] “Ti.” 74e}}; φ. τρίχας, πόδας καὶ πτερά, etc., [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 518a33, 554a29, etc.: for the joke in φύειν φράτερας, see at {φράτηρ}.}}]
**4** _metaphorically_, φρένας φῦσαι **get** understanding, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 804, “El.” 1463 (but also θεοὶ φύουσιν ἀνθρώποις φρένας [prev. author] “Ant.” 683): proverbial, ἁλιεὺς πληγεὶς νοῦν φύσει}} 'once bit, twice shy', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 222b; γέροντα τὸν νοῦν σάρκα δ᾽ ἡβῶσαν φύει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 622}}; δόξαν φῦσαι **get** glory or **to form** a high opinion of oneself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.91; θεὸς.. αἰτίαν φύει βροτοῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Niob.” in “PSI” 11.1208.15; αὑτῷ πόνους φῦσαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 647.}}
**II** in _present_ seemingly _intransitive_, **put forth shoots,** εἰς ἔτος ἄλλο φύοντι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.101; δρύες.. φύοντι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.75, compare 4.24: so ἀνδρῶν γενεὴ ἡ μὲν φύει ἡ δ᾽ ἀπολήγει}} one generation **is putting forth scions,** the other is ceasing to do so, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.149}}; ἐν στήθεσι φύει (perhaps φυίει) **grows up, appears,** {{LXX+7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 97; ῥίζα ἄνω φύουσα ἐν χολῇ LXX.Deu.29.18.}}
**B** _passive_, with _intransitive_ tenses of _active_, _aorist_ 2, _perfect_ and _pluperfect_, **grow, wax, spring up** or **forth,** especially of the vegetable world, θάμνος ἔφυ ἐλαίης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.190, compare 5.481; πρασιαὶ παντοῖαι πεφύασιν 7.128; τά γ᾽ ἄσπαρτα φύονται 9.109, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.483, 14.288, 21.352; φύεται αὐτόματα ῥόδα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.138, compare 1.193; ὑπὸ φηγῷ πεφυκυίῃ}}**growing there,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.56}}; πεφυκότα δένδρα trees **growing there,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.5; τὰ φυόμενα καὶ τὰ γιγνόμενα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 410d, compare “Phd.” 110d, “Plt.” 272a}}; τοῦ κέρα ἐκ κεφαλῆς ἑκκαιδεκάδωρα πεφύκει from his head **grew** horns sixteen palms long, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.109, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108, 3.133; φύονται πολιαί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.28}}; κεφαλαὶ πεφυκυῖαι θριξί **grown with** hair, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.50 (assuming variant); πέφυκε λίθος ἐν αὐτῇ}}**is produced,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 1.4: _metaphorically_, νόσημα ἐν ὀλιγαρχίᾳ φυόμενον, φυομένη πόλις, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 564b, “Lg.” 757d}}; ὁ σπέρμα παρασχών, οὗτος τῶν φύντων αἴτιος [κακῶν] **of the things produced,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.159}}; also κατὰ πάντων ἐφύετο **waxed great by** or **upon** their depression, {{Refs|[prev. work] 19.}}—In this sense _aorist 2_ is rare (see. above), except in phrases such as ἔν τ᾽ ἄρα οἱ φῦ χειρί (see. ἐμφύω), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.302.}}
**B.2** of persons, **to be begotten** or **born,** most frequently in _aorist 2_ and _perfect_, ὁ λωφήσων οὐ πέφυκέ πω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 27; τίς ἂν εὔξαιτο βροτὸς ὢν ἀσινεῖ δαίμονι φῦνα; [prev. author] “Ag.” 1342 (_anapaest meter_)}}; μὴ φῦναι τὸν ἅπαντα νικᾷ λόγον not **to be born** is best, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1224 (Lyric poetry)}}; γονῇ πεφυκὼς.. γεραιτέρᾳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1294; οὐχ ὑπὸ θυσιῶν οὐδ᾽ ὑπὸ εὐχῶν φύς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 461a}}; φύς τε καὶ τραφείς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 396c; μήπω φῦναι μηδὲ γενέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.7, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 197a: construed with _genitive_, πεφμκέναι or φῦναί τινος}} **to be born** or **descended from** any one, τὸ κοινὸν σπλάγχνον οὗ πεφύκαμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1036, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1379, etc.; θνατᾶς ἀπὸ ματρὸς ἔφυ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 61, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1359 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 562; ἀπ᾽ εὐγενοῦς ῥίζης 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 610; ἀπὸ δρυός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 34d, etc.; φ. ἔκ τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 458, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 325, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 415c, etc.; ἐκ χώρας τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.24, etc.}}; οἱ μετ᾽ ἐκείνου φύντες, opposed to οἱ ἐξ ἐκείνου γεγονότες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.30; ἐκ θεῶν γεγονότι.. διὰ βασιλέων πεφυκότι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.24.}}
**B.II** in _present_, **become,** οὐδεὶς ἐχθρὸς οὔτε φύεται πρὸς χρήμαθ᾽ οἵ τε φύντες.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 88.4; πιστοὺς φύσει φύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.13}}; the _perfect_ and _aorist 2_ take a _present_ sense, **to be** so and so **by nature,** κακός, σοφός πέφυκα (-κώς), etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 558, 1244, etc.; δρᾶν ἔφυν ἀμήχανος [prev. author] “Ant.” 79}}; φύντ᾽ ἀρετᾷ **born** for virtue, i.e. brave and good **by nature,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).20}}; so of things, τὸ μὲν εὖ πράσσειν ἀκόρεστον ἔφυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1331 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 479d, etc.; εὐχροώτεροι ὁρῷντο ἢ πεφύκασιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.41, compare “Oec.” 10.2; [τὸ πῦρ] πέφυκε τοιοῦτον [prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.1.10; τἄλλα ἕκαστος ἡμῶν, ὅπως ἔτυχε, πέφυκεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.56: with Advs., ἱκανῶς πεφυκότες}} **of** good **natural ability,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.1; δυσκόλως πεφ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.6; οὕτως πεφ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.7; also οἱ καλῶς πεφυκότες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 989, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.20; οἱ βέλτιστα φύντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 431c}}: then, simply, **to be** so and so, φῦναι Ζηνὶ πιστὸν ἄγγελον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 969; θεοῦ μήτηρ ἔφυς [prev. author] “Pers.” 157 (troch.); γυναῖκε.. ἔφυμεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 62}}; Ἅιδης ὁ παύσων ἔφυ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 575; ἁπλοῦς ὁ μῦθος τῆς ἀληθείας ἔφυ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 469}}: with _participle_, νικᾶν.. χρῄζων ἔφυν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1052; πρέπων ἔφυς.. φωνεῖν [prev. author] “OT” 9, compare 587; τοῦτο ἴδιον ἔφυμεν ἔχοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.48, compare 11.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.54.}}
**B.II.2** with _infinitive_, **to be formed** or **disposed by nature** to do so and so, τὰ δεύτερα πέφυκε κρατεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 279; πολλῷ γ᾽ ἀμείνων τοὺς πέλας φρενοῦν ἔφυς ἢ σαυτόν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 337; ἔφυν γὰρ οὐδὲν ἐκ τέχνης πράσσειν κακῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 88, compare “Ant.” 688; φύσει μὴ πεφυκότα τοιαῦτα φωνεῖν [prev. author] “Ph.” 79; πεφύκασι δ᾽ ἅπαντες.. ἁμαρτάνειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45, compare 2.64, 3.39, 4.61}}, etc.; πέφυκε.. τρυφὴ.. ἦθος διαφθείρειν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.15c.}}
**B.II.3** with Preps., γυνὴ.. ἐπὶ δακρύοις ἔφυ **is by nature prone** to tears, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 928}}; ἔρως γὰρ ἀργόν, κἀπὶ τοῖς ἀργοῖς ἔφυ **is inclined** to idleness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 322; also ἐπί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 507e; εἴς τι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.132; most frequently πρός τι, οἱ ἄνθρωποι πρὸς τὸ ἀληθὲς πεφύκασι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1355a16; εὖ πρὸς ἀρετὴν πεφυκότες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.1.2; πρὸς πόλεμον μᾶλλον.. ἢ πρὸς εἰρήνην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 547e; κάλλιστα φ. πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.3, etc.; also πρός τινι [prev. author] “Ath.” 2.19 (assuming variant, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.29.10); also εὖ πεφ. κατά τι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.55.}}
**B.II.4** with _dative_, **fall** to one **by nature, be** one's **natural lot,** πᾶσι θνατοῖς ἔφυ μόρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 860 (Lyric poetry); χαίρειν πέφυκεν οὐχὶ τοῖς αὐτοῖς ἀεί [prev. author] “Tr.” 440; ἐφύετο κοινὸς πᾶσι κίνδυνος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.18, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.19.}}
**B.II.5** _impersonal_, **it is natural, it happens naturally,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.30, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1261b7, “Po.” 1450a1.}}
**B.II.6** _absolutely_, ὡς πέφυκε as **is natural,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.15, +others; ᾗ πέφυκεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 81e}}; also expressed personally, τοῖς ἁπλῶς, ὡς πεφυκασι, βαδίζουσι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.68: also frequently in}}_participle_, τὰ φύσει πεφυκότα the order of **nature,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.29}}; φύντα, opposed to ὁμολογηθέντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 44 A i 32 (“Vorsokr.” 5)}}; ἄνθρωπος πεφυκώς man **as he is,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.3}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _bhū-_ 'to be, become', Lithuanian _búti_ 'to be', Latin _fui,_ Eng. **be,** etc.)

5454:
** φωλ-εός**, ὁ,
_Epic dialect_ _genitive_ φωλειοῖο {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 2.249, irregular _plural_ φωλεά 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 83}}, _Epic dialect_ _dative_ φωλειοῖς {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Th.” 79, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 2.578, +others}}:—**den, lair,** especially of **the caves of bears,** in which they hibernate, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.169e}}; of lions, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 106.2}}; of molluscs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 622b4}}; of a serpent, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 11}}; of foxes, {{NT|NT.Matt.8.20, NT.Luke.9.58}}; of animals in general, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.29}}; of cave-dwellers, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 11.5.7, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.37, etc.}}
**II** **schoolhouse,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

5455:
** φων-έω**,
(φωνή) **produce a sound** or **tone**:
**I** properly of men, **speak loud** or **clearly,** or simply, **speak, give utterance,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in}}_aorist_ (_present_ and _imperfect_ only in compounds); ὣς ἄρα φωνήσας ἀπέβη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.116, compare 11.531, +others; ἔπος φάτο φώνησέν τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.370; φωνήσας προσέφη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.41}}; καί μιν φωνήσας ἔπεα πτερόεντα προσηύδα addressed him **with a loud voice** in winged words, {{Refs|1.201, compare 4.284, +others}}; φωνοῦντος ἢ ἠχοῦντος ἢ ψοφοῦντος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.13U.}}; followed by the words spoken, φώνησε (φώνασε codices) δ᾽, εὕδεις, βασιλεῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.67; χρυσοῖς δὲ φωνεῖ γράμμασιν, πρήσω πόλιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 434, compare “Ag.” 1334 (_anapaest meter_)}}; οἱ βουλευταὶ ἐφώνησαν.. the Senators **exclaimed..,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|POxy. 2110.6 (4th c.AD)}}; “βέκος φ. **utter** the word βέκος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ὄπα φωνησάσης}} **having made** her voice **sound,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.535; φ. φάτιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 329: with _neuter_}} _adjective_, φ. μέγιστον ἀνθρώπων **to have the** loudest **voice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.141, 7.117; ὄρθιον φ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.76; ἄλλο τι φ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1063 (_anapaest meter_); τάδε φ. [prev. author] “Ch.” 314 (_anapaest meter_); μέγα φ. [prev. author] “Eu.” 936 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 574; ἄπυστα φ. [prev. author] “OC” 489; ὅσια φ. [prev. author] “Ph.” 662}}; δεινὸν φ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1225; εὔφημα [prev. author] “Aj.” 362, 591, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 687, etc.; μηδεὶς ἔπος φωνείτω “IG” 22.1368.108; τὸν ῥηϊδίως φωνεῦντα πᾶν ἔπος 4th c.BC: Anaxarchus Philosophus 1}}: _absolutely_, **cry aloud,** as in joy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 202}}; of a singer, ἀοιδὸς.. αἰόλα φωνέων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.44:}} —_passive_, τὰ φωνηθέντα **sounds** or **words uttered,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 262c, “Ti.” 72a, compare 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 39.4.}}
**I.2** of animals, **utter their cries,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 578a32}}; of birds,{{Refs|[prev. work] 593a14}}; [τὰ σελάχη] φωνεῖν οὐκ ὀρθῶς ἔχει φάναι, ψοφεῖν δέ{{LXX|[prev. work] 535b25; ἐφώνησε πέρδιξ LXX.Jer.17.11}}; of the cock, **crow,** {{NT|NT.Matt.26.34, +others}}
**I.3** as law-term, **affirm, testify** in court, {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 1.18, +others (written πωνίω).}}
**I.4** of a musical instrument, **sound,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 146 (Lyric poetry)}}; of sounds, ἡδὺ φωνεῖν **sound** sweetly, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1021b}}; but βροντὴ φ. it **has a voice, is significant,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 12.}}
**I.5** ἄφωνα καὶ φωνοῦντα consonants and **vowels,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 578 codices 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes (perhaps ἄφωνα φωνήεντα).}}
**II** with _accusative_ _person_, **call by name, call,** Αἴαντα φωνῶ I **call** 'Ajax' {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 73, compare “Ph.” 229, NT.Matt.27.47, etc.}}; **call** by a name, ὑμεῖς φωνεῖτέ με ὁ διδάσκαλος {{NT|NT.John.13.13}}:—_passive_, **to be called,** τὰ ἀρχαῖα ἐκπώματα κισσύβια φωνέεται {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 1.}}
**II.2** φ. τινα with _infinitive_, **command,** σὲ φωνῶ νεκρὸν.. μὴ συγκομίζειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1047.}}
**II.3** **invite,** τοὺς φίλους {{NT|NT.Luke.14.12.}}
**II.4** with _dative_ _person_, **call to, cry to,** Ζεῦ ἄνα, σοὶ φωνῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1485 (Lyric poetry), compare “OT” 1121; ἕρποντι φωνεῖς [prev. author] “Aj.” 543.}}
**III** with _accusative_ _of things_, **speak** or **tell of,** προσβολὰς Ἐρινύων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 283}}; ὁδοῦ τέλος.. οἷον οὐδὲ φωνῆσαί τινι ἔξεσθ᾽ **tell** to any one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1402}}; φ. τὸ Ἐπιχάρμειον **recite** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 366c.}}

5456:
** φων-ή**, ἡ,
**sound, tone,** properly, **the sound of the voice,** whether of men or animals with lungs and throat (ἡ φωνὴ ψόφος τίς ἐστιν ἐμψύχου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 420b5, compare 29, “HA” 535a27, “PA” 664b1)}}; opposed to φθόγγος (see. φθόγγος {{Refs|11):}}
**I** mostly of human beings, **speech, voice, utterance,** φ. ἄρρηκτος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.490; ἀτειρέα φ. 17.555}}; φ. δέ οἱ αἰθέρ᾽ ἵκανεν, of Ajax' battle-**cry,** {{Refs|15.686}}; of the battle-**cry** of an army, Τρώων καὶ Ἀχαιῶν.. φ. δεινὸν ἀϋσάντων {{Refs|14.400}}: _plural_, of the **cries** of market-people, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.3; ὁ τόνος τῆς φ. [prev. author] “Cyn.” 6.20, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.280, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.209}}; ὀξεῖα, βαρυτέρα, λεία, τραχεῖα φ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 67b; φ. μαλακή 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 979 (_anapaest meter_)}}; μιαρά, ἀναιδής, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 218, 638: with Verbs, φωνὴν ῥῆξαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 357 (_anapaest meter_); φ. ἱέναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2, 4.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 259d, etc.; φ. ἥσει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1295; προΐεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.23; ἀρθροῦν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.12; διαρθρώσασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 322a; ἐντείνασθαι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.157; φ. ἐπαρεῖ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.336; φωνῇ}}**with his voice, aloud,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.161, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.29; εἶπε τῇ φωνῇ τὰ ἀπόρρητα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.51; διὰ ζώσης φωνῆς Anonymus “Geog.Epit.” 1p.488M.}}; μιᾷ φ. **with** one **voice,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 14}}; ἀπὸ φωνῆς, with _genitive_, **dictated** by.., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “in Thd.” 1.103 at Title Page, 5th-6th c.AD: Marinus Biographus}} **in Euc.Dat.**p.234 M., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Platonis Gorgiam commentaria - Neue Jahrbücher für Philologie, Suppl.” p.1 N., Palladius Medicus “in Hippocratem” 2.1 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator: _plural_, αἱ φ.}} the **notes of the voice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 474e; σχήμασι καὶ φωναῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1306a32: proverbial, φωνῇ ὁρᾶν, of a blind man, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 138 (_anapaest meter_)}}; πᾶσαν, τὸ λεγόμενον, φ. ἱέντα, i.e. using every effort, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 890d, compare “Euthd.” 293a; πάσας ἀφιέναι φωνάς [prev. author] “R.” 475a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.195; φωνὰς ἀπρεπεῖς προΐεντο “PTeb.” 802.15 (2nd c.BC).}}
**I.2** the **cry** of animals, as of swine, dogs, oxen, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.239, 12.86, 396}}; of asses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.129}}; of the nightingale, **song,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.521; ἄνθρωπος πολλὰς φωνὰς ἀφίησι, τὰ δὲ ἄλλα μίαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 895a4.}}
**I.3** **any articulate sound,** opposed to inarticulate noise (ψόφος), φ. κωκυμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1206; ὥσπερ φωνῆς οὔσης κατὰ τὸν ἀέρα πολλάκις καὶ λόγου ἐν τῇ φωνῇ 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.4.12: στοιχεῖόν ἐστι φ. ἀδιαίρετος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1456b22}}; also especially of **vowelsound,** opposed to to that of consonants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 203b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 535a32}}; in literary criticism, of **sound,** opposed to meaning, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.20 (_plural_), 21.}}
**I.4** of **sounds** made by inanimate objects, mostly poetical, κερκίδος φ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 595; συρίγγων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 127 (Lyric poetry); αὐλῶν 4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 4.56 (_anapaest meter_)}}; rare in early Prose, ὀργάνων φωναί {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 397a; frequently in LXX, ἡ φ. τῆς σάλπιγγος LXX.Exo.20.18}}; φ. βροντῆς{{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.103(104).7; ἡ φ. αὐτοῦ ὡς φ. ὑδάτων πολλῶν NT.Rev.1.15.}}
**I.5** generally, **sound,** defined as ἀὴρ πεπληγμένος, πληγὴ ἀέρος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.21, Chrysipp.[prev. work]2.43.}}
**II** **faculty of speech, discourse,** εἰ φωνὴν λάβοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 548; παρέσχε φωνὴν τοῖς ἀφωνήτοις τινά [prev. author] “OC” 1283.}}
**II.2** **language,** hdt.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.114, 117; φ. ἀνθρωπηΐη [prev. author] 2.55; ἀγνῶτα φ. βάρβαρον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1051; φωνὴν ἥσομεν Παρνησίδα [prev. author] “Ch.” 563, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1397 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.5, 7.57, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 2.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17d, etc.; τῶν βαρβάρων πρὶν μαθεῖν τὴν φ. [prev. author] “Tht.” 163b; κατὰ τὴν Ἀττικὴν τὴν παλαιὰν φ. [prev. author] “Cra.” 398d, compare 409e.}}
**III** **phrase, saying,** τὴν Σιμωνίδου φ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Prt.” 341b; ἡ τοῦ Σωκράτους φ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.106b, compare 330f}}; of **formulae,** στοιχειώματα καὶ φ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.4U., compare “Sent.Vat.” 41 (= 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “fragment” 59); αἱ σκεπτικαὶ φ. 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.14, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.162b, etc.}}
**IV** **report, rumour,** {{LXX|LXX.Gen.45.16.}}
**IV.b** **message,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Sammelb. 7252.21 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**V** **loud talk, bragging,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 45.}}

5458:
** φωστήρ**, ῆρος, ὁ,
**that which gives light,** οἱ φ. the **lights of heaven, stars,** {{LXX+4th c.BC+|LXX.Gen.14, +others, 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Epictetum commentaria” p.72 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; οἱ δύο φ., i.e. sun and moon, {{NT+5th c.AD+|[prev. passage],compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 4.72, etc.; φαίνεσθε ὡς φωστῆρες ἐν κόσμῳ NT.Phil.2.15.}}
**2** **splendour, radiance,** ὁ φ. αὐτῆς ὅμοιος λίθῳ τιμιωτάτῳ {{NT|NT.Rev.21.11.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, of a king, τῷ φ. τῷ ἡμετέρῳ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 16.204c.}}
**4** of the **eyes,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 110.22.}}
**II** **opening for light, door** or **window,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

5459:
** φωσφόρ-ος**
(_paroxytone_), ον, _poetry_ **φαοσφόρος** Lyric poetry Adesp. in {{Refs|“PLit.Lond.” 51.5}}, **φαεσφόρος** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 204, etc.}}:— **bringing** or **giving light,** Ἕως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1157}}; φ. ἀστήρ, of Dionysus at the mysteries, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 342 (Lyric poetry); φ. πεῦκαι [prev. author] “fragment” 599}}; αἴγλη, Ἦμαρ, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 1246, Εὐχή 24.}}
**b** _substantive_, ὁ φ. (i.e. ἀστήρ), **the light-bringer,** i.e. **the morning-star,** a name specially given to the planet Venus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 96e, 97a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 392a27, 399a8, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 2.20.53, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.504, compare 1st c.BC: Alexander Ephesius Epicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Theon Smyrnaeus Philosophus p.138H.}}
**2** of the eye, φ. ὄμματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 45b}}; φ. κόραι, of the Cyclops, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 611 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2.b** name of an **eye-salve,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.747.}}
**II** **torch-bearing,** epithet of certain deities, especially of Hecate, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 569, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 858, “fragment” 594a}}; φ. θεά (i.e. Ἄρτεμις) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 21, cf. Call.[same place]; νὴ τὴν Φωσφόρον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 443, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 58.6}}; of Hephaestus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 66.3: _plural_, ἱερεὺς Φωσφόρων “Hesperia” 4.49 (Athens, 2nd c.AD).}}
**III** φωσφόρος, ἡ, **torch-bearer,** title of a priestess, Κλεοπάτρας θεᾶς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PRein.” 10.8, etc. (2nd c.BC).}}

5460:
** φωτεινός**, ή, όν,
(φῶς) **shining, bright,** ἥλιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.4}}; σκοτεινὰ καὶ φ. [σώματα{{LXX+1st c.AD+|] [prev. work] 3.10.1, cf. LXX.Sir.17.31 (_comparative_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2. 1110b, 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 26p.478M.; ἀήρ 1st c.AD: Athenaeus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 9.5.2; οἴκημα 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.10, +others}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **clear, distinct,** λόγος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.9b (_comparative_, assuming variant)}}; also in moral sense, {{NT+5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 3p.424M.; ὅταν ὁ ὀφθαλμός σου ἁπλοῦς ᾖ, καὶ ὅλον τὸ σῶμά σου φ. ἐστιν NT.Luke.11.34, compare 36.}}

5461:
** φωτίζω**,
_future_ φωτίσω {{NT|NT.1Cor.4.5}}:—I. _absolutely_, **shine, give light,** ὁ ἄνθραξ [οὐ δύναται] φωτίζειν ὥσπερ ἡ φλόξ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 30, compare 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 74.66.}}
**II** _transitive_, **illuminate,** ὁ ἥλιος φ. τὸν κόσμον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.48, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.931b (_passive_):}} —_passive_, τὸ φωτιζόμενον, opposed to τὸ φωτίζον,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]936b: opposed to σκοτίζομαι, [prev. work]1120e,compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Luct.” 2, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.3.5}}; of a planet, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 11(2).110; πεφωτισμέναι ἡμέραι 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 272.}}
**II.2** **bring to light, make known,** τὴν ἑκατέρων αἵρεσιν {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 22.5.10, compare 28.13.10; τὰ κρυπτὰ τοῦ σκότους NT.1Cor.1.c., cf. NT.2Tim.1.10}}:—_passive_, γράμματα ἑαλωκότα καὶ πεφωτισμένα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.8.1; φωτισθέντος τοῦ θανάτου [prev. author] 15.25.8.}}
**II.3** **enlighten, instruct, teach,** φ. τινάς, πῶς.. {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Ki.17.28; φ. πάντας, τίς ἡ οἰκονομία NT.Eph.3.9.}}
**II.4** **illuminate with spiritual light,** ὁ ὑπὸ τοῦ θεοῦ πεφωτισμένος {{Refs|“Corpus Hermeticum” 9.3, compare 13.18 (_passive_).}}
**II.4.b** in a special sense, **baptize,** in _passive_, {{NT|NT.Heb.6.4, 10.32.}}
**II.5** **throw light upon, illustrate,** of commentators, Ὅμηρον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.57.}}

5462:
** φωτ-ισμός**, ὁ,
**illumination, light,** Diocl(?).cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.530, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.929e, 931b, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.224: _plural_, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 23; σελήνης 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 28.7, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.167d; [ἡμισφαιρίων] [prev. work] 4.147b.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **light,** Κύριος φ. μου {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.26(27).1, cf. NT.2Cor.4.4, 6.}}

5463:
** χαίρω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.191, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _imperative_ χαιρόντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 575}}: _imperfect_, _Epic dialect_ χαῖρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.156}}, _Ionic dialect_ χαίρεσκον {{Refs|18.259}}: _future_ χαιρήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|20.363, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.128, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 64, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.101, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 5.20.6}}; _Epic dialect_ _reduplicate_ _infinitive_ κεχᾰρησέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.98}}; later χᾰρῶ variant in {{NT|NT.Rev.11.10}}: _aorist_ ἐχαίρησα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 25}}: _perfect_ κεχάρηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 764}}, _participle_ **-ηκώς**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, etc., _Epic dialect_ _accusative_ κεχᾰρηότα, _plural_ -ότας, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.312, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 77:}} —_middle_ (in same sense), χαίρομαι, noted as a barbarism in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 291 (see. Scholia), but found in “BCH” 36.622 (Perinthus, written χέρ-), 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 1.20, +others:}}_future_ χᾰρήσομαι Ps.-{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philopatr.” 24, (συγ-) 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.18.1, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 31.15}}; _Doric dialect_ χαρησοῦμαι {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus “Ep.” 3.7; χᾰροῦμαι LXX Zech.4.10, (κατα-)LXX.Pro.1.26}}; _Epic dialect_ κεχᾰρήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.266}}: _aorist 1_ _participle_ χαιρησάμενος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 742 ii 3 (2nd c.AD)}}: _Epic dialect_ _aorist 1_ χήρατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.270; ἐχ- 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.509, etc.}}; _participle_ χηράμενος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.198 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}: _Epic dialect_ _reduplicate_ _aorist_ 2, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ κεχάροντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.600 (χάροντο 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 6.315)}}; _optative_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ and _plural_ κεχάροιτο, -οίατο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.249, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.256:}} —_passive_ (in same sense), _aorist 2_ ἐχάρην [ᾰ] {{Refs|7.54}}, etc., _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ χάρη {{Refs|5.682, 13.609}}; _subjunctive_ χᾰρῇς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 606c; _optative_ χᾰρείη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.481}}; _infinitive_ χᾰρῆναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 164}}; _participle_ χᾰρείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.541, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 118, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).10, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 981 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; _perfect_ κεχάρημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Bacchum” 7.10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 200 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 389 (_anapaest meter_)}}; _participle_ κεχαρμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1122, “Tr.” 529 (Lyric poetry), “Cyc.” 367 (Lyric poetry):}}_pluperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ and _plural_ κεχάρητο, -ηντο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 65, “hymnus ad Cererem” 458}}:—**rejoice, be glad,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.111, 21.347, etc.; γραῦς ἥδε οἰνοφόρος κεχαρημένη ὧδε κάθηται “IG” 12(8).679 (Scyros, 2nd c.BC): χ. θυμῷ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.191, +others; ἐν θυμῷ 24.491, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.411; φρεσὶν ᾗσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.609; φρένα 6.481}}; χ. νόῳ **to rejoice** in wardly, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.78; χαίρει δέ μοι ἦτορ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.647; αὐτὰρ ἐμὸν κῆρ χ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.260; χ. καὶ γελᾶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1300; ἥδομαι καὶ χαίρομαι κεὐφραίνομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 291}}; opposed to λυπεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 266.3, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 555, etc.}}; opposed to ἀλγεῖν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1119.}} —Constr.,
**1** with _dative_ _of things_, **rejoice at, take pleasure in** a thing, νίκῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.312; φήμῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.35; δώρῳ 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 358; μόλπᾳ 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 25.5, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1070, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 238d, etc.: with _dative_ _person_, χαῖρε.. ἀνδρὶ δικαίῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.52}}; with a _participle_ added, χάρη δ᾽ ἄρα οἱ προσιόντι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.682, compare 24.706, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.463: with Preps., χαίρειν ἐπί τινι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 926, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.35, “Cyr.” 8.4.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 739d, etc.; πρὸς τοῖς παιδικοῖς 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 327}}; with a _participle_ added, ἐπ᾽ ἐξεργασμένοις κακοῖσι χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1040, compare 1033: rarely ἔν τινι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 996 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1119: also with _dative_ modi, χ. γέλωτι}} **express one's joy** by laughter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.33.}}
**1.b** of a plant, χαίρει ὑφάμμοις χωρίοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.5.2; also ἡ κύστις χ. τῇ χολῇ 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.646.}}
**2** rarely with _accusative_, with a _participle_ added, χαίρω δέ σ᾽ εὐτυχοῦντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 390; τοὺς γὰρ εὐσεβεῖς θεοὶ θνῄσκοντας οὐ χ. [prev. author] “Hipp.” 1340}}; χαίρω σ᾽ [ἐλθόντα] {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 673}} (this usage is said to be Oropian, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 808.4).}}
**2.b** with a _neuter_ _adjective_, ταὐτὰ λυπεῖσθαι καὶ ταὐτὰ χαίρειν τοῖς πολλοῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.292: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἁπλῆν χαίρειν ἡδονήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1154b26; χ. ἀνδραπόδων τινὰ χαράν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1091e.}}
**3** with _participle_, χαίρω.. τὸν μῦθον ἀκούσας **I rejoice at** having heard, **am glad to** hear, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.185, compare 7.54, 11.73; χαίρουσιν βίοτον νήποινον ἔδοντες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.377, compare 12.380, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 55; χαίρω.. κόμπον ἱείς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.49; χαίρεις ὁρῶν φῶς, πατέρα δ᾽ οὐ χαίρειν δοκεῖ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 691; χαίρω φειδόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 247; θωπευόμενος χαίρεις [prev. author] “Eq.” 1116 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 191e, etc.}}
**3.b** with _participle_ _present_, **delight in** doing, **to be wont** to do, χρεώμενοι χαίρουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.236, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 449, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 764, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 318d, 346c, 358a.}}
**4** χαίρειν ὅττι or ὅτι.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.51, 526, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.46}}; ἐχάρην καὶ ἐθρασυνάμην ὅτι ἔμαθον.. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “fragment” 42}}; χ. οὕνεκα.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.200.}}
**II** with _negative_, especially with _future_, οὐ χαιρήσεις **thou wilt** or **shalt** not **rejoice,** i.e. **thou shalt** not **go unpunished, shalt repent** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 64; οὐ χαιρήσετον [prev. author] “Eq.” 235; so οὐδέ τιν᾽ οἴω Τρώων χαιρήσειν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.363, compare 15.98, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.249, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 186}}; ἀλλ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ὣς Κῦρός γε χαιρήσει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.128}}; with an _interrogative_, σὺ.. χαιρήσειν νομίζει; {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 51}}: rarely with other tenses, ὅπως ἂν μὴ χαίρωσιν. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.299; οὐκ ἐχαίρησεν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 25: for a similar use of the}}_participle_, see below {{Refs|IV. 2.}}
**III** frequently in _imperative_ χαῖρε, dual χαίρετον, _plural_ χαίρετε, as a form of greeting,
**III.1** at meeting, **hail, welcome** (especially in the morning, _accusative_ to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 69.18, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Laps.”), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.197, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.229, etc.; χαῖρε, ξεῖνε, παρ᾽ ἄμμι φιλήσεαι 1.123; strengthened, οὖλέ τε, καὶ μάλα χαῖρε, θεοὶ δέ τοι ὄλβια δοῖεν 24.402; χαῖρέ μοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.19, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1137; repeated, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 996, 1014 (both Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 91, etc.; χαῖρ᾽ ὡς μέγιστα, χαῖρε [prev. author] “Ph.” 462}}; in greeting one's native land, the sun, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 508, 22, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1453 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**III.1.b** sometimes implied in the use of χαίρω, κῆρυξ Ἀχαιῶν, χαῖρε.. Answ. χαίρω I **accept the greeting,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 538}}; νῦν πᾶσι χαίρω, νῦν με πᾶς ἀσπάζεται I **hear the word** χαῖρε from all, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 596: so in}}_infinitive_, τὸ χαίρειν uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 164e}}; χαίρειν δὲ τὸν κήρυκα προὐννέπω I bid him **welcome,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 227; προσειπών τινα χ. οὐκ ἀντιπροσερρήθη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.13.1}}; but χαίρειν τἄλλ᾽ ἐγώ σ᾽ ἐφίεμαι I bid thee **have thy pleasure,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 112.}}
**III.1.c** _infinitive_ alone at the beginning of letters, Κῦρος Κυαζάρῃ χαίρειν (i.e. λέγει) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.27, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.1}}; used by Alexander the Great to Phocion as a mark of respect, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Duris Epigrammaticus 51J.}}
**III.2** at leavetaking, **fare-thee-well,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.205, 13.59, 15.151; χαῖρε πόλλ᾽ ὦδελφέ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 164; _plural_, χαίρετε πολλάκι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.144}}; frequently put into the mouth of the dying, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 863, “Tr.” 921, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116d, etc.}}: hence in sepulchral inscriptions, {{Refs|“IG” 7.203, etc.}}
**III.2.b** hence, _imperative_ χαιρέτω, χαιρόντων, **have done with.., away with**.., εἴτ᾽ ἐγένετο ἄνθρωπος εἴτ᾽ ἐστὶ δαίμων, χαιρέτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.96; χαιρέτω βουλεύματα τὰ πρόσθεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1044, χαιρόντων πόνοι [prev. author] “HF” 575; compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 199a, “Lg.” 636d, 886d.}}
**III.2.c** ἐᾶν χαίρειν τινά or τι **dismiss from one's mind, put away, renounce,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.23, 9.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1187, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 63e, “Prt.” 348a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.23, etc.; συχνὰ χ. ἐᾶν τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 59b; ἐλευθερίαν μακρὰ χ. ἐᾶν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Apol.” 3; μακρὰ χ. εἰποῦσα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 12.1; πόλλα μοι τὰν Πωλυανάκτιδα παῖδα χαίρην 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 86; τὴν Κύπριν πόλλ᾽ ἐγὼ χαίρειν λέγω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 113, compare 1059, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 188a; χ. κελεύων πολλὰ τοὺς Ἀχαρνέας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 200; εἰπεῖν χαίρειν τινά “Ath.Mitt.” 56.131 (Milet., Hellenistic), compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 50; χαίρειν προσαγορεύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 322 (_metaphorically_ in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 771a); χαίρειν προσειπεῖν 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 308: less frequently with _dative_ _person_ (never with ἐᾶν χ.), πολλὰ χαίρειν ξυμφοραῖς καταξιῶ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 572 (nisi to be read ξυμφοράς); φράσαι.. χαίρειν Ἀθηναίοισι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 609 (troch.); πολλὰ εἰπόντα χ. τῷ ἀληθεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 272e, compare “Phd.” 64c, “R.” 406d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.31 (codices, perhaps ἀλλήλους), 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator}}**ad Them.**255a.
**III.3** on other occasions, as in comforting, **be of good cheer,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.408; at meals, 4.60, 18.122}}; χαῖρε, γύναι, φιλότητι **good luck be** on our union,{{Refs|11.248; εὐχωλῇς χαίρετε 13.358: χαῖρε ἀοιδῇ “Hymni Homerici” 9.7.}}
**IV** _participle_ χαίρων **glad, joyful,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.446, etc.; χαίροντα φίλην ἐς πατρίδ᾽ ἔπεμπον εἰς Ἰθάκην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.461; χαίροντι φέρειν.. χαίρων 17.83}}; λυπούμενοι καὶ χαίροντες in sorrow and **in joy,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1356a16}}: also _perfect_ _participle_ κεχαρηκώς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.27, 42, etc.}}
**IV.2** joined with another Verb, **safe and sound, with impunity,** χαίροντα ἀπαλλάσσειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 69, compare 9.106, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.153}}; more frequently with a _negative_, οὐ χαίρων **to one's cost,** οὐ χαίροντες γέλωτα ἐμὲ θήσεσθε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.29; οὔ τι χαίρων.. ἐρεῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 363, compare “Ant.” 759, “Ph.” 1299, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 398, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 563, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 510d; οὐ γὰπ.. χαίρων τις.. τοὐμὸν ἀλγυνεῖ κέαρ 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 90; οὔτε χαίροντες ἂν ἀπαλλάζαιτε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.32; also οὔτι χαιρήσων γε σύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 186; compare above 11.}}
**IV.3** in the same sense as _imperative_ (above {{Refs|111), σὺ δέ μοι χαίρων ἀφίκοιο}}**fare**-thee-**well,** and may'st thou arrive, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.128, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.163}}; χαίροισ᾽ ἔρχεο go thy way **rejoicing,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.7}}; ἀλλ᾽ ἑρπέτω χαίρουσα let her go **with a benison,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 819}}; χαίρων ἴθι **fare**-thee-**well,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 813, “Ph.” 921; χαίρουσα.. στεῖχε [prev. author] “Hipp.” 1440.}}
**IV.4** τὸ χαῖρον τῆς ψυχῆς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.136c, 1089e.}}
**V** Astrology texts, of a planet, occupy the position appropriate to another of its own αἵρεσις, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Serapio Astrologus in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).230, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 51, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 63.6, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.348}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _háryati_ 'take pleasure in', Umbr. **heriest** 'will wish', Latin _horior._)

5464:
** χάλαζ-α** [χᾰ], ης, ἡ,
**hail** {{NT+8th c.BC+|compare (5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 59e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 394b1), ὄμβρον.. ἠὲ χάλαζαν ἢ νιφετόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.6, compare 15.170, NT.Rev.8.7, etc.: _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 5.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 188b, “R.” 397a; χ. στρογγύλαι}}**hailstones,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1127 (troch.); ἀπὸ τῶν χαλαζῶν.. ἄπαγε σεαυτόν [prev. author] “Ra.” 852}}: _metaphorically_, **any pelting shower,** ὀμβρία χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1503; χ. αἵματος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).27.}}
**II** **any small knot like a hailstone,**
**II.1** **pimple** or **tubercle** in the flesh of swine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 603b18, “Pr.” 963b34, Androsth. cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.93c.}}
**II.2** **small cyst,** such as grows on the eyelid, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.437, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.198, etc.}}
**II.3** **a knot** or **hard lump;** in an egg, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 560a28; in coal, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 25; in ivory, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 2.13; λίθοι χαλάζης}}**crystals,** {{Refs|1 “Enoch” 14.9,10}}. (Cf. Sloven. **žled,** Little Russian **oželeda** 'sleet, ice'.)

5465:
** χᾰλάω**,
{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 219, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ χαλόωσιν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 2.451}}; _Aeolic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ χόλαισι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.9 codices 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus:}}_future_ χᾰλάσω [λᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 8, “Epid.” 7.80}}: _aorist_ ἐχάλᾰσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 177 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.23, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ χάλασσα {{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 6}}, _plural_ _subjunctive_ χαλάσσομεν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 23.10}}; _Doric dialect_ _participle_ χᾰλάξαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.6}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _future_ or _aorist_ _subjunctive_ χαλάξει (uncertain meaning) {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.164 (from Cyrene)}}:—_middle_, _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ χαλάσαντο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1264:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ ἐχαλάσθην, _subjunctive_ χᾰλασθῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 991, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 86c}}: _perfect_ κεχάλασμαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.297 (Antip.), 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μιθριδάτειος” 74, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.3.16}}: _pluperfect_ ἐκεχάλαστο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.315J.}}
**I** _transitive_, **slacken, loosen,** χ. βιόν, τόξα, **unstring** the bow, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 6, “Hymni Homerici” 27.12}}; χ. τὰ νεῦρα, opposed to συντείνειν, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 98d; χ. τὸν πόδα, of a ship, see at {πούς} II.2: _metaphorically_, τὰ τῆς πολιτείας χ., opposed to ἐπιτείνειν, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.827b:}}] —_passive_, opposed to ἐπιτείνεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 86c, 94c; χαλᾶσθαι καὶ διαφθείρεσθαι [prev. author] “Lg.” 653c; χαλᾶσθαι ὑπὸ τῆς ἡδονῆς 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 7.}}
**I.2** **let down, let fall,** πτέρυγα χαλάξαις Pi.[same place]; χαλάσας ὀλίγον τὸ μέτωπον **having unbent** the brow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 655 (_anapaest meter_)}}; μαστοὺς χάλασον, says the Cyclops to his ewe, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 55 (Lyric poetry)}}; κράββατον, δίκτυα χ., {{NT|NT.Mark.2.4, NT.Luke.5.5; τὴν ἱερὰν ἄγκυραν 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}; **dip** in a liquid, εἰς αἷμα {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.2886}}; **soak,** {{Refs|PHolm. 14.33:}} —_middle_, ἱστὸν χαλάσαντο **lowered** it, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1264.}}
**I.3** **let loose, release,** τινὰ ἐκ δεσμῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 177 (_anapaest meter_)}}; _absolutely_, **let go, slacken one's hold,** μηδαμῇ χάλα {{Refs|[prev. work] 58.}}
**I.4** ἡνίας χ. **slacken** the reins, especially in _metaphorically_ sense, χ. τὰς ἡνίας τοῖς λόγοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338a, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 409.}}
**I.5** κλῇθρα χ. **loose** the bars or bolts, i.e. **undo** or **open** the door, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1187, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 808; κλῇδας [prev. author] “Med.” 1314; χ. τοὺς μοχλούς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 310; but also πύλας μοχλοῖς χαλᾶτε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 879.}}
**I.6** **loosen** or **undo** things drawn tightly together, χ. κρεμαστὴν ἀρτάνην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1266; χ. πᾶν κάλυμμ᾽ ἀπ᾽ ὀφθαλμῶν [prev. author] “El.” 1468; χ. δεσμά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 577; ἀσκόν [prev. author] “Cyc.” 161; τὸ στόμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 6.8}}:—_passive_, τὰ χαλώμενα ὅπλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 43; πρὶν ἂν χαλασθῇ δεσμά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 991.}}
**I.7** of the bowels, etc., ὑγρὰ χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.99, compare “Coac.” 20; ἢν αἱ μῆτραι μὴ χαλάσωσι τὰ ἐπιμήνια [prev. author] “Mul.” 1.61.}}
**I.8** _metaphorically_, τὴν ὀργήν χ. **let** it **go,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 727 (_anapaest meter_); χ. [τὸν νόον] ἐς ὄψιν τινός 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 104c; χ. ἐπιθυμίαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.133a; τὸ βαρὺ καὶ ἀμειδές 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.3}}; **remit,** μήτε τῆς προνοίας χαλώσης τὴν.. ὑπεροχήν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 122}}; τὸ ἀεὶ ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχειν ἐχάλασαν **relaxed** the strict **principle** that.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 242e}}:—_passive_, **to be softened,** λίθος εἰς ὑγρότητα κεχαλασμένος {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Callistratus Sophista “Statuarum descriptiones” 5}}; also κεχαλάσθαι εἰς τὸ αὐτεξούσιον **to have free play,** opposed to συντετάχθαι, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.3.16.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **become slack** or **loose,** opposed to συντείνω, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 98d; χόλαισιν ἄγκυρραι 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.9 (assuming variant); ζῶναι χαλῶσι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 935}}; πύλαι χαλῷσαι **open** gates, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.29}}: _metaphorically_ with _genitive_, **have a remission of,** χαλάσσομεν τὰς θυμοβόρω λύας {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 23.10; τί χαλᾷ μανιῶ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1057 (_anapaest meter_); (also _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 203 (Lyric poetry), 840)}}; **relax,** φρονήματος χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 716; τῆς ὀργῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 383 (troch.); [τὸ ὂν] χαλάσαν τῆς τοῦ ἑνὸς ἁπλότητος 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 13.}}
**II.2** with _dative_ _person_, χ. τινι **give way** or **yield to** any one, **be indulgent to** him, εἰ τοῖσιν.. κτείνουσιν ἀλλήλους χαλᾷς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 219; χάλα τοκεῦσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 403; with _genitive_ add., μοι τῆς ἀρχῆς χάλασον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 86e, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 7}}: _absolutely_, **give way,** εἴκειν ὁδοῦ χαλῶντα τοῖς κακίοσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 637.}}
**II.3** _absolutely_, **grow slack** or **weak,** ἐπειδὰν αἱ ἐπιθυμίαι παύσωνται κατατείνουσαι καὶ χαλάσωσι {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 329c}}; **abate,** χαλάσει ὁ παγετός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 8; ὀδύνη [prev. author] “Acut.” 16.}}

5466:
** χαλδ-αῖος**, ὁ,
**Chaldaean,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.155, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 638, etc.}}
**II** **astrologer,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 35, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Div.” 1.1.2, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.42S., 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 2.5}} (since the Chaldaeans were given to astrology,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.181, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.17.1, etc.)}}:—**Χαλδαία** (i.e. γῆ), ἡ, **Chaldaea,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Stephanus Byzantius, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Geographia” 5.19}}:— **Χαλδαϊκός**, ή, όν, **Chaldaean,** γράμματα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.529f}}; ἱστορίαι, title of work by Berosus, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 10.11.1, compare “Ap.” 1.20}}; μέθοδος, i.e. astrology, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.43 (so -κή alone, [prev. work] 45); ἐπιτηδεύματα 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus cited in 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} see entry {Χαλδαϊκοῖς}: _comparative_ **-ώτερος**{{Refs|[prev. author] “Pr.” 206.}} _adverb_ -κῶς{{Refs|[prev. work] 183}}— **Χαλδαϊστί**, _adverb_ **in the Chaldaean language,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Dan.2.26, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.546.}}

5467:
** χαλεπ-ός**, ή, όν,
**difficult** (ὃ ἂν μὴ ῥᾴδιον ᾖ ἀλλὰ διὰ πολλῶν πραγμάτων γίγνηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 341d: opposed to ῥᾴδιος, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1363a24, in various relations):}}
**I** in reference to the feelings, **hard to bear, painful, grievous** (so frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus), κεραυνός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.417; θύελλα 21.335; ἄνεμοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.286; πόνος 23.250; ἄλγος, πένθος, 2.193, 6.169; γῆρας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.103; ἄλη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.464; χαλεπώτερος ἄεθλος 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 800}}; ἄλλα τῶν κατεχόντων πρηγμάτων - ώτερα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.40; χ. πνεῦμα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 166 (Lyric poetry); δύα [prev. author] “Th.” 228 (Lyric poetry)}}; χαλεπώτατα [πράγματα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1273 (_anapaest meter_); συμφορά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 768 (Lyric poetry)}}; νόσος, πλάνη, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.37, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 245e (_comparative_), etc.}}; ἡ ἐσβολὴ αὕτη -ωτάτη τοῖς Ἀθηναίοις ἐγένετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.26}}; [θώρακες] δύσφοροι καὶ χ., of ill-fitting cuirasses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.13: τὸ χ. τοῦ πνεύματος}} the **severity** of the wind, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 4.5.4}}; τὰ χ. **hardships,** opposed to τὰ τερπνά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 2.1.23, etc.; τερπνῶν χαλεπῶν τε κρίσις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 131, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.9.14}}: _comparative_, **more unpleasant,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.202c.}}
**I.2** **hard to do** or **deal with, difficult, irksome,** -ώτατον ἔργον ἁπάντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 516 (_anapaest meter_); compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59 (_superlative_), etc.}}; χαλεπὰ τὰ καλά proverbial cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 304e, +others}}, attributed to Solon by Scholia (at [prev. work]); χαλεπὸν ὁ βίος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 299e: with}}_infinitive_ _active_ or _middle_, χαλεπή τοι ἐγὼ μένος ἀντιφέρεσθαι, = {χαλεπόν ἐστί μοι ἀντιφέρεσθαί σοι}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.482; χαλεποὶ δὲ θεοὶ φαίνεσθαι ἐναργεῖς 20.131}}; χαλεπὸν δέ τ᾽ ὀρύσσειν [τὸ μῶλυ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.305; χ. προϊδέσθαι καπρός 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 386; χ. ἔρις ἀνθρώποις ὁμιλεῖν κρεσσόνων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.72; χ. προσπολεμεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.138, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.51 (_comparative_)}}; χ. συγγενέσθαι, εὑρεῖν, γενέσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c, 412b, 502c; χ. πάσχειν [prev. author] “Cri.” 49b (_comparative_)}}: also with _infinitive_ _passive_, χαλεπὸς διαγνωσθῆναι καὶ δειχθῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.1, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.94, etc.; χαλεπὸν ληφθῆναι ὁ τόπος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 212a8}}; χαλεπόν [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, 'tis **hard, difficult** to do, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.651}}; with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, 'tis **difficult for** one to do.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.620, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.313}}: with _dative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.184, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.156.}}
**I.3** **dangerous,** λιμένες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|19.189; θάλασσα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.24; χ. τὰ παρόντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.2.}}
**I.4** of ground, **difficult, rugged,** χωρία χ. καὶ πετρώδη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.9; ὁδός [prev. author] 5.58, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328e; χ... καὶ προσάντης.. ὁδός ἐστιν 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 56; πρόσοδοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.3; πορεία [prev. work] 5.6.10; σταθμός [prev. work] 4.5.3}}; χωρίον -ώτατον a place **most difficult to take,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.8.2.}}
**II** of persons, **hard to deal with, cruel, harsh, stern** (opposed to πρᾷος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 493b (_superlative_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1126a26), βασιλεύς, δαίμων, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.232, 19.201; χαλεποί τε καὶ ἄγριοι 8.575}}; **-ώτερος** **a more bitter enemy,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40}}; -ώτατοι **most difficult to deal with, most dangerous** or **troublesome,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 42, compare 7.21; -ώτεροι πάροικοι [prev. author] 3.113; χαλεπόν γε θυγάτηρ κτῆμα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 18}}: with _dative_ _person_, **cruel** or **harsh** towards one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.388; τοῖς ξυμπροθυμηθεῖσι τὸν ἔκπλουν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.1, etc.}}; πρὸς τοὺς πολεμίους, τοὺς ἀγνῶτας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 375c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1328a8 (also πρὸς τοὺς δρόμους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.17); ἐπὶ νύμφαις ἀλλοτρίαις 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.145.}}
**II.b** of words, χαλεπῷ ἠνίπαπε μύθῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.245, etc.; ἐρεθιζέμεν αἰεὶ μύθοισιν χ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.395}}; χ. ὀνείδεα, ὁμοκλαί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.438, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.189; φῆμις 14.239; μῆνις 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.178.}}
**II.c** especially of judges, ἦν τὸ δίκαιον φυλάσσων χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.100, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 107d, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.36; also χ. ἀρχή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77; τιμωρία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 39c (_comparative_)}}; νόμοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hp.Mi.” 372a (_comparative_), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.44, 35.50.}}
**II.d** **savage, fierce,** κύνες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.24, “Cyn.” 10.23}}; of bees, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 624b30 (_comparative_)}}; [θηρία] χ. τὰς φύσεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 274b.}}
**II.2** **ill-tempered, testy,** χ. ὢν καὶ δύσκολος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 942, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.26; ὀργὴν χ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.131}}; χαλεπῇ τῇ χειρί with a **rough** hand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1116.}}
**II.3** of plants, **hurtful to the soil,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.9.3 (_superlative_).}}
**B** _adverb_ -πῶς **hardly, with difficulty,** διαγνῶναι χ. ἦν ἄνδρα ἕκαστον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.424; χ. δέ σ᾽ ἔολπα τὸ ῥέξειν 20.186; χ. κε φύγοις κακόν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 684; χ. ὀργὰς μεταβάλλουσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 121 (_anapaest meter_); χ. γνῶναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.1; τὰ τοῖς ἄλλοις χ. εὑρημένα ῥᾳδίως μανθάνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.18, compare 44}} οὐ or μὴ χ. without **much ado,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2, 7.81, etc.}}
**B.2** **hardly, scarcely,** δοκέω.. χ. ἂν Ἕλληνας Πέρσῃσι μάχεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.103; χ. παρὰ τοῖς ἐχθροῖς εὑρεθήσεται 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 29.2; χ. ἂν πείσαιμι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84d.}}
**B.3** χ. ἔχει, ={χαλεπόν ἐστι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.53}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.6.}}
**B.4** **painfully, miserably,** -ώτερον ζῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 579d; ἐν τοῖς -ώτατα διῆγον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71.}}
**B.II** of persons, **angrily, cruelly, harshly,** χ. τιμωρεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.46; ἀποκρίνασθαι [prev. author] 5.42, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 203 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 60, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 269b}}; χ. φέρειν τι take it **ill,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330a, etc.}}; also χ. ἔφερον τῷ πολέμῳ, τοῖς πράγμασιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.29, “An.” 1.3.3; ἐπὶ τῇ πολιορκίᾳ [prev. author] “HG” 7.4.21, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.50; also χ. φέρειν τινός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.62; also χ. λαμβάνεσθαι τοῦ παιδός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. ; χ. λαμβάνειν περί τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.61}}; of the laws {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare(above 11.1c), χ. προστάττειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 925d.}}
**B.II.2** frequently in the phrase χ. ἔχειν to be **angry,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.16, etc.}}; τινι **with** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HG” 1.5.16; πρὸς τοὺς λόγους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.3, compare 51}} χ. ἔχειν τισὶν ἐπί τινι **with** persons for a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.135, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 43; χ. διακεῖσθαι πρὸς ἅπαντας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 7.5; χ. πρὸς φιλοσοφίαν διακεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 500b; χ. πρὸς ἡμᾶς διετέθησαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.79; ἐπί τινι χ. διατεθείς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 36.}}
**B.II.2.b** χ. ἔχειν, also, to be **in a bad way,** χ. ἔχω ὑπὸ τοῦ πότου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 176a, compare “Tht.” 142b.}}— Beside the regular _comparative_ χαλεπώτερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77, 7.50, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 94d, etc.)}} we have **-ωτέρως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.50, 8.40, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.7.1}}: _superlative_ **-ώτατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71, 8.95, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 579d, etc.}}

5469:
** χᾰλῑνός**
(_Aeolic dialect_ χάλιννος probably in {{Refs|“Etymologicum Gudianum” 561.5)}}, ὁ, irregular _plural_ χαλινά {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1607, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.191, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.613c, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.100, etc.}}:—**bit,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἐν δὲ χαλινοὺς γαμφηλῇς ἔβαλον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.393; χαλινὸν ἐμβαλεῖν γνάθοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 492; χ. ἐξαιρεῖται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 3.2}}; of the horse, χ. οὐκ ἐπίσταται φέρειν (_metaphorically_ of Cassandra) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1066}}; χ. δέχεσθαι, λαμβάνειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 3.2, 6.10}}; λαβεῖν, ἔχειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1393b16, 21}}; τὸν χ. ἐνδακεῖν champ the **bit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254d}}; of the rider, δοτέον τὸν χ. one must give a horse the **rein,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 10.12}}; ὀπίσω σπάσαι, ξυνέχειν ἀνάγκῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254e, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 25.1; [χ.] εἰς ἄκρον τὸ στόμα καθιέμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 6.9.}}—Expld. of the **bit,** opposed to reins (ἡνίαι), by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.148; so ἡνίας τε.. καὶ χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 601c; χ. τινα χαλκεῖ ἐκδιδόντα σκευάσαι [prev. author] “Prm.” 127a; κατὰ [τὸν κυνόδοντα] ἐμβάλλεται ὁ χ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 576b18, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 123 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1067 (Lyric poetry)}}; but distinguished from στόμιον by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.215, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 207 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 6.9, 10.9, etc.}}; and may be used generally for **bit and bridle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.118, 4.64, “IG” 12.374.176, “PCair.Zen.” 659.11 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, of **anything which curbs, restrains,** or **compels,** Ἀργοῦς χ., of an anchor, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.25}}; χ. λινόδετοι, ={χαλινωτήρια}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1043}}; παρθενίας χ. λύειν, of the virgin zone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).48}}; χαλινοῖς ἐν πετρίνοισιν, of Prometheus' bonds, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 562 (_anapaest meter_); Διὸς χ., of the will of Zeus, [prev. work] 672}}; χαλινῶν ἀναύδῳ μένει, of forcible constraint, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 238 (Lyric poetry)}}; πολλῶν χαλινῶν ἔργον οἰάκων θ᾽ ἅμα, i.e. it requires much skill and force to guide, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 869}}; τῷ δήμῳ χ. ἐμβαλεῖν ὕβρεως **a bridle to curb** their violence, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp. Per.Fab.” 1, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 82; τῆς γλώττης τὰ χ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.613c; ἐπέστω τῷ στόματι χ. 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 315.4; χ. οὐδεὶς ὀμμάτων 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.11.}}
**II** generally, **strap, thong,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 461 (dual).}}
**III** part of the **tackle of a ship,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1610.11,14.}}
**IV** **corner of the** horse's **mouth,** where the bit rests, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.90 (_plural_)}}; of the human mouth, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 117, 223, “PUniv.Giss.” 44.7, (2nd-1st c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in Orib 48.31.4, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.100, “PSI” 9.1016.25 (2nd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.9, 5th c.AD: Caelius Aurelianus Medicus “TP” 1.4, 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 8.27}}; but, ={ἡ σύνδεσις τῶν γνάθων}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 53, compare 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 8.40.}}
**IV.2** **fangs of serpents,** from their shape and position in the mouth, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 234.}}

5470:
** χάλκεος**, έα,
_Ionic dialect_ -έη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always -είη (see. χάλκειος))}}, εον (also εος, εον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.222 (ὄπα χάλκεον Αἰακίδαο, where 4th-3rd c.BC: Zenodotus Grammaticus χαλκέην as _disyllable_), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below): rarely in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 686, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 534.3, 7 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1}}; _Aeolic dialect_, _Doric dialect_ **χάλκιος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 79, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 15.3, “SIG” 945.6 (Assos, 4th c.BC), “IGRom.” 4.1302.35 (Cyme, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD)}}, also _Boeotian dialect_, compare χαλκοῦ; _Attic dialect_ **χαλκοῦς**, ῆ, οῦν {{Refs|(“IG” 12.313.55, etc.}}, but χαλκέων δέλτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 371a codices)}}; _Epic dialect_ also **χάλκειος**, see at {χάλκειος}: (χαλκός):—**of copper** or **bronze, brazen,** οὐδός, δόμος, τεῖχος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.15, 18.371, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.4}}; ἄξων, κύκλα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.30, 5.723; χ. Ἀράων θάλαμοι 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus in “PMilan.” 17.48; χ. καὶ ἀδαμαντίνοις τείχεσι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.84; ὀδός 4th c.BC: Astydamas Tragicus 9, 3rd c.BC: Ister Historicus 30}}; especially of arms and armour, ἔγχος, ξίφος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.317, 335; σάκος 7.220; θώρηξ, χιτών, 13.398, 440}}; ἔντεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|18.131, etc.; χαλκέοις ὅπλοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1359; also λέβητος χαλκέου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 686, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 392}}; χαλκέοισικάδοις, χαλκέοις δρεπάνοις, S.[same place]; in Trag. mostly _contraction_, χαλκοῖς βάθροισι {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 1591; χαλκῆς ὑπαὶ σάλπιγγος [prev. author] “El.” 711; χαλκῆς ἐκ δέλτου [prev. author] “Tr.” 683.}}
**b** of statues, χ. Ζεύς, χ. Ποσειδέων, a **bronze** statue of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.81; χ. ταῦρος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.95; ἡ χαλκῆ Ἀθηνᾶ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.272; ἱστάναι τινὰ χαλκοῦν [prev. author] 13.21; ἄξιος σταθῆναι χαλκοῦς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1410a33; στήλη ἐφ᾽ ᾗ ὁ στρατηγός ἐστιν ὁ χαλκοῦς 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.38}}; compare χαλκῆ.
**c** χ. ἀγών a contest **for a shield of brass,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.22.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **brazen,** i. e. **hard, stout, strong,** χάλκεος Ἄρης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.704, etc.}} (unless **wearing brazen armour,** compare χάλκεοι ἄνδρες Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.152)}}; Χαλκοῦς, nickname of Aristomedes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator cited in 1st c.BC: Didymus Grammaticus}}**in D.**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|9.57, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 1.2 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 11; χ. στονόεντ᾽ ὅμαδον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).27; χ. αὐδά [prev. author] “Pae.” 2.100}}; χάλκεον ἦτορ a heart **of brass,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.490; ὄπα χ. 18.222; χ. ὕπνος, i. e. the sleep of death, 11.241}}; χαλκέοισι νώτοις, of Atlas, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1.}}
**3** χαλκῆ μυῖα, a boy's game, a sort of **blind-man's-buff,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 9a, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.123.}}
**II** as _substantive_, see at {χαλκοῦς. [χάλκεοι} is _disyllable_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 150.]}}

5471:
** χαλκ-εύς**, έως, ὁ:
_plural_ χαλκεῖς, _Attic dialect_ **-ῆς**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 490 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 370d, _Epic dialect_ -ῆες (see. below): _accusative_ χαλκέας [prev. author] “Smp.” 221e, “R.” 428d, χαλκεῖς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.214a}}:—**coppersmith,** opposed to τέκτων (joiner, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 370d), ἣν [ἀσπίδα] χ. ἤλασεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.295, etc.; μίτρη, τὴν χαλκῆες κάμον ἄνδρες 4.187, 216.}}
**2** generally, **worker in metal,** of a **goldsmith,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.432}}; of a **worker in iron.** {{Refs|9.391}}; hence later, **blacksmith, smith** (χαλκέας τοὺς τὸν σίδηρον ἐργαζομένους {{LXX+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1461a29), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68, Ar.[same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.17; ἀνὴρ χ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.200; χ. χαλκοῦ καὶ σιδήρου LXX.Gen.4.22.}}
**II** **John Dory, Zeus faber,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.133, probably in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 535b18}}; distinguished from χαλκίς, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.328d.}}

5473:
** χαλκίον**, τό,
**copper vessel, cauldron, kettle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.393, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1128, “fragment” 107, 330, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 108, 256, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.19}}; χ. θερμαντήριον, ={θερμαντήρ}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 22.1416, 4.39 (from Aegina), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.663.}}
**2** **cymbal,** τὸ Δωδωναῖον χ., proverbial of a **chatterbox,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 66, compare 2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 6.5.}}
**3** **bowl of the** κότταβος, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.110.}}
**4** **copper ticket** given to the dicasts, bearing the name of the court in which they were to serve, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.10.}}
**5** **piece of copper money,** πονηρὰ χαλκία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 724 (troch.); παραλαβὼν τὼ χαλκίω 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 83; compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.90.—Freq. written χαλκεῖον in codices, but χαλκίον is required by the metre in Comedy texts, and corroborated by Inscrr. (see. above), and Papyrus, “PCair.Zen.” 630.4 (3rd c.BC), “PMich.Zen.” 65.2 (3rd c.BC), “PSI” 6.625.12 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**II** probably _diminutive_ of χάλκη, ={κάλχη}{{Refs 4th c.BC+|11, “IG” 42(1).102.242 (Epid., 4th c.BC).}}

5475:
** χαλκός**,
Cretan dialect **καυχός** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“GDI” 5011.4 (3rd c.BC)}}, ὁ:— **copper,** χ. ἐρυθρός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.365; with reference to its polished surface, αἶθοψ, ἦνοψ, νῶροψ, φαεινός, 4.495, 16.408, 2.578, 12.151; Τρῶες.. χαλκῷ μαρμαίροντες 13.801; πεδίον.. λάμπετο χαλκῷ 20.156; τῆλε δὲ χ. λάμφ᾽ ὥς τε στεροπή 10.153, compare 11.65, 19.363; σάκος.. χαλκῷ παμφαῖνον 14.11}}; and of the ornaments of a house, χαλκοῦ τε στεροπήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.72}}; of **copper** as the first metal that men learnt to smelt and work, τῶν δ᾽ ἦν χάλκεα μὲν τεύχεα, χάλκεοι δέ τε οἶκοι, χαλκῷ δ᾽ εἰργάζοντο, μέλας δ᾽ οὐκ ἔσκε σίδηρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 151}}; χ. ἐρυθρός (compare above) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἑλκῶν” 17, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 57, 3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 1; χ. ἐρυθρὸς καὶ λευκός 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 71; χ. Κύπριος 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 52J., 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.102, compare 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 3.10.14}}; alloyed with tin to form **bronze,** the usual meaning of the word in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below 11)}} and frequently in later writers: σίδηρος δὲ καὶ χ. πολέμων ὄργανα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 956a, etc.; χ. κεκραμένος 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 28.3.}}
**II** in Poets frequently for **anything made of metal,** especially of arms (hence {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus calls it πολιός}} the proper epithet of iron, {{Refs|“P.” 3.48)}}; of offensive arms, ὀξέϊ χαλκῷ, νηλέϊ χ., of a spear, a sword, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.540, 3.292, +others}}; of a knife, {{Refs|1.236, +others}}; of an axe, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|13.180, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.244, +others}}; of a fish-hook, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.408}}; of defensive arms, as the plates laid on a shield, {{Refs|20.275}}; χαλκὸν ζώννυσθαι, of a warrior girding on his armour,{{Refs|23.130; κεκορυθμένος, αἴθοπι χ. 4.495; ἐδύσετο νώροπα χ. 2.578}}; of both combined, πλάγχθη δ᾽ ἀπὸ χαλκόφι χαλκός **the spear of bronze** glanced off **the helm of bronze,** {{Refs|11.351.}}
**II.2** of vessels, **copper, cauldron, urn,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|18.349, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.426}}; of a cinerary urn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 758}}; collectively of **bronze plate,** χ. μυρίος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.45; θάλαμον.., ὅθι νητὸς χρυσὸς καὶ χ. ἔκειτο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.338, compare 13.19, 21.10, 62, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.226}}; used in payment of ransom, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|22.50, compare 340, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.38.}}
**II.3** of a **bronze mirror,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 393, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Lavacrum Palladis” 21, “Ap” 6.210 (4th-3rd c.BC: Philetas)}}; used as a burning-glass, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 73.}}
**II.4** collectively, **copper money**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|“IPE” 12.24.15 (Olbia, 4th c.BC), NT.Matt.10.9, NT.Mark.12.41, compare ἰσόνομος 11}}; generally, **money,** opposed to κύαμοι, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“IG” 14.423 ii 21 (from Tauromenium), compare “BGU” 822.12 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; χαλκοῦ σπάνις Men{{Refs|“Mon.” 156; χαλκὸν ἔχων πῶς οὐδὲν ἔχεις μάθε “Anthologia Graeca” 11.167 (Pollian.).}}
**II.5** ={χάλκωμα}, **bronze plate** or **tablet,** τὰν προξενίαν γράψαντας εἰς χαλκὸν ἀνθέμεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).682 (Corcyra, 4th c.BC)}}; οὐετρανοὶ οἱ χωρὶς χαλκῶν, who have not received **bronze copies** of the privileges granted on discharge, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 113.5 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II.6** a weight, 1/8 obol, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.752.}}
**III** χαλκοῦ ἄνθος, particles thrown off by copper when cooling, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.104, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 102.34, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.77.}}
**III.b** χαλκοῦ λεπίς, small pieces that scale off under the hammer, {{Refs|[prev. work]78. (Perh. cf. Lith.}}**geležìs** 'iron'.)

5476:
** χᾰμ-αί**,
_adverb_ **on the ground,** χ. ἧσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.160; τὸν αὖ χ. ἐξενάριξε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.145; χ. ἐρχομένων ἀνθρώπων 5.442, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 94, etc.; χ. τιθεὶς πόδα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 906; αἷμα μητρῷον χ. [prev. author] “Eu.” 261 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 869, “Eq.” 155, “Nu.” 231, +others; θέντες χ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.67; χ. καθίζοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 120b; χ. κείμενος “IG” 22.1672.305}}; of birds, ποιεῖν νεοττιὰν χ., opposed to ἐπὶ δένδρου, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 618a10.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, ἐσλὸν χ. σιγᾷ καλύψαι to bury in silence **underground,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.7}}; χ. ἐρχόμενοι cleaving to **earth,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 5, “Icar.” 6; ὁ χ. βίος 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “fragment” 38.}}
**II** ={χαμᾶζε}, **to earth,** ἐν κονίῃσι χ. πέσεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.482; χ. βάλον ἐν κονίῃσι 5.588, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.188, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.526; ἐκ δίφροιο χαμαὶ θόρε 8.320; μὴ χ. πεσεῖν}}**to the ground,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1170; οὐ χ. πεσεῖται ὅ τι ἂν εἴπῃς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 14d; ἔπτυσε χ. NT.John.9.6; also ἐκβαλεῖν εἰς τὸ χ. “Anthologia Graeca” 11.89 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}. (Cf. Latin _humus, humi,_ Lithuanian _ž[etilde]mė_ 'earth'.)

5479:
** χᾰρά**, ἡ:
(χαίρω):— **joy, delight,** first in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 1.6, then in Trag. and (less frequently) in Comedy texts and Prose: with _genitive_, στόματος ἐν πρώτῃ χ.}} of a hungry man, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 258}}; with _genitive_ objecti, **joy in** or **at** a thing, μελέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 579 (Lyric poetry)}}; πρὸς χαρὰν λόγων in accordance with **joyous** tidings, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 179 (variant{χάριν})}}]; κέρτομος θεοῦ χ. **a joy** sent by some mocking god, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1125; ἅμα χαρᾷ δακρυούσας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.9; χ. ἐπί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 413e; ἔδωκας ἡμῖν χ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 201; ἐλάβομεν χ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1449 (Lyric poetry); ἐμπλῆσαί τινα χαρᾶς [prev. author] “Ph.” 170; χαρά μ᾽ ὑφέρπει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 270}}; λέγεις μοι χαράν wishest me **joy,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 637 (Lyric poetry); χ. ἐνεργάσασθαι 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Hel.” 8; χαίρειν ἀνδραπόδων τινὰ χαράν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1091e, cf. NT.Matt.2.10; ἡδονὴ καὶ τέρψις καὶ χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 19; ἀπορέουσι.. χαρᾶς 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 293, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 2; ζήλου καὶ χαρᾶς καὶ ἐπαίνων ἡ πόλις ἦν μεστή 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.217; εἴσελθε εἰς τήν χ. τοῦ Κυρίου σου NT.Matt.25.21: χαρᾷ}}**with joy,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1630, “Ch.” 233, etc.; μετὰ χαρᾶς “Comica Adespota” 771, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.25, LXX.1Ch.29.22, +others; μετὰ μεγάλης χ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.34.12 (assuming variant); μετὰ πολλῆς χ. “BGU” 1141.3 (1st c.BC), compare 1768.7 (1st c.BC)}}; χαρᾶς ὕπο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 541; ὑπὸ χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.15; σὺν χαρᾷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 934, etc.}}; opposed to λύπη, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.46, +others}}
**II** in concrete sense, **a joy,** of persons, χ. μου {{NT|NT.Phil.4.1, cf. NT.1Thes.2.20.}}

5480:
** χάραγ-μα** [χᾰ], ατος, τό,
(χαράσσω) **any mark engraved, imprinted,** or **branded,** χ. ἐχίδνης the serpent's **mark,** i. e. its **bite, sting,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 267; ἐν ἰσχίοις μὲν ἵπποι πυρὸς χάραγμ᾽ ἔχουσιν “Anacreontea” 26 B 2; ἔχειν τὸ χ. τοῦ θηρίου NT.Rev.16.2, compare 13.16}}; χ. χειρός, i.e. writing, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.401 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus)}}; χαράγματα παμβασιλῆος, of an imperial **missive,** Epic. in {{Refs|“BKT” 5(1).115}}: _absolutely_, **inscription,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 7.220 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus), compare “PLond.” 5.1688.8 (4th c.AD)}}; **stamped document,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Sammelb. 5275.11 (1st c.AD)}}; **brand** on a camel, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 2.50 (a). 5 (2nd c.AD)}}; χ. τέχνης **carved work,** {{NT|NT.Acts.17.29}}; τὸ χ. τοῦ νομίσματος the **impress** on the coin, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 16, compare “Ages.” 15, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Misopogon” 355d (_plural_), etc.}}; hence,
**2** **stamped money, coin,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 5.29 (1st c.BC: Antipater Thessalonicensis Epigrammaticus), “POxy.” 144.6 (6th c.AD).}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **mark, stamp, character,** τὸ τῆς μονάδος σημαντικὸν χ. {{Refs|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 6.}}
**4** **endorsement,** {{Refs|Arch.Papyrus 1.85.}}

5481:
** χᾰρακτήρ**, ῆρος, ὁ,
(χαράσσω) **engraver,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Euryphamus Pythagoreus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.39.27}}; **one who mints coins**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IPE” 1216 A 14 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC).}}
**2** **graving tool,** [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Daimachus Historicus 4J. (_plural_) cited in 5th c.AD(?): Stephanus Byzantius see at {Λακεδαίμων}.}}]
**3** **die, stamp,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1013.64, 1408.11, 1424a120, 280, 1469.107; in a simile, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 781a28.}}
**4** **branding-iron, Clara Rhodos** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2.171 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II** **mark engraved, impress, stamp** on coins and seals, ἀργύρου λαμπρὸς χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 559, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 289b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1257a40}}; **coin type, standard,** ἦν δ᾽ ὁ ἀρχαῖος χ. δίδραχμον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ath.” 10.2, compare “OGI” 339.45 (Sestos, 2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.66}}; Κότυος χ. Head {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Hist.Num.” 2285 (Thrace, 1st c.BC)}}: hence, in _plural_, ={χάραγμα} 1.2, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PFlor.” 61.21 (1st c.AD): _metaphorically_, οἷς ἡ ἀρετὴ εὐδοξίας χαρακτῆρα τοῖς ἔργοις ἐπέβαλεν}} set a **stamp** upon them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.8}}; Κύπριος (assuming variant) χαρακτὴρ.. ἐν γυναικείοις τύποις εἰκὼς πέπληκται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 282.}}
**II.2** especially of **figures** or **letters,** οἱ τῶν γραμμάτων χ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.214f}}; ὁ τύπος τῶν χ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work]577f, compare 1120f, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.67}}; of the **letters** used by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus in “Epid.” 3.1, Zeno and Apollonius cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).618}}, compare 524f; of a single **letter** of the alphabet, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.72a; ξυλήφια βραχέα ἔχοντα χαρακτῆρα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.35.7}}; **brand** on a camel, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGen.” 29.8 (2nd c.AD)}}; of **symbols** in a prescription, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.995}}; of magical **symbols** (such as the seven vowels), τῶν χ. ἡ ἀπόρρητος φύσις {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.216c, compare 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 3.13, 4th c.AD: Sallustius Philosophus 15}}; of hieroglyphs, opposed to γράμματα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 44.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **distinctive mark** or **token impressed** (as it were) **on** a person or thing, by which it is known from others, **characteristic, character,** χ. γλώσσης, of a particular dialect, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.57, 142; χ. αὑτὸς ἐν γλώσσῃ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 176; χ. ἡμεδαπὸς τῶν ῥημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 220}}; ὁ Ἑλληνικὸς χ. Greek **idiom,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 3}}: frequently of persons, **feature,** ὁ χ. τοῦ προσώπου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116; εἰληφέναι χαρακτῆρα ἑκατέρου τοῦ εἴδους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 263b; οἱ τῆς ὄψεως χ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.91; ἀνδρῶν οὐδεὶς χ. ἐμπέφυκε σώματι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 519; δεινὸς χ. κἀπίσημος.. ἐσθλῶν γενέσθαι [prev. author] “Hec.” 379; φανερὸς χ. ἀρετᾶς [prev. author] “HF” 659 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἠθικοὶ χ., title of work by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus: _plural_, οἱ χ.}} the **features** of the face, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 13.12.1, compare “OGI” 508.13 (Ephesus, 2nd c.AD); χ. μορφῆς ἐμῆς [prev. work]383.60 (Nemrud Dagh, 1st c.BC, _sing._)}}; [τοῦ ἐμβρύου] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.33 (_plural_): hence}}
**II.4** **type** or **character** (regarded as shared with others) of a thing or person, rarely of an **individual nature,** ἀνδρὸς χ. ἐκ λόγου γνωρίζεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 72; χ. μοχθηρότατον παραπλάττεσθαι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.6 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; τὸν χ. τὸν Διογένους 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.22.80; τίνα ἔχει χ. τὰ δόγματ; [prev. work] 4.5.17}}; of nations, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.34.7.}}
**II.5** **style,** frequently in Rhet., ὁ Δημοσθένους χ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 9, compare “Pomp.” 1, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Quintum Fratrem” 2.15(16).5; χ. δικανικός 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.137S.}}; χ. **optimi** the ideal **type,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Oralor” 11.36, compare 39.134; χ. ἰσχνός, μεγαλοπρεπής, γλαφυρός, δεινός 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 36, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 33; χ. λέξεως [prev. author] “Lys.” 11; χ. Ἀσιανός 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.66.}}
**II.6** **impress, image,** τῆς ὑποστάσεως [τοῦ θεοῦ] {{NT|NT.Heb.1.3}}; πάθους, ἀρετῆς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 22.1, 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.243 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator: _absolutely_, οἱ Σεβάστειοι χ.}} the imperial **seal,** i.e. the emperor himself, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).268.24 (Mantinea, 1st c.BC).}}
**II.7** Grammars, **typical form,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 20.10, 103.23.}}

5482:
** χάραξ** [χᾰ], ᾰκος, ὁ,
also ἡ, (χαράσσω) **pointed stake**: especially,
**I** **vine-prop, pole,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 986, “V.” 1201, “Pax” 1263, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70, “BGU” 1122.17 (1st c.BC): proverbial ἐξηπάτησεν ἡ χ. τὴν ἄμπελον}} of those who trust in a 'broken reed', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1291.}}
**II** **pale,** used in fortifying the entrenchments of a camp, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 1178, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.167}}; = Latin _vallus,_ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.29.3, 18.18.1:}}
**II.2** collectively, ={χαράκωμα}, **palisaded camp,** Theophil.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Comedy texts 9, “SIG” 363.1 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC), 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 77, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 17 (_plural_), 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.60b; τὰν ἐκτὸς τοῦ χ. χώραν “IG” 42(1).76.21 (Epid., 2nd c.BC)}}; **palisade,** χάρακα βαλέσθαι πρὸς τῇ πόλει (variant{χαράκωμα}) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.87}}; = Latin _vallum,_ {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.80.11, 3.45.5, +others, NT.Luke.19.43}}; χάρακα τίθεσθαι form **an entrenched camp,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.29; χ. βαλέσθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 17, compare “Marc.” 18, etc.; βάλλειν [prev. author] “Sull.” 28}}; ἀποταφρεύειν, περιταφρεύειν,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 21, “Luc.” 31; διασπᾶν [prev. author] “Ant.” 18; χ. σεσιδηρωμένος καὶ ἁλύσεσι δεδεμένος 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.83; χ. κύκλῳ τῆς νεώς 4th c.BC: Moschio Tragicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.208d.}}
**III** **cutting, slip,** especially of an olive, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.1.2, “CP” 5.1. [4], “Geoponica” 9.11.5}}; of other plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.12.9.}}
**III.2** collectively, ={ἀκανθώδη φυτά}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**IV** a seafish, one of the **breams, Sargus,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in Ath 8.355e, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.173}}; also a fish of the Red Sea, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 12.25.}}
**V** name of a **bandage,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 48.31 at Title Page, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de fasciis - Opera Hippocratis et Galeni” 17, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18 (1).777. (2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 43 gives ἡ χ.}} in _signification_ 1, and in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PS” p.125B. gives ὁ χ. in signification 11, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.162; but this distinction is not observed by later writers.)}}

5483:
** χᾰρίζω**,
_future_ χαριῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.381 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, “Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}: _aorist_ _imperative_ χάρισον {{Refs|“PMag.Lond.” 122.17}}:—usually _middle_ **χαρίζομαι**, _future_ **-ιοῦμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40, 8.65}}; χαριῇ (variant{-εῖ}) also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90; Cretan dialect χαριξίομαι “GDI” 5176.16 (found at Teos); also χαρίξομαι [prev. work]5178.17 ([prev. passage]); χαρίηνται}} is a false _Aeolic dialect_ form in {{NT+2nd c.AD+|“Milet.” 3 No.152.56; later χαρίσομαι NT.Rom.8.32, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 22.4}}: _aorist_ ἐχαρισάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91, etc.; _optative_ χαρίσαιτο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.49}}; _Aeolic dialect_ _imperative_ χάρισσαι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 16.4}}; Cretan dialect _infinitive_ χαρίξασθαι {{Refs|“GDI” 5163b8 (from Mylasa):}} —_passive_ forms, _future_ χαρισθήσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{NT|NT.Philm.22:}}_aorist_ ἐχαρίσθην in _passive_ sense, {{NT|NT.Act.3.14, NT.1Cor.2.12:}}_perfect_ κεχάρισμαι in _active_ sense, κεχάρισαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1045}}, **-ισται** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 54}}; also in _passive_ sense, _imperative_ **-ίσθω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250c}}: _pluperfect_ ἐκεχάριστο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.5}}, _Epic dialect_ κεχάριστο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.23}}:—**say** or **do something agreeable** to a person, **show** him **favour** or **kindness, oblige, gratify,** with _dative_ _person_, frequently in _participle_, χαριζομένη πόσεϊ ᾧ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.71, compare 11.23, 15.449, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.538, 13.265; once in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, ποίησε.. χαριζόμενος Διί “Th.” 580; πᾶσι χαριζοίμην ἄν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.130, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40; τοῖς θεοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.16}}; Καλλίᾳ χαριζόμενος **to oblige, humour** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 362a, compare “Men.” 75b, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1368}}; of a judge, give a **partial** verdict, χ. οἷς ἂν δοκῇ αὐτῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35c; also χ. τῷ ἵππῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 10.12}}: _absolutely_, **make oneself agreeable, comply,** opposed to ἀντία φάσθαι, once in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, “Pers.” 700 (Lyric poetry); οἱ ὑπὲρ καιρὸν χαριζόμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.7: with _accusative_ _cognate_, χάριτας χ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 360.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.31, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.239; χ. τι καὶ αὐτός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42}}; with _participle_ added, χαρίζετο ἱερὰ ῥέζων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.61, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1045, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 338a, 426c, etc.: more frequently with _dative_ modi, μήτε τί μοι ψεύδεσσι χαρίζεο}} **do** not **court favour** by lies, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.387; χαριζόμενος φιλότητι 10.43, etc.; λόγῳ θωπεῦσαι καὶ ἔργῳ χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173a codices}}; opposed to τὰ βέλτιστα λέγειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.2, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.66a.}}
**2** **gratify** or **indulge** a humour or passion, once in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, θυμῷ χαρίζεσθαι κενά “El.” 331, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.25; ὀργῇ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 31; γλώσσῃ [prev. author] “Or.” 1514 (troch.); ἔρωτι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 127; τῇ ἐπιθυμίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 561c: τῷ σώματι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.23}}; τῇ γαστρί{{Refs|[prev. work] 2.1.2, “Cyr.” 4.2.39; τῇ ἡδονῇ [prev. work] 4.3.2.}}
**3** in erotic sense, **grant favours** to a man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 629 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 182a, “Phdr.” 231c, 256a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.12, etc.}}: hence of Comedy, ὀλίγοις χαρίσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 517 (_anapaest meter_): with _accusative_ _cognate_, χ. θήλειαν ἀπόλαυσιν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 27.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _of things_, **give graciously** or **cheerfully,** δῶρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.283; ἄποινα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.49, 10.380; χαρίζεσθαί τινί τι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 437, “Eq.” 54, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.9, etc.; πωλεῖν καὶ χ. καὶ τέκνοις μεταδιδόναι “PGrenf.” 1.60.45 (6th c.AD)}}; so with _accusative_ _person_, χαρίζομαί σε τοῖς ὄχλοις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PFlor.” 61.61 (1st c.AD)}}: with a strong oxymoron, ξείνια δυσμενέσιν λυγρὰ χ. {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 7}}: with _infinitive_ with _Article_, χ. τὸ ποθεῖν {{LXX+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.609a; τὸ ζῆν LXX.2Mac.3.33}}; without the _Article_, πολλοῖς ἐχαρίσατο βλέπειν (variant{τὸ β}.) {{NT|NT.Luke.7.21}}; χάρισαι [αὐτοῖς] μένειν **allow** them to remain, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 19, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 5.236 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}; so ἆρ᾽ ἄν τί μοι χαρίσαιο τοιόνδε—μή μου καταγελᾶ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 364c.}}
**II.b** χ. τὴν δέησιν **grant** the request, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 14.}}
**II.c** _passive_, with _accusative_, **to be favoured with,** ἀνάγκᾳ πνεῦμα χαριζόμενος {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 204.18 (from Cnidus).}}
**II.2** with _genitive_ _partitive_, **give freely of** a thing, ἀλλοτρίων χ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.452}}; ταμίη.. χαριζομένη παρεόντων **giving freely of** such things as were ready, {{Refs|1.140}}, etc.; παντοίων ἀγαθῶν γαστρὶ χαριζόμενοι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1000; γλώσσης μαφιδίοιο χ. παρεοῦσι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.188}}; προικὸς χαρίζεσθαι, of his bounty, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.15.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ _person_, **give up as a favour,** τῇ μητρὶ χ. Ὀκτάβιον, by dropping a law aimed at him, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “C. Gracchus” 4}}; but also, by unjust condemnation, {{NT|NT.Act.25.11, 16}}; also τῷ θεῷ με ἐχαρίσω, of a **dedication** ceremony, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PBremen” 49.14 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.4** **forgive,** τὴν ἀδικίαν τινί {{NT|NT.2Cor.12.13, cf. NT.Col.2.13: _absolutely_, NT.2Cor.2.7, etc.}}
**III** _passive_, especially in _perfect_ and _pluperfect_, κεχάριστο θυμῷ **was dear** to her heart, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.23}}; τοῖσι Εὐβοεῦσι ἐκεχάριστο the **pleasure** of the Euboeans **was done,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.5}}; ταῦτα μὲν οὖν μνήμῃ κεχαρίσθω **let a tribute be paid**.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250c; compare χάρις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus V.}}
**III.2** mostly _participle_ _perfect_ κεχαρισμένος, η, ον, as _adjective_, **acceptable, welcome,** ἐμῷ κεχαρισμένε θυμῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.243, 826, etc.}}; κεχαρισμένα δῶρα θεοῖσι δίδωσι,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|20.298, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.184, 19.397}}; κεχαρισμένα θεῖναί τινι to do **things pleasing** to one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.661; ἀνὴρ κεχαρισμένα εἰδώς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.584; θεοις κεχαρισμένα ποιεῖν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.33}}; κεχ. τοῖς θεοῖς λέγειν τε καὶ πράττειν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 14b, compare “Phdr.” 273e; δοίη ᾧ κ᾽ ἐθέλοι καί οἱ κεχαρισμένος ἔλθοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.54, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87, 3.119, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.10, etc.; κεχαρισμένα θύρσῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 892 (Lyric poetry); κεχαρ. χοιρίδιον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 386 (Lyric poetry); πᾶσιν κεχαρισμένος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 218a; λόγος κεχ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.1; σιτίον ἢ ποτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.24; ἐν τοῖς μὴ κεχαρισμένοις.. πρὸς τὴν αἴσθησιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 645a7}}; compare κεχαρισμένως.
**III.3** later, _comparative_ κεχαρισμενώτερος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 12.7}}; _superlative_ **-ώτατος**{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.65.}} —Rare in Trag., but frequently in _Attic dialect_ Prose.

5484:
Included with: ** χάρις** [ᾰ], ἡ,
_genitive_ χάρῐτος: _accusative_ χάριν [ῑ in arsi, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.874]}}, etc.; also χάριτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.41, 9.107, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 61, “Hel.” 1378, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.16, 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 24 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, “PGen.” 47.17 (4th c.AD), etc. (un-Attic, _accusative_ to Moer.p.414P.): χάριταν “Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”: _plural_ χάριτες}}; _dative_ χάρισι, χαρίτεσσι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.237, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.51, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.93}}: (χαίρω):—**grace**:
**I** in objective sense, **outward grace** or **fauour, beauty,** properly of persons or their portraits, θεσπεσίην δ᾽ ἄρα τῷ γε χάριν κατεχεύατ᾽ Ἀθήνη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.12, etc.; χάριν ἀμφιχέαι κεφαλῇ 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 65; εὐμόρφων δὲ κολοσσῶν ἔχθεται χ. ἀνδρί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 417 (Lyric poetry)}}: _plural_, **graces,** κάλλεϊ καὶ χάρισι στίλβων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.237; ὄσσοις χάριτας Ἀφροδίτης ἔχων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 236; μετὰ χαρίτων}}**gracefully,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.41: less frequently of things, χ. δ᾽ ἀπελάμπετο πολλή}} of ear-rings, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.183}}; of works, ἔργοισι χάριν καὶ κῦδος ὀπάζει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.320}}; of words, οὔ οἱ χ. ἀμφιπεριστέφεται ἐπέεσσιν {{Refs|8.175}}; πλείστη δὲ χ. κατὰ μέτρον ἰούσης [γλώσσης] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 720; ταὶ Διωνύσου σὺν βοηλάτα χάριτες διθυράμβῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.19; ἡ τῶν λόγων χ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.38, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 23; μῦθοι πληθόμενοι χαρίτων “Anthologia Graeca” 9.186 (1st c.BC: Antipater Thessalonicensis Epigrammaticus).}}
**I.2** **glory,** Φερενίκου χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.18, compare 8.57, 80.}}
**II** in subjective sense, **grace** or **favour felt,** whether on the part of the doer or the receiver (both senses appear in such phrases as ὅτ᾽.. ἡ χάρις χάριν φέροι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 779; χάρις χάριν γάρ ἐστιν ἡ τίκτουσ᾽ ἀεί [prev. author] “Aj.” 522, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1234, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1385a16):}}
**II.1** on the part of the doer, **grace, kindness, goodwill,** τινος **for** or **towards** one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 190; τῶν Μεσσηνίων χάριτι πεισθείς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.95}}; οὐ χάριτι τῇ ἐμῇ not for any **kind feeling towards** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.41: _absolutely_, εἰ δέ τις μείζων χ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 960; τῆς παλαιᾶς χ. ἐκβεβλημένη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 808}}; ἦ μεγάλα χ. δώρῳ σύν ὀλίγῳ {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.24; χ. εὑρεῖν ἐναντίον τοῦ θεοῦ LXX.Gen.6.8, +others; χάριν ἔχειν πρὸς τὸν δῆμον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 7}}; **partiality, favour,** μήτε ἔλεον μήτε συγγνώμην μήτε χ. μηδεμίαν περὶ πλείονος ποιήσασθαι τῶν νόμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.40; οὐ συμφωνοῦσιν ὀργαὶ καὶ χάριτες μακαριότητι 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Ep.” i p.28 U., compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 740c.}}
**II.2** more frequently on the part of the receiver, **sense of favour** received, **thankfulness, gratitude,** χάριν καὶ κῦδος ἄροιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.95; ἀρέομαι πὰρ Σαλαμῖνος Ἀθαναίων χ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.76}}; τινος **for** a thing, οὐδέ τίς ἐστι χάρις μετόπισθ᾽ εὐεργέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.695, compare 22.319; ἀντὶ πόνων χ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86: less frequently with}}_infinitive_, οὐκ ἄρα τις χάρις ἦεν μάρνασθαι one has, it seems, no **thanks for** fighting, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.316, 17.147; οἵ οἱ ἀπεμνήσαντο χ. εὐεργεσιάων 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 503, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137; χάριν φέρειν τινί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).17; χ. τροφεῦσιν ἀμείβων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 728 (Lyric poetry); φιλότητος ἀμειβόμεναι χ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 134 (Lyric poetry)}}; χάριν εἰδέναι τινί to acknowledge **a sense of favour, feel grateful,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἐγὼ δέ κέ τοι ἰδέω χ. ἤματα πάντα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.235; frequently in Prose, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.21, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.175, etc.}}; τούτων **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.11, etc.; τοῖς διαπεπραγμένοις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 62; μοι χ. οἶδεν ἐπὶ τούτοις 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 17; χ. προσειδέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20a; ἀποδιδόναι [prev. author] “R.” 338a; τινὰ ἀποστερῆσαι χάριτος [prev. author] “Hp.Mi.” 372c; later χ. γνῶναι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 2.17; πολλὴν γνοῦσα χ. 2nd c.AD(?): Xenophon Ephesius Scriptor Eroticus 3.5; χ. ἐπίσταμαι πᾶσι 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 3.4, compare 8.5, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.142, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 8.246c; also τῶν παροιχομένων ἔχειν σφι μεγάλην χ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.120, compare 1.71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 767 (Lyric poetry), “IT” 847 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.1, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 5}}: with _participle_, χ. ἔχειν σωθέντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.14}}; also χάριτας ἔχων πατρός owing him a debt of **gratitude,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 244: but ἀσπασμάτων χάριν τίν᾽ ἕξει}}; what **thanks** will she have **for**.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hec.” 830; χ. ἂν ἐν τούτῳ μείζω ἔτι ἔσχεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.87}}; χ. ὀφείλειν to owe **gratitude,** be beholden, τοῖς θεοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 331, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.30; προσοφείλειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.31; χ. οὐδεμία ἐφαίνετο πρὸς Ἀθηναίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.90}}; χάριν ἀθάνατον καταθέσθαι to lay up a store of undying **gratitude,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.178, compare 6.41; τῇ πόλει χ. καταθέσθαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.61, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.33}}; χάριν λαβεῖν τινος receive **thanks from** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1004, etc.; ἀπολαβεῖν παρά τινων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.31}}; τινος **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.5, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.4; διπλῆν ἐξ ἐμοῦ κτήσει χάριν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1370; κἀπ᾽ ἐμοῦ κτήσει χ. [prev. author] “Tr.” 471; κομίσασθαι χ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58; χάριτος τυχεῖν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 135; ἀπέχειν χάριτας 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 51.4, etc.}}; τοῖς θεοῖς χάρις (i.e. ἐστί) ὅτι.., **thank** the gods that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.14, “Cyr.” 7.5.72; χ. τινί τινος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 36; τινὶ ὑπέρ τινος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1122a.}}
**II.3** **favour, influence,** opposed to force, χάριτι τὸ πλέον ἢ φόβῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9}}; χ. καὶ δεήσει, opposed to ἀπειλῇ, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 38.}}
**II.4** **love-charm, philtre,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 5, “Merc.Cond.” 40.}}
**III** in concrete sense, **a favour** done or returned, **boon,** χάριν φέρειν τινί confer **a favour** on one, do a thing **to oblige** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.211, 874, 9.613, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.307, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 14, “Or.” 239, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.24}} (so in _middle_, of the recipient,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 9); ἄλλοις χ. φέροντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.54}}; χάριν θέσθαι or τίθεσθαί τινι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.60, 107, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 782, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1211, etc.; προσθέσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 767; χ. ὑπουργῆσαί τινι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 635; παρασχεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1183; πράσσειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 36, 896 (Lyric poetry); δράσας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40}}; ἀνύσαι probably in [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 995 (_anapaest meter_); νέμειν [prev. author] “Aj.” 1371; χ. δοῦναί τινι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 821, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1489 (but χ. δοῦναι, ={χαρίζεσθαι} (1.2)}}], **indulge, humour,** ὀργῇ[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 855; γαστρί 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 317); χ. χαρίζεσθαι, see at {χαρίζομαι} I.1: χ. ἀνθυπουργεῖν}}] return **a favour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 339; τίνειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 985, “Ag.” 821; χάριτας πατρῴας ἐκτίνων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 453, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 242c, etc.; χ. ἀποδιδόναι τινί 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.60, 28.17; ἀντί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.29; ὑπέρ τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.56; τῶν ἔργων τὰς χάριτας ἀποδ. τινί 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.24; χάριτας ἀντιδιδόναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.63}}; opposed to χάριν ἀπαιτεῖν to ask the repayment of **a boon,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 276, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.23, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.156; χάριτας ἀπ. 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 139; χάριν ἐξαιτεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 586}}; χ. ἀποστερεῖν withhold **a return** for what one has received, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 520c}}; τὰς αὑτοῦ εἰς τοὺς φίλους χ. the **favours** one has done them, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 729d}}; χ. ἄχαρις α thankless **favour,** one which receives, or deserves, no thanks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 545 (Lyric poetry); χ. ἀχάριτος [prev. author] “Ch.” 42 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1757 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III.b** **grant** made in legal form, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 273.14 (1st c.AD), “PGrenf.” 2.70.5 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; αἱ τῶν Σεβαστῶν χ. imperial **grants**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 669.44 (Egypt, 1st c.AD).}}
**III.2** especially in erotic sense, of **favours granted** (see. χαρίζομαι 1.3), ἀλόχου χάριν ἰδεῖν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.243, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1206: more frequently in _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.34, 7.6, etc.}}; βίᾳ δ᾽ ἔπραξας χάριτας ἢ πείσας κόρη; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Tragica Adespota” 402; in full, χάριτες ἀφροδισίων ἐρώτων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 128, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254a, +others}}
**IV** **gratification, delight,** τινος **in** or **from** a thing, συμποσίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.5; νίκας [prev. author] “O.” 10(11).78; ὕπνου χ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 159 (Lyric poetry); even χ. γόων [prev. author] “Supp.” 79 (Lyric poetry)}}; also concrete, of things, **a delight,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.19 (_plural_); τὰν βοτρυώδη Διονύσου χ. οἴνας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 535 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 311 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 3.125b; ἔνοπτρα, παρθένων χάριτας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1108 (Lyric poetry): _absolutely_, Ἔρως.. εἰσάγων γλυκεῖαν χ. [prev. author] “Hipp.” 527 (Lyric poetry)}}; opposed to λύπη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 821, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 655 (Lyric poetry)}}; opposed to πόνος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 232 (Lyric poetry); θανεῖν πολλὴ χάρις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 550, compare 1304; βίου χ. μεθεῖσα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 227; οὐδεμίαν ἔχω τῷ βίῳ χάριν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 865; τοῖς δὲ σιτίοις χ. οὐδεμίαν οἶδ᾽ ἐσθίων [prev. work] 869}}; less frequently in Prose, χ. καὶ ἡδονή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 462c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.26; τοσαύτην ἔχει χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.10.}}
**V** δαιμόνων χάρις **homage due** to them, their **worship, majesty,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 182 (Lyric poetry); ἀθίκτων χ. [prev. work] 371 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὅρκων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 439 (Lyric poetry).}}
**V.2** **thank-offering,** εὐκταία χ. τινός, opposed to a common gift, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1387, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 8.4; ἔπεμψε χαίτην κουρίμην χ. πατρός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 180, compare 517; τιμὴ καὶ γέρα καὶ χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 15a, compare “La.” 187a.}}
**VI** Special usages:
**VI.1** _accusative_ _singular_ as _adverb_, χ. τινός **in** any one's **favour, for** his **pleasure, for** his **sake,** χ. Ἕκτορος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.744}}; ψεύδεσθαι γλώσσης χ. **for** one's tongue's **pleasure,** i.e. **for** talking's **sake,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 709, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 266}}; rarely with _Article_, τὴν Ἀθηναίων χάριν ἐστρατεύοντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.99.}}
**VI.1.b** as _preposition_, sometimes before its case (once in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, “P.” 2.70; χάριν πλησμονῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241c; χ. φιλίας 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 28}}; χ. τίνο; {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.2Ch.7.21, compare “POxy.” 743.29 (1st c.BC), etc.)}}, but mostly after, **for the sake of, on behalf of, on account of,** κακά νιν ἕλοιτο μοῖρα δυσπότμου χάριν χλιδᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 888 (Lyric poetry)}}; τοῦ χάρι; **for** what **reason?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 53; συγχωρῶ τοῦ λόγου χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 475a}}; so ἐμὴν χάριν, χάριν σήν, **for** my, thy **pleasure** or **sake,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1046 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1238, etc.; κείνου τε καὶ σὴν ἐξ ἴσου κοινὴν χ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 485}}: less frequently with the _Article_, τὴν σὴν δ᾽ ἥκω χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1413 (_anapaest meter_); σοῦ τε τήν τ᾽ ἐμὴν χ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 762:—pleonastic, τίνος χάριν ἕνεκ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 701d}}; also χάριν τινός **as far as regards.., as to**.., ἔπους σμικροῦ χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 443}}; δακρύων χάριν **if** tears **would serve,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 557.6; χ. θανάτου πόλιν ἀτείχιστον οἰκοῦμεν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 31}}; also, **about,** ἔπεμφεν ἐπὶ τὴν πενθεράν σου χ. τοῦ κτήματος **about** the farm, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 126.5 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}—Orig. an _accusative_ in apposition with the sentence, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.744, etc.}}, **being a favour, since it is (was) a favour,** as is evident in κακῆς γυναικὸς χάριν ἄχαριν ἀπώλετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 566; τινὸς νίκας ἀκάρπωτον χ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 176 (Lyric poetry).}}
**VI.2** with Preps.:
**VI.2.a** εἰς χάριν **to do a pleasure,** οὐδὲν ἐς χ. πράσσων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “OT” 1353 (Lyric poetry); ἐς χ. τίθεσθαί τι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 46; μηδὲ κρίσιν εἰς χ. ἕλκε 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 9 (but ἐς τὴν τῶν ξυμμάχων χ.}} in such a way as to earn **thanks**.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.37); also κατὰ χάριν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 740c; χάριτος ἕνεκα [prev. work] 771d.}}
**VI.2.b** πράσσειν τί τινι πρὸς χάριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1776 (_anapaest meter_); δρᾶσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1281; τοῖσι πολλοῖς πρὸς χάριν λέγειν τι [prev. author] “Hec.” 257, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.4, “HG” 6.3.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.18, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.1 (but πρὸς χ. βορᾶς}} **for the sake** of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 30)}}; πρὸς χ., opposed to κλαίων, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 1152:—but πρὸς χ. εὐσεβίας, just like{χάριν}, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.8; τίνος νόμου ταῦτα πρὸς χ. λέγ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 908; πρὸς ἰσχύος χ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 538}}]; πρὸς χ. alone, **as a favour, freely,** πρὸς χ. τε κοὐ βίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 28}}; but κορέσαι στόμα πρὸς χ. **to** their **heart's content,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1156 (Lyric poetry).}}
**VI.2.c** ἐν χάριτι κρίνειν τινά to decide **from partiality** to one, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.69}}; but also, **for** one's **gratification, pleasure,** ἐν χάριτι διδόναι or ποιεῖν τινί τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 115b: παραλαμβάνειν ἐν χάρισιν}}**gratefully,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 796b.}}
**VI.2.d** διὰ χαρίτων εἶναι or γίγνεσθαί [τινι] to be **pleasing** to one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 9.1, 2.}}
**VI.2.e** ἐθελοντὶ καὶ μετὰ χάριτος **of pure good will,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.22.5, etc.; ἐθελούσιοι καὶ χάριτος ἕνεκα ἐξιόντες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.11.}}
**VII** _metaphorically_ of **the cypress,** {{Refs|Gp. 11.4.1}}; of some kind of **myrtle,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.51}}; of **salt,** ὅτι τὸ ἀναγκαῖον ἡδὺ ποιοῦσιν (i.e. ἅλες) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.685a.}}
**B** **Χάρις**, ἡ, as a mythological _proper name_ declined like{χάρις}, save that the _accusative_ is generally Χάριτα (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.148 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 15.1, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.35.4): poetry _dative_ _plural_ Χαρίτεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.51, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.54; Χάρισσιν [prev. work] 5.54}}:—**Charis,** wife of Hephaestus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.382}}; mostly in _plural_ Χάριτες, αἱ, **the Graces,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.267, 275, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.18, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.50, etc.}}; three in number, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 907, etc. (τέσσαρες αἱ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus}}, as a compliment, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 52.1)}}; attendants of Aphrodite, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.338, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 73, “hymnus ad Venerem” 61, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.24.7}}; coupled with Μοῦσαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 64}}; κόμαι Χαρίτεσσιν ὁμοῖαι, i.e. like that of the **Graces,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.51}}; worshipped at Orchomenus in Boeotia, Ἐτεόκλειοι Χάριτες θεαί {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.104, cf. Scholia (at [prev. work]), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.2.40, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.35.3, 9.38.1}}: but at Lacedaemon and Athens only two were originally worshipped, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.18.6, 9.35.2; Χαρίτων ἱερὸν ἐμποδὼν ποιοῦνται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1133a3; θύειν ταῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.141f; in adjurations, πρὸς τῶν Χαρίτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152c; νὴ τὰς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 26; ὦ φίλαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.710d.— Rarely in _sing._, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus ζωθάλμιος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.11; Χάριτος ἡδίστης θεῶν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 228.4.}}

5485:
** χάρις** [ᾰ], ἡ,
_genitive_ χάρῐτος: _accusative_ χάριν [ῑ in arsi, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.874]}}, etc.; also χάριτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.41, 9.107, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 61, “Hel.” 1378, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.16, 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 24 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, “PGen.” 47.17 (4th c.AD), etc. (un-Attic, _accusative_ to Moer.p.414P.): χάριταν “Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”: _plural_ χάριτες}}; _dative_ χάρισι, χαρίτεσσι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.237, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.51, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.93}}: (χαίρω):—**grace**:
**I** in objective sense, **outward grace** or **fauour, beauty,** properly of persons or their portraits, θεσπεσίην δ᾽ ἄρα τῷ γε χάριν κατεχεύατ᾽ Ἀθήνη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.12, etc.; χάριν ἀμφιχέαι κεφαλῇ 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 65; εὐμόρφων δὲ κολοσσῶν ἔχθεται χ. ἀνδρί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 417 (Lyric poetry)}}: _plural_, **graces,** κάλλεϊ καὶ χάρισι στίλβων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.237; ὄσσοις χάριτας Ἀφροδίτης ἔχων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 236; μετὰ χαρίτων}}**gracefully,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.41: less frequently of things, χ. δ᾽ ἀπελάμπετο πολλή}} of ear-rings, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.183}}; of works, ἔργοισι χάριν καὶ κῦδος ὀπάζει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.320}}; of words, οὔ οἱ χ. ἀμφιπεριστέφεται ἐπέεσσιν {{Refs|8.175}}; πλείστη δὲ χ. κατὰ μέτρον ἰούσης [γλώσσης] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 720; ταὶ Διωνύσου σὺν βοηλάτα χάριτες διθυράμβῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.19; ἡ τῶν λόγων χ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.38, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 23; μῦθοι πληθόμενοι χαρίτων “Anthologia Graeca” 9.186 (1st c.BC: Antipater Thessalonicensis Epigrammaticus).}}
**I.2** **glory,** Φερενίκου χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.18, compare 8.57, 80.}}
**II** in subjective sense, **grace** or **favour felt,** whether on the part of the doer or the receiver (both senses appear in such phrases as ὅτ᾽.. ἡ χάρις χάριν φέροι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 779; χάρις χάριν γάρ ἐστιν ἡ τίκτουσ᾽ ἀεί [prev. author] “Aj.” 522, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1234, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1385a16):}}
**II.1** on the part of the doer, **grace, kindness, goodwill,** τινος **for** or **towards** one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 190; τῶν Μεσσηνίων χάριτι πεισθείς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.95}}; οὐ χάριτι τῇ ἐμῇ not for any **kind feeling towards** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.41: _absolutely_, εἰ δέ τις μείζων χ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 960; τῆς παλαιᾶς χ. ἐκβεβλημένη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 808}}; ἦ μεγάλα χ. δώρῳ σύν ὀλίγῳ {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.24; χ. εὑρεῖν ἐναντίον τοῦ θεοῦ LXX.Gen.6.8, +others; χάριν ἔχειν πρὸς τὸν δῆμον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 7}}; **partiality, favour,** μήτε ἔλεον μήτε συγγνώμην μήτε χ. μηδεμίαν περὶ πλείονος ποιήσασθαι τῶν νόμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.40; οὐ συμφωνοῦσιν ὀργαὶ καὶ χάριτες μακαριότητι 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Ep.” i p.28 U., compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 740c.}}
**II.2** more frequently on the part of the receiver, **sense of favour** received, **thankfulness, gratitude,** χάριν καὶ κῦδος ἄροιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.95; ἀρέομαι πὰρ Σαλαμῖνος Ἀθαναίων χ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.76}}; τινος **for** a thing, οὐδέ τίς ἐστι χάρις μετόπισθ᾽ εὐεργέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.695, compare 22.319; ἀντὶ πόνων χ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86: less frequently with}}_infinitive_, οὐκ ἄρα τις χάρις ἦεν μάρνασθαι one has, it seems, no **thanks for** fighting, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.316, 17.147; οἵ οἱ ἀπεμνήσαντο χ. εὐεργεσιάων 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 503, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137; χάριν φέρειν τινί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).17; χ. τροφεῦσιν ἀμείβων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 728 (Lyric poetry); φιλότητος ἀμειβόμεναι χ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 134 (Lyric poetry)}}; χάριν εἰδέναι τινί to acknowledge **a sense of favour, feel grateful,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἐγὼ δέ κέ τοι ἰδέω χ. ἤματα πάντα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.235; frequently in Prose, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.21, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.175, etc.}}; τούτων **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.11, etc.; τοῖς διαπεπραγμένοις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 62; μοι χ. οἶδεν ἐπὶ τούτοις 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 17; χ. προσειδέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20a; ἀποδιδόναι [prev. author] “R.” 338a; τινὰ ἀποστερῆσαι χάριτος [prev. author] “Hp.Mi.” 372c; later χ. γνῶναι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 2.17; πολλὴν γνοῦσα χ. 2nd c.AD(?): Xenophon Ephesius Scriptor Eroticus 3.5; χ. ἐπίσταμαι πᾶσι 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 3.4, compare 8.5, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.142, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 8.246c; also τῶν παροιχομένων ἔχειν σφι μεγάλην χ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.120, compare 1.71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 767 (Lyric poetry), “IT” 847 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.1, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 5}}: with _participle_, χ. ἔχειν σωθέντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.14}}; also χάριτας ἔχων πατρός owing him a debt of **gratitude,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 244: but ἀσπασμάτων χάριν τίν᾽ ἕξει}}; what **thanks** will she have **for**.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hec.” 830; χ. ἂν ἐν τούτῳ μείζω ἔτι ἔσχεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.87}}; χ. ὀφείλειν to owe **gratitude,** be beholden, τοῖς θεοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 331, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.30; προσοφείλειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.31; χ. οὐδεμία ἐφαίνετο πρὸς Ἀθηναίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.90}}; χάριν ἀθάνατον καταθέσθαι to lay up a store of undying **gratitude,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.178, compare 6.41; τῇ πόλει χ. καταθέσθαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.61, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.33}}; χάριν λαβεῖν τινος receive **thanks from** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1004, etc.; ἀπολαβεῖν παρά τινων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.31}}; τινος **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.5, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.4; διπλῆν ἐξ ἐμοῦ κτήσει χάριν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1370; κἀπ᾽ ἐμοῦ κτήσει χ. [prev. author] “Tr.” 471; κομίσασθαι χ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58; χάριτος τυχεῖν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 135; ἀπέχειν χάριτας 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 51.4, etc.}}; τοῖς θεοῖς χάρις (i.e. ἐστί) ὅτι.., **thank** the gods that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.14, “Cyr.” 7.5.72; χ. τινί τινος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 36; τινὶ ὑπέρ τινος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1122a.}}
**II.3** **favour, influence,** opposed to force, χάριτι τὸ πλέον ἢ φόβῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9}}; χ. καὶ δεήσει, opposed to ἀπειλῇ, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 38.}}
**II.4** **love-charm, philtre,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 5, “Merc.Cond.” 40.}}
**III** in concrete sense, **a favour** done or returned, **boon,** χάριν φέρειν τινί confer **a favour** on one, do a thing **to oblige** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.211, 874, 9.613, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.307, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 14, “Or.” 239, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.24}} (so in _middle_, of the recipient,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 9); ἄλλοις χ. φέροντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.54}}; χάριν θέσθαι or τίθεσθαί τινι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.60, 107, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 782, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1211, etc.; προσθέσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 767; χ. ὑπουργῆσαί τινι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 635; παρασχεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1183; πράσσειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 36, 896 (Lyric poetry); δράσας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40}}; ἀνύσαι probably in [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 995 (_anapaest meter_); νέμειν [prev. author] “Aj.” 1371; χ. δοῦναί τινι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 821, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1489 (but χ. δοῦναι, ={χαρίζεσθαι} (1.2)}}], **indulge, humour,** ὀργῇ[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 855; γαστρί 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 317); χ. χαρίζεσθαι, see at {χαρίζομαι} I.1: χ. ἀνθυπουργεῖν}}] return **a favour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 339; τίνειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 985, “Ag.” 821; χάριτας πατρῴας ἐκτίνων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 453, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 242c, etc.; χ. ἀποδιδόναι τινί 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.60, 28.17; ἀντί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.29; ὑπέρ τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.56; τῶν ἔργων τὰς χάριτας ἀποδ. τινί 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.24; χάριτας ἀντιδιδόναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.63}}; opposed to χάριν ἀπαιτεῖν to ask the repayment of **a boon,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 276, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.23, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.156; χάριτας ἀπ. 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 139; χάριν ἐξαιτεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 586}}; χ. ἀποστερεῖν withhold **a return** for what one has received, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 520c}}; τὰς αὑτοῦ εἰς τοὺς φίλους χ. the **favours** one has done them, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 729d}}; χ. ἄχαρις α thankless **favour,** one which receives, or deserves, no thanks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 545 (Lyric poetry); χ. ἀχάριτος [prev. author] “Ch.” 42 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1757 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III.b** **grant** made in legal form, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 273.14 (1st c.AD), “PGrenf.” 2.70.5 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; αἱ τῶν Σεβαστῶν χ. imperial **grants**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 669.44 (Egypt, 1st c.AD).}}
**III.2** especially in erotic sense, of **favours granted** (see. χαρίζομαι 1.3), ἀλόχου χάριν ἰδεῖν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.243, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1206: more frequently in _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.34, 7.6, etc.}}; βίᾳ δ᾽ ἔπραξας χάριτας ἢ πείσας κόρη; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Tragica Adespota” 402; in full, χάριτες ἀφροδισίων ἐρώτων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 128, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254a, +others}}
**IV** **gratification, delight,** τινος **in** or **from** a thing, συμποσίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.5; νίκας [prev. author] “O.” 10(11).78; ὕπνου χ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 159 (Lyric poetry); even χ. γόων [prev. author] “Supp.” 79 (Lyric poetry)}}; also concrete, of things, **a delight,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.19 (_plural_); τὰν βοτρυώδη Διονύσου χ. οἴνας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 535 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 311 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 3.125b; ἔνοπτρα, παρθένων χάριτας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1108 (Lyric poetry): _absolutely_, Ἔρως.. εἰσάγων γλυκεῖαν χ. [prev. author] “Hipp.” 527 (Lyric poetry)}}; opposed to λύπη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 821, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 655 (Lyric poetry)}}; opposed to πόνος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 232 (Lyric poetry); θανεῖν πολλὴ χάρις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 550, compare 1304; βίου χ. μεθεῖσα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 227; οὐδεμίαν ἔχω τῷ βίῳ χάριν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 865; τοῖς δὲ σιτίοις χ. οὐδεμίαν οἶδ᾽ ἐσθίων [prev. work] 869}}; less frequently in Prose, χ. καὶ ἡδονή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 462c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.26; τοσαύτην ἔχει χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.10.}}
**V** δαιμόνων χάρις **homage due** to them, their **worship, majesty,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 182 (Lyric poetry); ἀθίκτων χ. [prev. work] 371 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὅρκων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 439 (Lyric poetry).}}
**V.2** **thank-offering,** εὐκταία χ. τινός, opposed to a common gift, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1387, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 8.4; ἔπεμψε χαίτην κουρίμην χ. πατρός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 180, compare 517; τιμὴ καὶ γέρα καὶ χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 15a, compare “La.” 187a.}}
**VI** Special usages:
**VI.1** _accusative_ _singular_ as _adverb_, χ. τινός **in** any one's **favour, for** his **pleasure, for** his **sake,** χ. Ἕκτορος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.744}}; ψεύδεσθαι γλώσσης χ. **for** one's tongue's **pleasure,** i.e. **for** talking's **sake,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 709, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 266}}; rarely with _Article_, τὴν Ἀθηναίων χάριν ἐστρατεύοντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.99.}}
**VI.1.b** as _preposition_, sometimes before its case (once in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, “P.” 2.70; χάριν πλησμονῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241c; χ. φιλίας 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 28}}; χ. τίνο; {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.2Ch.7.21, compare “POxy.” 743.29 (1st c.BC), etc.)}}, but mostly after, **for the sake of, on behalf of, on account of,** κακά νιν ἕλοιτο μοῖρα δυσπότμου χάριν χλιδᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 888 (Lyric poetry)}}; τοῦ χάρι; **for** what **reason?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 53; συγχωρῶ τοῦ λόγου χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 475a}}; so ἐμὴν χάριν, χάριν σήν, **for** my, thy **pleasure** or **sake,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1046 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1238, etc.; κείνου τε καὶ σὴν ἐξ ἴσου κοινὴν χ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 485}}: less frequently with the _Article_, τὴν σὴν δ᾽ ἥκω χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1413 (_anapaest meter_); σοῦ τε τήν τ᾽ ἐμὴν χ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 762:—pleonastic, τίνος χάριν ἕνεκ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 701d}}; also χάριν τινός **as far as regards.., as to**.., ἔπους σμικροῦ χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 443}}; δακρύων χάριν **if** tears **would serve,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 557.6; χ. θανάτου πόλιν ἀτείχιστον οἰκοῦμεν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 31}}; also, **about,** ἔπεμφεν ἐπὶ τὴν πενθεράν σου χ. τοῦ κτήματος **about** the farm, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 126.5 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}—Orig. an _accusative_ in apposition with the sentence, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.744, etc.}}, **being a favour, since it is (was) a favour,** as is evident in κακῆς γυναικὸς χάριν ἄχαριν ἀπώλετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 566; τινὸς νίκας ἀκάρπωτον χ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 176 (Lyric poetry).}}
**VI.2** with Preps.:
**VI.2.a** εἰς χάριν **to do a pleasure,** οὐδὲν ἐς χ. πράσσων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “OT” 1353 (Lyric poetry); ἐς χ. τίθεσθαί τι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 46; μηδὲ κρίσιν εἰς χ. ἕλκε 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 9 (but ἐς τὴν τῶν ξυμμάχων χ.}} in such a way as to earn **thanks**.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.37); also κατὰ χάριν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 740c; χάριτος ἕνεκα [prev. work] 771d.}}
**VI.2.b** πράσσειν τί τινι πρὸς χάριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1776 (_anapaest meter_); δρᾶσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1281; τοῖσι πολλοῖς πρὸς χάριν λέγειν τι [prev. author] “Hec.” 257, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.4, “HG” 6.3.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.18, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.1 (but πρὸς χ. βορᾶς}} **for the sake** of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 30)}}; πρὸς χ., opposed to κλαίων, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 1152:—but πρὸς χ. εὐσεβίας, just like{χάριν}, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.8; τίνος νόμου ταῦτα πρὸς χ. λέγ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 908; πρὸς ἰσχύος χ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 538}}]; πρὸς χ. alone, **as a favour, freely,** πρὸς χ. τε κοὐ βίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 28}}; but κορέσαι στόμα πρὸς χ. **to** their **heart's content,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1156 (Lyric poetry).}}
**VI.2.c** ἐν χάριτι κρίνειν τινά to decide **from partiality** to one, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.69}}; but also, **for** one's **gratification, pleasure,** ἐν χάριτι διδόναι or ποιεῖν τινί τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 115b: παραλαμβάνειν ἐν χάρισιν}}**gratefully,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 796b.}}
**VI.2.d** διὰ χαρίτων εἶναι or γίγνεσθαί [τινι] to be **pleasing** to one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 9.1, 2.}}
**VI.2.e** ἐθελοντὶ καὶ μετὰ χάριτος **of pure good will,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.22.5, etc.; ἐθελούσιοι καὶ χάριτος ἕνεκα ἐξιόντες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.11.}}
**VII** _metaphorically_ of **the cypress,** {{Refs|Gp. 11.4.1}}; of some kind of **myrtle,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.51}}; of **salt,** ὅτι τὸ ἀναγκαῖον ἡδὺ ποιοῦσιν (i.e. ἅλες) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.685a.}}
**B** **Χάρις**, ἡ, as a mythological _proper name_ declined like{χάρις}, save that the _accusative_ is generally Χάριτα (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.148 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 15.1, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.35.4): poetry _dative_ _plural_ Χαρίτεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.51, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.54; Χάρισσιν [prev. work] 5.54}}:—**Charis,** wife of Hephaestus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.382}}; mostly in _plural_ Χάριτες, αἱ, **the Graces,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.267, 275, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.18, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.50, etc.}}; three in number, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 907, etc. (τέσσαρες αἱ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus}}, as a compliment, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 52.1)}}; attendants of Aphrodite, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.338, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 73, “hymnus ad Venerem” 61, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.24.7}}; coupled with Μοῦσαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 64}}; κόμαι Χαρίτεσσιν ὁμοῖαι, i.e. like that of the **Graces,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.51}}; worshipped at Orchomenus in Boeotia, Ἐτεόκλειοι Χάριτες θεαί {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.104, cf. Scholia (at [prev. work]), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.2.40, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.35.3, 9.38.1}}: but at Lacedaemon and Athens only two were originally worshipped, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.18.6, 9.35.2; Χαρίτων ἱερὸν ἐμποδὼν ποιοῦνται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1133a3; θύειν ταῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.141f; in adjurations, πρὸς τῶν Χαρίτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152c; νὴ τὰς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 26; ὦ φίλαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.710d.— Rarely in _sing._, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus ζωθάλμιος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.11; Χάριτος ἡδίστης θεῶν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 228.4.}}

5489:
** χάρτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
= {χαρτηρία}, **papyrus,** or a **roll** made thereof, χ. δύο {{LXX+5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.374.279; τὰ γραμματεῖα τούς τε χ. ἐκφέρων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 194, cf. LXX.Isa.8.1 codex 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor, “PCair.Zen.” 687.4 (3rd c.BC), “Inscription Délos” 442 A 182 (2nd c.BC), “PTeb.” 112.61 (2nd c.BC), 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.86, 1st c.AD: Cebes Philosophus 4, etc., “Anthologia Graeca” 9.174 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus), 401 ([prev. author])}}; ὥσπερ χάρτην εὐεργὸν εἰς ἀπολραφήν (of the soul at birth) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.28; χάρται βυβλίων 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 283}}; χ. βασιλικοί, of the finest papyrus, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 26.3}}; χ. κεκαυμένος used in Medicine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.382, 13.315; also χάρτου σποδιά 4th-3rd c.BC: Lycus Historicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.25.15; τὸ διὰ χάρτου μέλαν 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.41.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **any leaf** or **thin plate,** χ. μολύβδινοι **sheets** of lead, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd or1st c.BC: Lysimachus Historicus cited in 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 1.34.}}

5490:
** χάσμα**, ατος, τό,
(χαίνω) **yawning chasm, gulf,** χ. μέγα, of Tartarus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 740; Ταρτάρου.. ἄβυσσα χ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1605; χ. γῆς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.30; τὰ χ. τῆς γῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 111e}}; χθονός, πέτρας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 281, “IT” 626; σεισμοὶ καὶ χάσματα 5th-6th c.AD: Julianus Laodicensis Astrologus in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 1.134.}}
**II** **open, gaping mouth,** χ. θηρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 363 (Lyric poetry)}}; as forming a helmet, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 209}}; of a **yawning gulf,** χάρυβδις.. ἄρμα περιβαλοῦσα χάσματι {{Refs 6th c.AD+|[prev. author] “Supp.” 501; Σκύλλης χάσμασιν “Anthologia Graeca” 11.379 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}; χ. φάρυγος, of a lion,{{Refs 7th c.BC+|[prev. work] 6.218 (7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus); χ. ὀδόντων “Anacreontea” 24.4, etc.}}
**III** generally, **any wide opening,** θυρέτρων χ. ἀχανές {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 1.18}}; also, **expanse,** of the sky and sea, χ. πελάγεος τὸ δὴ Αἰγαῖον καλέεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.85; τὸ χ. τοῦ οὐρανοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 614d.}}

5491:
** χεῖλος**
(_Doric dialect_ χῆλος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 1.5}}, _Aeolic dialect_ χέλλος {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus in “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 2.278)}}, εος, τό: _plural_, _genitive_ χειλῶν {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 492b26; χειλέων 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.4, LXX.Pro.12.13, +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 12, variant in 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 14: poetry _dative_ χείλεσσι (see. below)}}:—**lip,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.: proverbial, ἐγέλασσε χείλεσιν}} laughed **with the lips only,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.102}}; χείλεα μέν τ᾽ ἐδίην᾽, ὑπερῴην δ᾽ οὐκ ἐδίηνε wetted **the lips,** but not the palate, i.e. drank sparingly,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|22.495; νέκταρ ἐν χείλεσσι στάξοισι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.63}}; πειθώ τις ἐπεκάθιζεν ἐπὶ τοῖς χ., of Pericles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 94.5; χείλεσιν διδοὺς ὀδόντας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 621 (troch.)}}; χείλεσιν ἀμφιλάλοις, of incessant talk, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 678 (Lyric poetry)}}; δάκνων τὰ χ., of one in a difficulty, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 53.6; ἄχρις ἡ ψυχή.. ἐπὶ χειλέων λειφθῇ 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus [same place]}}; ἐπὶ τοῖς χείλεσι τὰς ψυχὰς ἔχοντες 'with their hearts in their mouths', {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 32.50}}; ἀπὸ χειλέων, opposed to ἀπὸ καρδίας, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [same place]; ἀπ᾽ ἄκρου χ. φιλοσοφεῖν}}**on the surface only,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Apol.” 6}}; ἐπ᾽ ἄκρου τοῦ χ. on the tip of one's **tongue,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ind.” 26}}; προσαρμόσαι τὰ χ. (i.e. τῇ κύλικι) {{Refs|[prev. author] “DDeor.” 5.2}}; προσαρμόζειν τὰ χ., χείλη προσεγγίσαι χείλεσιν, of persons kissing, {{Refs|[prev. author] “DMeretr.” 5.3, “Am.” 53}}; χείλεσιν διερρυηκόσιν with gaping **lips,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 873; ἐν τοῖς χ. τιμᾶν LXX.Isa.29.13}}; ἐν χ. ἑτέρων λαλεῖν, i.e. in strange **speech,** {{LXX+NT|NT.1Cor.14.21; χ. ἓν πάντων LXX.Gen.11.6, cf. LXX.Pro.10.19.}}
**2** of horses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 6.8}}: of birds, **bill, beak,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1199, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 3.247, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.333 (3rd c.BC: Mnasalcas Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** _metaphorically_ of things, **edge, brink, rim,** of a bowl, χρυσῷ δ᾽ ἐπὶ χείλεα κεκράανται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.616, compare 132; Ἐλπὶς.. ἔμιμνε πίθου ὑπὸ χείλεσιν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 97, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.123, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 459}}; of a ditch, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.52, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.179, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.23}}; of the ocean, {{LXX+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 11.7, cf. LXX.Gen.22.17; τῶν τῆς γῆς τροχῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 115e}}; of rivers, lakes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.70, 94, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 570a22}}; of the whorls, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 616d, 616e}}; αὐλαίας, τείχους, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Exo.26.4, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.44.11}}; of the womb, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 583a16}}; of a wound, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.127.}}

5492:
** χειμ-άζω**
(_future_ -άσω{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 38)}}:—_transitive_, **expose to the winter cold**: found only in _passive_, **to be exposed thereto,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 3.68}}; **pass the winter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 503; ὅπως χειμασθῇ καὶ ἡλιωθῇ ἡ γῆ 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.20.7}}; of trees, **live through the winter,** χειμασθέντα [δένδρα] {{Refs|[prev. author] “HP” 4.14.1; χειμασθῆναι χειμῶσι ὡραίοις καὶ καλοῖς [prev. author] “CP” 2.1.2.}}
**2** _intransitive_, **pass the winter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1097 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 5.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.54, etc.}}: of armies, **go into winter quarters,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.133, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.15, 3.2.1, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 27.18.1, etc.}}
**II** **raise a storm** or **tempest,** θεοῦ τοιαῦτα χειμάζοντος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1504; ὅταν χειμάζῃ ὁ θεὸς ἐν τῇ θαλάττῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.16, compare “IG” 7.4255.5 (Orop.); χειμάσει [ἡ νεφέλη] ἐφ᾽ ἡμᾶς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.195d}}: _impersonal_, ἐχείμαζε ἡμέρας τρεῖς **the storm continued,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.191}}; χειμάσει **there will be stormy weather,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Sign.” [prev. cited]}}
**III** with _accusative_, **drive forth** or **away,** of a storm, ἔξω χ. [τοὺς μύας] {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 174.7}}:—_passive_, **to be driven by a storm, overtaken by it,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.25, 3.69, +others; χειμασθεὶς ἀνέμῳ [prev. author] 8.99; ἐν θαλάττῃ χειμαζομένου πλοίου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 540b, etc.}}
**III.2** _metaphorically_, **toss like a storm, distress,** τόδ᾽ αἷμα χ. πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 101; τὴν σάρκα τὸ παρὸν μόνον χειμάζειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 452}}: also, **annoy, vex,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 331, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 208, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.61 O., “POsl.” 48.8 (1st c.AD)}}; σφὴξ τοῖς κέντροις πλήσσων ἐχείμαζε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 393}}:—_passive_, **to be tempest-tossed, distressed,** especially of the state considered as a ship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 269, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 361; δόμων ὄλβος χειμάζεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 966}}; also of single persons, κατὰ θάλασσαν χειμασθεῖσαι (as example of a ψυχικὸν πάθος) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 3.84}}; **suffer grievously,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 563 (_anapaest meter_), 838, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1460 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Pal.” 11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 273d; ἰσχὺς ἐν νόσῳ χειμάζεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 267; ταῖς σαῖς ἀπειλαῖς αἷς ἐχειμάσθην [prev. author] “Ant.” 391; ἄλλῃ δ᾽ ἐν τύχῃ χ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 315; χειμαζόμεθα.. ὑπ᾽ ἀπορίας ἐν τοῖς νῦν λόγοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 29b, compare “La.” 194c; ὑπό τινων “PSI” 4.349.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐν στρατείαις ἢ νόσοις ἢ ἐν θαλάττῃ χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 170a}}; of feverish patients, χειμάζονται μάλιστα πεμπταῖοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 24.}}

5493:
** χειμάρροος**, ον,
_contraction_ **χειμάρρους**, ουν, and shortened **χείμαρρος**, ον: (χεῖμα, ῥέω):— **winter-flowing, swollen by rain and melted snow,** of mountain-streams,
**I** joined with ποταμός, ὅν τε [the stone] ποταμὸς χειμάρροος ὤσῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.138; ὡς δ᾽ ὁπότε πλήθων ποταμὸς πεδίονδε κάτεισιν χειμάρρους κατ᾽ ὄρεσφιν 11.493}}: frequently in contracted forms, ποταμῷ πλήθοντι ἐοικὼς χειμάρρῳ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5.88; ὡς δ᾽ ὅτε χείμαρροι ποταμοὶ κατ᾽ ὄρεσφι ῥέοντες 4.452; χειμάρρῳ ποταμῷ ἴκελος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 348; παρὰ ῥείθροισι χειμάρροις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 712; φάραγγες ὕδατι χειμάρρῳ ῥέουσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 449 (troch.); διὰ χειμάρρου νάπης [prev. author] “Ba.” 1093; χαράδρα χ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.30.2.}}
**I.2** πλεκτάνη χειμάρροος seems to be **rushing, furious** lightning {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 281.}}
**II** _substantive_, **torrent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 736b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.7; ὥσπερ χειμάρρους ἂν εἰς τὴν πόλιν εἰσέπεσε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.153.}}
**II.2** simply, **river,** {{LXX|LXX.Num.34.5.}}
**II.3** **drain, gutter,** οἱ ἐκ τῶν οἰκιῶν χ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 55.19.}}
**II.4** **valley, watercourse,** {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.4Ki.3.16. (Plur. accented χείμαρροι by Ptolemaeus Ascalonita, χειμάρροι by Nicias, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 496.37; later _nominative_ χείμαρρος 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.33.7, 10.37.3, _accusative_ χείμαρρον LXX [prev. cited] codex 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor, LXX.Psa.123(124).4, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.35.7.)}}

5494:
** χειμών**, ῶνος, ὁ,
(χεῖμα) **winter,** χειμῶνος δυσθαλπέος ὅς ῥά τε ἔργων ἀνθρώπους ἀνέπαυσεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.549}}; χειμῶνι **in winter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|21.283; ἐν χειμῶνι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.42, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 969, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.8; ἐν τῷ χ. [prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.8.17; χειμῶνος ὥρᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.137}}; also χειμῶνος **in winter-time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 415e}}; χ. μέσου **in** mid-**winter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 569.1; τοῦ χ.}}**in the course of the winter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.31; τοῦ αὐτοῦ χ. [prev. author] 8.30}}; διὰ χειμῶνος, διὰ τοῦ χ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 74c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.9; χειμῶνα}}**during winter,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1138 (variant{χειμῶνι})}}]; τὸν χ. **during** the **winter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.4.1; τὸν δεινὸν χ. [prev. author] “An.” 7.6.9; τὸν χ. ὅλον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 345; ὁ ἀμφὶ τὸν χ. χρόνος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.22}}; ὄρος ἄβατον ὑπὸ χειμῶνος in consequence of **the cold weather,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.138, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.101: _plural_, νιφοστιβεῖς χειμῶνες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 671}}; opposed to καύματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 829b; ἀμυντικὴ χειμώνων [prev. author] “Plt.” 280e.}}
**2** **the wintry quarter** of the heavens, **the north,** Βορέης καὶ χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.26.}}
**II** **wintry, stormy weather**: generally, **storm,** ἐπεὶ οὖν χειμῶνα φύγον καὶ ἀθέσφατον ὄμβρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.4; οὐ νιφετὸς οὔτ᾽ ἂρ χ. πολὺς οὔτε ποτ᾽ ὄμβρος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.566; ὅτε τις χ. ἔκπαγλος ὄροιτο 14.522; ὀπωρινὸν ὄμβρον καὶ χειμῶν᾽ ἐπιόντα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 675, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 11.6, etc.; Γαιάοχος εὐδίαν ὄπασσεν ἐκ χειμῶνος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).39; θεὸς χειμῶν᾽ ὦρσε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 496, compare “Ag.” 649, 656, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1145, etc.; χ. ὀρνιθίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 876; χ. κατερράγη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87; ἐπέπεσέ σφι χ. τε μέγας καὶ πολλὸς ἄνεμος [prev. author] 7.188, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 344d; ἐπιγενόμενος χ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.34, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.6; χειμῶνι χρησάμενοι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.21}}; χ. νοτερός **a storm** of rain, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.21; ἐν εὐδίᾳ χειμῶνα ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.14: _plural_, ὑπὸ τῶν χ.}} because of the **winter-storms,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.62; ἔν γε χειμῶσιν καὶ ἐν εὐδίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 961e, compare 919a.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, θεόσσυτος χ. **storm of calamity** sent by the gods, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 643}}; χ. καὶ κακῶν τρικυμία{{Refs|[prev. work] 1015, compare “Ch.” 202 (_plural_), 1066 (_anapaest meter_)}}; δορὸς.. ἐν χειμῶνι in the **storm** of battle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 670}}; θολερῷ.. χ. νοσήσας, of the madness of Ajax, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 207 (_anapaest meter_)}}; χ. γήρως βαρύς, of life's **winter,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 10.100 (1st c.AD: Antiphanes Macedo Epigrammaticus)}}; of a person, χ. ὁ μειρακίσκος ἐστὶ τοῖς φίλοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 178.7, compare 46.4; χ. κατ᾽ οἴκους.. κακὴ γυνή 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 540}}: rare in Prose, of battle, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 32.10}}; of mental and moral **trouble,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.62U., Polystr. p.19W.}}; χ. τοῦ κλύδωνος χαλεπώτερος, of pirates, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 23.286a: _plural_, χειμῶνας ἔχειν}} to have **trouble** (in cutting teeth), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ὀδοντοφυΐης” 12.}}

5495:
** χείρ**, ἡ, χειρός, χειρί, χεῖρα,
dual χεῖρε, χεροῖν, _plural_ χεῖρες, χερῶν, χεῖρας, _penultimate_ being regularly short, when the _ultima _ is long; _dative_ _plural_ regularly χερσί (χειρσί occurs in codex Vat. of {{LXX|LXX, as LXX.Judg.7.19, LXX.1Ch.5.10}}, and late Inscrr. as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2811A b.10 (from Aphrodisias), 2942c (from Tralles): but Poets used the penultimate long or short in all cases, as the verse required, χερός, χερί, χέρα, χέρε, χέρες, χέρας (of which 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only χερ; χέρα “hymnus ad Panem” 40); _genitive_ dual χειροῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 206 (Lyric poetry), 1394 (Lyric poetry), “IG” 22.1498.76; _genitive_ _plural_ χειρῶν [prev. work]31, common in Prose.—poetical forms, _dative_ _plural_ χείρεσι (ν) once in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.468, also 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 2.401, 5.469 (variant); χείρεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.382, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).62, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 976 (Lyric poetry), 1297 (Lyric poetry)}}, and once in _trimeter_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 756}}; χέρεσσι (ν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 519, 747, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 17.49; χερέεσσιν “AJA” 36.460 (from Galatia)}}:—_Doric dialect_ _nominative_ **χέρς** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Timocreon Lyricus 9}}; **χήρ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus in “PSI” 11.1214a3}} (also, ={δίψακος}, Ps.-{{Refs 7th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.11); _genitive_ χηρός 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 32, “IG” 42(1).121.22 (Epid., 4th c.BC); _accusative_ _plural_ χῆρας [prev. work]96, _Aeolic dialect_ χέρρας 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 4.21, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.9.—On the accent and declension of these forms, see Hdn.Gr. 2.277, 748}}:— **the hand,** whether **closed,** παχεῖα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.376; βαρεῖα 11.235, +others}}; or **open, flat,** χερσὶ καταπρηνέσσι, χειρὶ καταπρηνεῖ,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.114, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.164, +others; εἰς τὴν χ. ἐγχεάμενοί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.9}}: frequently in _plural_ where **a single hand** is meant, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.384, etc.}}; reversely, _singular_ where more than one **hand** is spoken of, e.g. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.37, etc.}}; dual joined with _plural_, ἄμφω χεῖρας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.135; χεῖρε ἀμφοτέρας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.115.}}
**2** **hand and arm, arm** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare (2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 11, 82, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.347), πῆχυν χειρὸς δεξιτερῆς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.166; κατὰ χεῖρα μέσην ἀγκῶνος ἔνερθε 11.252; χεῖρες ἀπ᾽ ὤμων ἀΐσσοντο 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 150; χ. εἰς ὤμους γυμναί 3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 1.4}}; ἐν χερσὶ γυναικῶν πεσέειν into **the arms,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.81, etc.}}: hence, words are added to denote the **hand** as distinct from the **arm,** ἄκρην οὔτασε χεῖρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5.336; περὶ ἄκραις ταῖς χ. χειρῖδας ἔχουσι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 352a.}}
**3** of the **hand** or **paw** of animals, ὅσα [ζῷα] χεῖρας ἔχει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.14}}; πορεύεσθαι ἐπὶ χειρῶν go on **all fours.** {{LXX|LXX.Lev.11.27}}; so of monkeys, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 502b3}}; of the **fore-paws** of the hyena, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 369}}; of the bear, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.919a.}}
**II** Special usages:
**II.1** to denote position, ποτέρας τῆς χερό; on which **hand?** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 681; ἐπὶ δεξιὰ χειρός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.19; ἐπ᾽ ἀριστερὰ χειρός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.277; χειρὸς εἰς τὰ δεξιά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 598; λαιᾶς χειρός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 714 (but χείρ is often omitted with δεξιά, ἀριστερά}} as we say **the right, the left**).
**II.2** frequently in _dative_ of all numbers with Verbs which imply the use of hands, λάβε χειρί, χερσὶν ἑλέσθαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.302, 10.501; χερσὶν ἀσπάζεσθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.35; προκαλίζεσθαι 18.20}}; χειρί, χεροῖν ψαῦσαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1510, 1466}}: sometimes this _dative_ is added _pleonastic_ by way of emphasis, ὄνυξι συλλαβὼν χερί {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 310.}}
**II.3** _genitive_, **by the hand,** χειρὸς ἔχειν τινά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.154; χειρὸς ἑλών 1.323, etc.}}; γέροντα δὲ χειρὸς ἀνίστη he raised him **by the hand,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|24.515, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.319; χερὶ χειρὸς ἑλών 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.122; τινὰ χειρός ἑλκειν [prev. author] “N.” 11.32; ἀνέλκειν τινὰ τῆς χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 569 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.4** the _accusative_ is used when one takes the **hand** of a person, χεῖρα γέροντος ἑλών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.361; χεῖρ᾽ ἕλε δεξιτερήν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.121}}; χεῖράς τ᾽ ἀλλήλων λαβέτην, in pledge of good faith, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.233; so ἔμβαλλε χ. δεξιὰν πρώτιστά μοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1181; also ἔμβαλλε χειρὸς πίστιν [prev. author] “Ph.” 813, compare “OC” 1632.}}
**II.5** other uses of the _accusative_:
**II.5.a** in prayer or entreaty, χεῖρας ἀνασχεῖν [θεοῖς] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.275, etc.; ποτὶ γούνασι χεῖρας βάλλειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.310; ἀμφὶ.. Ἀρήτης βάλε γούνασι χεῖρας Ὀδυσσεύς 7.142; ἀμφὶ δὲ χεῖρας δειρῇ βάλλ᾽ Ὀδυσῆϊ 23.207; ἀμφί τινι χεῖρε β. 21.223; περίβαλε δὲ χέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 914, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1559 (_anapaest meter_); χεῖρας προΐσχεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58, 66; so also χεῖρας ἀείρων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.423, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.130 (tmesis)}}; χ. ἀνατείνειν (see. ἀνατείνω {{Refs|1.1).}}
**II.5.b** τὰς χεῖρας αἴρειν to hold up **hands** in token of assent or choice, of persons voting, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 264; τὴν χ. αἴρειν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.41; ὅτῳ δοκεῖ ταῦτα, ἀράτω τὴν χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.33, compare 7.3.6; ἀνατεινάτω τὴν χ. [prev. work] 3.2.9, 33; χεῖρας ὀρεγνύς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.37; χεῖρ᾽ ὀρέγων εἰς οὐρανόν 15.371; χεῖρας ὀ. τινί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.257; πρός τινα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.240; ποτὶ στόμα χεῖρ᾽ ὀρέγεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.506 (but χεῖρά τισι ὀ.}} to reach them **one's hand** in help, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.17); also χεῖρε ἑτάροισι πετάσσας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.523, etc.; πιτνὰς εἰς ἐμὲ χεῖρας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.392 (but χεῖρε πετάσσας _absolutely_, of one swimming, etc., 5.374, +others).}}
**II.5.c** [Ἰλίου] χεῖρα ἑὴν ὑπερέσχε held **the hand** over as a protector, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.420, etc.}}: less frequently τισι, {{Refs|4.249, compare 5.433; χεῖρά θ᾽ ὕπερθεν ἔχεις “IG” 14.1003.10 (from Rome).}}
**II.5.d** in hostile sense, χεῖρας or χεῖρα ἐπιφέρειν τινί, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.89, 19.261, +others; χεῖρας ἐφιέναι τινί 1.567, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.254, +others; χεῖρας ἐπιβάλλειν τισί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.2.8, etc.; χέρα τινὶ προσενεγκεῖν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.36; χεῖρας ἐπί τινι ἰάλλειν, see at {ἰάλλω} I.1.}}]
**II.5.e** χεῖρας ἀπέχειν keep **hands** off, λοιμοῖο βαρείας χεῖρας ἀφέξει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.97 codices; κερτομίας δέ τοι.. καὶ χεῖρας ἀφέξω.. μνηστήρων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.263; ἀθανάτων ἀπέχειν χέρας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 350 (Lyric poetry); τὼ χεῖρε ἀπέχεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 213d; παύειν χεῖράς τινος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.294.}}
**II.5.f** χεῖρας ἐπιτιθέναι τινί, in token of consecration, {{NT|NT.1Tim.5.22, etc.}}
**II.6** with Preps.:
**II.6.a** ἀνὰ χεῖρας ἔχειν τινάς to be intimate with.., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.6.5; αἱ ἀνὰ χεῖρά τινων ὁμιλίαι 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.64}}; τὰ ἀνὰ χεῖρα πράγματα the matters **in hand,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.614b, etc. (also οἱ ἀνὰ χ. χρόνοι}} the **current** period, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 88.21 (2nd c.AD)}}; τὰ ἀνὰ χ. what comes his way, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Centil.” 18}}; ἀνὰ χ. τῆς πύλης **hard by**.., {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.15.2.}}
**II.6.b** ἀπὸ χειρὸς λογίσασθαι to reckon **off-hand, roughly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 656 (_anapaest meter_), compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 29: but πότισον τὴν γῆν ἀπὸ χειρός}} by **hand,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 155 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.6.c** διὰ χερῶν ἔχειν, λαβεῖν, literally, to have or take **between** the **hands,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 193, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 916}}; διὰ χειρὸς ἔχειν to hold **in the hand,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1258 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 597 (_anapaest meter_)}}; to have **in hand,** i. e. **under control,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76; διὰ χειρῶν ἔχειν τὴν πολιτείαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1308a27}}; τὰ τῶν ξυμμάχων keep **under control,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13}}: later, to have a work **in hand,** be engaged in it, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.69M. (χερός), 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 4; τὰ ὅπλα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 2, etc. (also διὰ χ. by direct payment, opposed to διὰ τῆς τραπέζης by banker's order, “BGU” 1156.8 (1st c.BC), etc.;compare διὰ χ. ἔσπευδε τὴν πρᾶσιν 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 1.12)}}; of arms, διὰ χειρὸς εἶναι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 35}}; διὰ χ. ἔχειν, with _participle_, to be **continually** doing, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.767c; διὰ χειρός τινος ποιεῖν τι LXX.Josh.17.4, +others, cf. NT.Act.7.25, +others}}
**II.6.d** ἐς χεῖρας λαβεῖν τι literally, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1120, etc.}}; to take a matter **in hand,** undertake it, πρᾶγμ᾽ ἐς χέρας λαβόντ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1242; ἄγεσθαί τι ἐς χεῖρας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 4.79, etc.}}; δοῦναί τινι ἐς χέρας, εἰς χεῖρα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1348, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.22; καταστῆσαι εἰς τὰς χ. τινος 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.28}}; of persons, ἵκεο χεῖρας ἐς ἁμάς thou hast fallen into our **hands,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.448 (in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus also simply ὅ τι χεῖρας ἵκοιτο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.331, compare 24.172); so εἰς χεῖρας ἐλθεῖν τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.10, compare 2.4.15}}: generally, to have to do with any one, **converse** with him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 1.2.26 (so ἐς χεῖρα γῇ ξυνῆψαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 429): most frequently ἐς χεῖρας ἐλθεῖν τισι}} to come to **blows** or **close quarters** with.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 680; ἀλλήλοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.44: _absolutely_, εἰς χ. ἐλθεῖν [prev. author] 4.96; ἐς χ. ἰέναι [prev. author] 2.3, 4.72, compare “PTeb.” 765.6 (2nd c.BC)}}; συνιέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.22}}; also ἐς χειρῶν νόμον (perhaps νομόν) ἀπικέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.48}}; ἐν χειρῶν νόμῳ (perhaps νομῷ) ἀπόλλυσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.89, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.5, “SIG” 167.37 (Mylasa, 4th c.BC), 4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus “περὶ πολιτειῶν - fragment” 25, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.34.5, 5.111.6}}; **ἐν** χειρὸς νόμῳ {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1285a10, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.26; ἐν χειρῶν νομαῖς “SIG” 700.29 (Lete, 2nd c.BC), see reading in LXX.3Mac.1.5}}; ἐν χεροῖν δίκῃ conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“E.Ba.” 738; εἰς χεῖρας συμμεῖξαι τοῖς πολεμίοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.11}}; also εἰς χεῖρας δέχεσθαί τινας to await their charge, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 4.3.31; ἐς χ. ὑπομεῖναί τινας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.72.}}
**II.6.e** ἐκ χειρός by **hand of man,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 27}}: from **near at hand, at close range,** ἐκ χειρὸς βάλλειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.15}}; ἀμύνασθαι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 5.4.25; μάχεσθαι [prev. author] “HG” 7.2.14, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.6; πληγὰς ἐκ χ. ἀναδέξασθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus}} **tim.**4; οὐ μὴ σωθῇ ἐκ χ. σιδήρου {{LXX|LXX.Job.20.24}}; ἡ ἐκ χ. δίκη **lynch** law, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.37; ἡ ἐκ χ. βία 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.4.6: _metaphorically_, ἡ ἐκ χ. θεωρία}} **closerange** reading, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 2}}; so of time, **out of hand, off-hand, forthwith,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.41.7, +others}}
**II.6.f** δέπας μητρὶ ἐν χειρὶ τίθει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.585, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.57, 15.120, +others (always so of a cup, hence ἐν χερσὶ τίθει δέπας}} though found in most codices, was condemned by the critics in Il.[same place], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.51, 15.130); πρεσβήϊον ἐν χερὶ θήσω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.289; τόξον, ἔγχος ἔχων ἐν χειρί, 15.443, 17.604; σκῆπτρον δέ οἱ ἔμβαλε χειρί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.37; but ἐν.. χειρὶ σκῆπτρον ἔθηκεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.568}}; of a gift, ἐν χερσὶ τίθει {{Refs|1.441, 446}}; ἐν ταῖς χ. ἔχειν, literally, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 432d; τὰ ὅπλ᾽ ἐν ταῖς χ. ἔχων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.8, etc. (_metaphorically_, ἔτι μεμνημένων ὑμῶν καὶ μόνον οὐκ ἐν ταῖς χερσὶν ἕκαστ᾽ ἐχόντων [prev. author] 18.226)}}; but ἐν χερσὶν ἔχειν also, to have **in hand, be engaged in,** τὸν γάμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.35; ἑορτήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 13; τὴν περὶ Δημοσθένους πραγματείαν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 1; ἐν χειρί τινα δίκην ἔχων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 172e}}; ὁ ἐν χερσὶ πόλεμος the war **in hand,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.87}}; περιτειχισμὸς ἐν χερσὶν ὤν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 21; ἡ ἐν χ. ζήτησις 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 11.208, etc.}}; frequently of fighting, ἐν χερσί **hand to hand,** ἐν χ. ἦν ἡ μάχη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.43; ἐν χ. ἀποκτεῖναι [prev. author] 3.66, compare 4.57, 96, etc.}}; ἐν χ. γίγνεσθαι τοῖς ἐναντίοις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.72; ἐν χ. εἶναί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.6.11; δίκη ἐν χερσί 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 192; ὁ ψόφος τῶν ὅπλων καὶ τῶν ἵππων ὁ φρυαγμὸς ἐν χερσὶν ἐδόκει εἶναι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.31}}; ἡ ἐν χερσὶν [δυστυχία] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 22}}: also in dual, τἀν χεροῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1345 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐν χειρί τινος **by the hand** of.., {{LXX+NT|LXX.Josh.21.2, +others; ἐν χ. ἀγγέλου NT.Act.7.35 (variant).}}
**II.6.g** ἐπὶ χειρὸς ἔχειν **on** or **in** one's **hand,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 490}}; ἐπὶ χεῖράς τινων ἐκφέρουσι put into their **hands,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.815b}}; also ἐπὶ χεῖρά τινος **next** to, {{LXX|LXX.Neh.3.4.}}
**II.6.h** κατὰ χειρός, of washing the hands before meals, ὕδωρ κατὰ χειρός (i.e. φερέτω τις), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1216, compare “Av.” 464 (_anapaest meter_), “fragment” 502 (Lyric poetry), 3rd c.BC: Philoxenus Epigrammaticus 1, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.408e}}; (without ὕδωρ) κατὰ χ. ἐδόθη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 261.2, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Archedicus Comicus 2.3}}: proverbial of that which is easily come by, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 1.2 (_anapaest meter_); πάντα μοι κατὰ χ. ἦν τὰ πράγματα}}**at hand,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 146.5}}; also κατὰ χειρῶν δοῦναι, χέειν, λαβεῖν, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Philyllius Comicus 3, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 287 (variant), 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 470 (troch.), cf. Phot. see at {κατὰ χειρὸς ὕδωρ}: κατὰ χεῖρα}}] in **deed** or **act,** κατὰ χ. γενναιότατοι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.6}}; opposed to συνέσει, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 7}}; κατὰ χεῖρά σου according to thy **will,** {{LXX|LXX.Sir.25.26: but κατὰ χεῖρας [τῆς σοφίας]}} by her **side,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 14.25.}}
**i** μετὰ χερσὶν ἔχειν **between,** i.e. **in, the hands,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.4, 15.717; [ἄλεισον] μετὰ χ. ἐνώμα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.10: μετὰ χεῖρας ἔχειν}} to have **in hand,** be engaged in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16.β, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138.}}
**k** λάβε παρὰ χεῖρα take **in hand,** {{LXX|LXX.Tob.11.4}}; but τὸ πὰρ χειρός the work **in hand,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 13.10.}}
**l** πρὸ χειρῶν **close before one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1279, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1207 (assuming variant), “Rh.” 274}}; πρὸ χειρὸς εἶναι conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 69.5.}}
**m** πρὸς χειρός τινος by his **hand,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 66 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; πρὸς ἐμὴν χεῖρα at the signs **given by** my **hand,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 148 (_anapaest meter_)}}; πρὸς χεῖρα ὑποβορβορύζοντες on **pressure,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.7.}}
**n** ὑπὸ χερσὶ ἁλοῦσα **under,** i.e. **by,** another's **hands,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.374, etc.}}; ὑπὸ χεῖρα ποιεῖσθαι to bring **under one's power,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.22}}; οἱ ὑπὸ χ. persons **in one's power,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.34}}; ὑπὸ τὴν χ. ἐλθεῖν to come **into one's hand,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 57, etc.}}; ὑπὸ χ. in **hand,** i.e. in **stock,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 369b33}}; but also, **at hand,** i.e. **at once,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.548e}}; τὰ ὑπὸ χ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work]56b, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.35}}; ὁ ὑπὸ χ. the attendant, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.75; παρέργως καὶ ὑπὸ χ.}}**extempore,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 3, etc.}}; also καθύπο χεῖρα κινῶν [τὰς οὐσίας], in Alchemy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pseudo-Democritus Alchemista p.51 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**III** **the hand** often receives the attributes of **the person using it,** χ. μεγάλη, of Zeus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.695 (χ. παγκρατής, of God, 2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 3}}; χ. ὑπερμήκης, of the 'long **arm**' of the king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.β᾽)}}; θοὴ χ., of one throwing, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.306; ἀφνειά 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.1, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 458}}; εὐσεβεστέρα, εὐφιλής, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 141, “Ag.” 34}}; κάρβανος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1061; γεραιά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 143 (_anapaest meter_); πονηρά [prev. author] “Ion” 1316, etc.}}: to denote wealth or poverty, πλειοτέρῃ σὺν χ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.359; κενεὰς σὺν χ. ἔχοντες 10.42, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1280, etc.}}
**III.2** it is represented as acting of itself, χεῖρες μαιμῶσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.77, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 50; χεὶρ ὁρᾷ τὸ δράσιμον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 554; δήμου κρατοῦσα χ. [prev. author] “Supp.” 604 (uncertain reading): proverbial, ἁ δὲ χ. τὰν χ. νίζει 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 273; or simply, ἁ χ. τὰν χ. “Anthologia Graeca” 5.207 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}
**III.3** _plural_, in theurgy, name for spiritual **powers,** αἱ δημιουργικαὶ [τοῦ Ἀπόλλωνος] δυνάμεις ἃς θεουργῶν παῖδες χεῖρας ἀποκαλοῦσιν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Cratylum commentaria” p.101}} P., compare [same work] **in R.**{{Refs|2.252K.}}
**IV** to denote **act or deed,** opposed to mere words, in _plural_, ἔπεσιν καὶ χερσὶν ἀρήξειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.77}}; μνῆμ᾽ Ἑλένης χειρῶν of her **handiwork,** her **art,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.126 (so in _sing._, δώρημ᾽ ἐκείνῳ τἀνδρὶ τῆς ἐμῆς χ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 603); χερσὶν ἢ λόγῳ [prev. author] “OT” 883 (Lyric poetry), compare “OC” 1297, etc.}}; τῇ χειρὶ χρᾶσθαι to use one's **hands,** i.with be active, stirring, opposed to ἀργὸς ἐπεστάναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.78, compare 9.72}}; τὰς χ. προσφέρειν to apply **force,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.31: _sing._, βούλευμα μὲν τὸ Δῖον, Ἡφαίστου δὲ χείρ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 619}}; μιᾷ χειρί single-**handed,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.219; χειρὶ καὶ ποδὶ καὶ πάσῃ δυνάμει 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.109, compare 2.115; χερσίν τε ποσίν τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.360, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).62}}, especially of using the hands in a fight, compare above {{Refs|11.6d}}, e, f; of deeds of violence, πρὶν χειρῶν γεύσασθαι before we try **force,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.181}}; ἀδίκων χ. ἄρχειν to give the first **blow,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.13, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.1, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.11, etc.; ἀμυνόμενος ἄρχοντα χειρῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 869d: generally, χεῖρες}} **violent measures, force,** ἐπίσχετε θυμὸν ἐνιπῆς καὶ χειρῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.267; ὑπόδικος χερῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 260 (Lyric poetry); χερσὶ πεποιθώς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.624, etc.; ἐν χειρῶν νόμῳ see above 11.6d}}; ὅπως θανάτοιο βαρείας χ. ἀλάλκοι, variant for{κῆρας}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.548.}}
**V** **a number, band, body** of men, especially of soldiers, χεὶρ μεγάλη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.157; in _dative_, οὐ σὺν μεγάλῃ χ. [prev. author] 5.72; πολλῇ χ. 1.174, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.96, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 337; pleonastic, χ. μεγάλῃ πλήθεος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.20; δεδωμάτωμαι δ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ἐγὼ σμικρᾷ χερί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 958}}; οἰκεία χείρ, for χεὶρ οἰκετῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 629; σὺν πλήθει χερῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 123.}}
**VI** **handwriting,** τὴν ἑαυτοῦ χεῖρα ἀρνήσασθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Lyc.fragment” 5, compare “IG” 9(1).189 (from Phocis)}}; τῇ ἐμῇ χ. Παύλου {{NT|NT.1Cor.16.21, NT.Col.4.18}}: **copy, counterpart** of a document, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 712.31 (Crete, 2nd c.BC)}}; **deed, instrument,** ἡ χ. ἥδε κυρία ἔστω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PRein.” 28.18 (2nd c.BC),compare “PCair.Zen.” 477 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**VI.b** **handiwork** of an artist or workman, γλαφυρὰ χ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “epigram” 8.5, etc.; αἱ Ἐφεσίου χεῖρες 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.72, compare 6.66; σοφαὶ χέρες “APl.” 4.262; τὰς Φειδίου χ. 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 30.22.}}
**VII** of any implement resembling a hand:
**VII.1** a kind of **gauntlet,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 12.5, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.135 (_plural_).}}
**VII.2** χ. σιδηρᾶ **grappling**-iron, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.25, 7.62}}; also of an **anchor,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 6.38 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus).}}
**VII.3** **axle-tree,** {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.7.18(32).}}
**VII.4** in LXX, **pillar** or **cairn,** as it were **a finger pointing to heaven,** χεὶρ Ἀβεσσαλώμ {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.18.18}}; also ἀνέστακεν αὐτῷ χεῖρα, i.e. **trophy,**{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.15.12.}}
**VII.5** χεῖρες ἐλάτιναι, of oars, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Persae” 7.}}
**VII.6** **catch** of a trigger, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 13.9; χ. κατάγουσα τὴν τοξῖτιν 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 68.4, compare 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 78.2.}}
**VII.7** instrument of torture, {{LXX|LXX.4Mac.8.13.}}
**VIII** **handful,** κορώνῃ χεῖρα πρόσδοτε κριθέων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phoenix Iambographus 2.1.}}
**IX** **ointment** containing **five** ingredients, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 89, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Trall. 7.1}}. (Cf. Armenian _jein_ (**dzern**), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Albinus Philosophus}} **dore,** Tocharian (A-dialect) **tsar,** (B-dialect) **sar,** all = **hand.**)

5497:
** χειρᾰγωγ-ός**, όν,
**leading, guiding,** χ. ἀρχή {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 8.464 (from Egypt).}}
**2** _substantive_, **leader, guide,** ἔχει.. χ. τὸν πλοῦτον ὁ γέρων {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 127; cf. NT.Act.13.11, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.794d: τοῦ βίου τυφλὴ χ. (of Τύχη), [prev. work]98b}}; θεοῖς ἕπεσθαι χειραγωγοῖς ἡγούμενοι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 61.4.}}

5498:
** χειρόγρᾰφ-ος**, ον,
**written with the hand, holograph, manuscript,** σύμβολον, ἀσφάλ[ε]ια, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 303 (2nd c.AD), “PGrenf.” 2.75.13 (4th c.AD)}}: hence **χειρόγρᾰφον**, τό, **manuscript note,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1013.52, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.8.4, LXX.Tob.5.3, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.8, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 3.40; τὸ καθ᾽ ἡμῶν χ. NT.Col.2.14.}}
**2** **note of hand, bond,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PRein. 7.22 (2nd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.829a, etc.}}; also **χειρόγρᾰφος**, ὁ, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4629 (from Syria).}}
**II** _paroxytone_, χειρογράφος [ᾰ], ὁ, **clerk, amanuensis,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|PTeb. 209 (1st c.BC).}}
